// "Key takeaways" shown at the top of each article (keyed by post slug). 3 short, factual bullets.
module.exports = {
  'wordpress-website-cost-india': ['WordPress plans are WordPress.com\'s hosted tiers; the WordPress.org software is free, and most Indian business sites pay for a domain, hosting and the build instead.', 'Most small business WordPress sites in India cost roughly ₹15,000–₹40,000 to build, plus yearly domain and hosting; unique layouts, features and content work drive the price more than page count.', 'Budget for yearly costs too (domain, hosting renewals, licences and maintenance), and send a clear brief to get an accurate fixed quote.'],
  'wordpress-vs-wix-vs-shopify': ['WordPress suits most service businesses, content-heavy sites and stores that want low running costs, Indian payment gateways and full ownership.', 'Shopify suits product brands that want a hosted store with hosting and security handled, and accept a monthly plan, app fees and possible extra transaction fees.', 'Wix suits very small DIY sites that will not grow much; it is the easiest to start with but the hardest to leave, and its payment options in India are limited.'],
  'why-is-my-wordpress-site-slow': ['A slow WordPress site is almost always down to hosting, missing page caching, heavy images, plugin or page builder bloat, or third-party scripts, not WordPress itself.', 'Diagnose before fixing: PageSpeed Insights, the browser Network tab and Query Monitor show in minutes whether the problem is the server, the front end or the admin.', 'Fix in order of impact, one change at a time, after a backup: server response and caching first, then images, theme and plugins, scripts and fonts, then the database.'],
  'signs-wordpress-site-hacked': ['A hack usually shows up in one of four places: what visitors see, what Google shows, what the WordPress admin shows and what the server shows.', 'To check if a WordPress site is hacked, run a site: search, open the Search Console Security Issues report, review admin users and scan files, then visit as a stranger on mobile data.', 'If you find a problem, back up the infected copy, change every password, clean core, plugins and database, hunt for backdoors and close the entry point before asking Google for a review.'],
  'clinic-website-checklist-for-doctors': ['A clinic website earns trust through details patients can check: degrees with university and year, registration number, real clinic photos and genuine reviews.', 'Make booking effortless on a phone with tap-to-call, WhatsApp and a short form that asks only for name, phone and preferred session, never symptoms or reports.', 'Give each major treatment its own page, keep your Google Business Profile consistent with the site, and keep every medical claim factual and within current NMC guidance.'],
  'freelancer-vs-agency-web-developer': ['For most small and medium business websites, an experienced freelancer gives direct communication and lower cost; an agency fits projects that need branding, content, ads and development managed together.', 'The bigger risks are the same either way: vague scope, accounts in someone else\'s name and no plan for support, so get scope, ownership and after-launch terms in writing.', 'Judge any developer by live sites you can open on your phone, who will actually do the work, and how clearly they answer your questions before you pay.'],
  'woocommerce-vs-shopify-india': ['WooCommerce suits Indian stores that want low running costs, only the gateway\'s fee on payments, flexible COD and GST rules, strong content and full ownership, with a developer handling upkeep.', 'Shopify suits stores that want hosting, security and updates handled, and accept a monthly plan, monthly app fees and any Shopify transaction fee that applies to third-party gateways.', 'Both work with Indian gateways, UPI, COD and couriers such as Shiprocket; the real differences are how features are added, how much you can customise and how easily you can leave.'],
  'wordpress-maintenance-checklist': ['A WordPress maintenance schedule splits tasks into weekly backups and updates, monthly security and SEO checks, quarterly restore tests and yearly renewals.', 'Put the schedule on real dates, keep updates away from busy selling days and log every change so you can trace problems quickly.', 'Most of the checklist becomes quick once backups, uptime monitoring and staging are set up; hand it over if you cannot keep to it.'],
  'get-more-enquiries-from-your-website': ['First work out whether you have too few visitors, visitors who do not act, or enquiries that are lost or untracked, because each needs a different fix.', 'Say what you do and for whom in the headline, put one clear call to action on every page, and offer WhatsApp, a tap-to-call number and a short form.', 'Real proof, a page for each service, a fast mobile site and tracking every form, call and WhatsApp click turn the same traffic into more enquiries.'],
  'local-seo-guide-small-business-india': ['Google ranks local results on relevance, distance and prominence, so a complete, accurate Business Profile and a steady flow of genuine reviews do most of the early work.', 'Keep your name, address and phone identical everywhere, give each main service its own page, and build area or branch pages only where you genuinely serve or operate.', 'Track calls, direction requests and website visits from your profile, plus enquiries by area, so you know which parts of local SEO actually bring customers.'],
  'b2b-manufacturer-website-guide': ['Plan the site for everyone in the buying process: engineers need specifications and drawings, purchase teams a quick RFQ, quality teams verifiable certificates and approvers a company profile.', 'Applications pages, a technical downloads library and a separate route for dealers are the parts most manufacturer websites miss, and each one answers a different kind of buyer.', 'B2B searches are specific, so match each type of query, from product plus specification to application and comparison, with a page written for it, and show only certifications you currently hold.'],
  'elementor-vs-gutenberg': ['Gutenberg vs Elementor: Gutenberg is built into WordPress, free, lighter by default and stores content natively; Elementor gives visual drag-and-drop design with popups, forms and theme building in Pro.', 'Speed depends more on how a site is built than on the editor: a careful Elementor build can be fast, and a bloated block theme can be slow.', 'Choose Elementor for design-heavy marketing pages edited by non-technical teams, Gutenberg for content sites and long-term flexibility, or use both on one site.'],
  'choose-wordpress-hosting-india': ['Decide the type of hosting first, shared, managed WordPress, cloud or VPS, based on your traffic, whether you sell online and who will look after the server.', 'For visitors in India, compare server location, server software and caching, PHP version, off-site backups and WordPress-aware support before you compare prices.', 'Work out the three-year cost including renewal and GST, read the limits behind "unlimited", and keep the hosting account in your own business name.'],
  'landing-page-mistakes-google-ads': ['Most wasted ad spend comes from the page, not the ad: a headline that ignores the ad, a slow mobile load, a long form or tracking that counts the wrong thing.', 'Fix tracking first, because without it you cannot tell which change helped, then fix anything broken or against policy, then speed, message match, the form and proof.', 'Change one thing at a time, judge it on real enquiries rather than clicks, and keep a short log so you know what worked and what to try next.'],
  'how-long-to-build-wordpress-website': ['Counted from when content is ready, a landing page usually takes 3–5 working days, a small business site 1–2 weeks and a WooCommerce store 2–4 weeks.', 'Late content, scattered feedback and features added midway cause more delays than the build itself, so prepare text, photos and logins before day one.', 'To hit a launch date, work backwards from it, start third-party approvals in the first week, and launch the essential pages first if time is short.'],
  'redesign-website-without-losing-rankings': ['Benchmark and crawl the old site first, then map every URL that changes to its closest new equivalent with a single 301 redirect, never a blanket redirect to the homepage.', 'Keep the content, titles, headings, internal links and schema of the pages that already rank, and keep staging out of Google with a password rather than relying on noindex.', 'On launch day remove the noindex, test every old URL and check canonicals, then watch Search Console daily for two weeks and fix 404s and drops quickly.'],
  'accept-online-payments-wordpress-india': ['Open an account with an Indian payment gateway in your business name, complete KYC and publish your policy pages, then connect the gateway to WooCommerce, a payment form or payment links.', 'Set up webhooks so orders paid by UPI are marked paid even if the customer never returns to your site, and check the settlement cycle so you know when money reaches your bank.', 'Test in test mode first, including failed, cancelled and refunded payments on a phone, then switch to live keys and confirm with a real low-value order.'],
  'signs-you-need-a-new-website': ['The clearest signs are a site that is hard to use on a phone, slow, dated, impossible to edit, insecure, short of enquiries or no longer describing your business.', 'One or two specific problems can usually be fixed with a refresh; a site that fails on phones, cannot be updated safely or no longer fits the business usually needs rebuilding.', 'Before any redesign, record which pages bring visits and enquiries today, so the new site keeps them and redirects every address that changes.'],
  'wordpress-security-checklist': ['Most WordPress hacks come from automated bots exploiting outdated plugins and weak logins, so updates, strong passwords and 2FA block the majority of attacks.', 'Harden the site itself: turn off file editing, set sensible file permissions, block PHP in uploads and disable XML-RPC if nothing on your site needs it.', 'Run one security plugin, keep tested off-site backups and monitor uptime and Search Console, because hardening reduces risk but never removes it.'],
  'core-web-vitals-explained': ['A page passes Core Web Vitals when LCP, INP and CLS are all good for at least 75% of real visits over 28 days.', 'Field data from real Chrome users decides the pass or fail; lab tools such as Lighthouse help find the cause.', 'On Indian mobile networks, heavy hero images, sliders and chat or ad scripts are the most common reasons pages fail.'],
  'setup-google-analytics-search-console': ['Search Console shows how people find you in Google; GA4 shows what they do on your site. You need both, and both should be owned by a business Google account.', 'Verify a Domain property in Search Console with a DNS record, add GA4 to WordPress one way only (Site Kit, a plugin or Tag Manager), and check it in Realtime.', 'Link them in GA4 under Admin, Product links, Search Console links, then publish the Search Console reports from the Library and mark enquiries as key events.'],
  'migrate-wix-to-wordpress': ['Wix designs cannot be exported, so a move to WordPress is a rebuild on staging with your text, images, blog posts and products carried across.', 'List every Wix URL before you start, then match it in WordPress permalinks or redirect it with a 301 to its closest new page, never all to the homepage.', 'Keep the Wix site and its plan running until the new site is live and checked, and copy every DNS record, including email, before the domain switches.'],
  'google-business-profile-checklist': ['Get the foundations exactly right first: your real business name, the most specific primary category, an accurate address or service area, hours and a link to the most relevant page.', 'Reviews are the biggest ongoing factor, so ask every happy customer with your direct review link, reply to every review and never buy or incentivise them.', 'Manage the profile like a routine: weekly review replies, monthly photos, posts and detail checks, and a quarterly look at categories, services and performance.'],
  'how-to-write-website-content': ['Plan before you write: decide who each page is for, the one action it should lead to, and the questions customers ask before they buy.', 'Write service pages first, then the homepage, About and contact pages, using plain words, the customer first and a specific fact behind every claim.', 'Make every page scannable on a phone, use the words customers search for naturally, and fill in a short content brief for each page before drafting.'],
  'website-design-mistakes': ['The costliest mistakes are the basic ones: a headline that does not say what you do, pages that are hard to use on a phone, slow loading and contact details visitors cannot find.', 'Most of them can be fixed without a full redesign: rewrite the headline, add clear calls to action, simplify forms, use real photos and show genuine proof.', 'If several mistakes stack up and the site was not built for phones, a redesign on staging with proper redirects usually costs less than patching it.'],
  'wordpress-vs-custom-coded-website': ['WordPress is a ready-made system you build on with themes and plugins; a custom-coded site is written from scratch, so every feature, editing screen and change is built by developers.', 'For most business websites, blogs and online stores, WordPress launches faster, costs less and lets your team edit content; custom code earns its cost for web applications.', 'Neither is automatically faster, more secure or better for SEO: those depend on how well the site is built, hosted and maintained.'],
  'hotel-website-direct-bookings': ['Many guests who find you on an OTA search your name next, so the website has to beat the OTA listing on photos, room detail, trust and how easy it is to book.', 'Give guests a reason to book direct that fits your OTA agreements, such as perks or packages, and check any rate parity terms before advertising a lower price.', 'Treat WhatsApp, your Google listing and returning guests as direct channels, and measure the share of bookings that come without commission each month.'],
  'website-for-lawyers-and-chartered-accountants': ['Clients arrive with a specific problem, so give each practice area a page that describes the situations you handle, the process, the documents needed and the limits of your scope.', 'Show credentials clients can verify, such as enrolment or membership numbers, and keep wording within Bar Council or ICAI rules: factual, with no success claims for advocates.', 'Treat every enquiry as confidential from the first click: ask for an outline only, no documents, run conflict checks before discussing details, and collect as little personal data as you need.'],
  'solar-company-website-guide': ['Give homeowners, housing societies and businesses their own pages, because they ask different questions, decide differently and expect different proof.', 'Keep subsidy and savings information honest: explain the process as you handle it, link to the official portal, date any figures and label every calculator result as an estimate.', 'Make the site survey the main goal, and build trust with dated project pages, plain warranty explanations and only the brands, certifications and empanelment you genuinely hold.'],
  'white-label-wordpress-development-agencies': ['In white-label development, your agency sells and manages the project while a partner builds the WordPress site under your brand, with no credits and no client contact.', 'Agree the basics in writing before the first project: scope and revision rounds, the pricing model, an NDA, who owns accounts and code, and how feedback flows.', 'Quality comes from process: a clear brief, staging builds, the partner\'s QA before your review, one consolidated feedback list per round and a handover note in your branding.'],
  'real-estate-website-must-have-features': ['A complete project page with configurations, carpet areas, floor plans, location and RERA details matters more than any extra feature.', 'Store floor plans and price sheets as editable fields with dates, so the team can update them without stale PDFs circulating.', 'Add an NRI section only if NRIs are real buyers, covering video visits, call times and WhatsApp contact.'],
  'school-coaching-website-what-parents-look-for': ['Parents compare several institutes in one sitting, so the website must answer results, faculty, fees, safety, timings and location quickly, with details they can check.', 'Results persuade only when they are verifiable: name the exam, year and course taken, with consent, and leave out anything you cannot back up.', 'Plan the admission enquiry as a sequence, from form to counsellor call, demo or visit, registration and confirmation, so no family is lost between steps.'],
  'temple-ngo-website-online-donations': ['To accept online donation to temples, get documents and a bank account in the trust\'s name, apply to a payment gateway and build a donation form with clear purposes.', 'Add a UPI QR for devotees at the temple, send automatic receipts after every payment, and offer seva, puja and annadanam options alongside general donations.', 'Test in the gateway\'s test mode, make one small real donation, and reconcile donations with the trust\'s accounts every month.'],
  'startup-website-checklist': ['Before building, put the domain and every account in the company name, write a one-sentence positioning line and choose one primary call to action.', 'Launch with a homepage, product, pricing if you sell self-serve, about, contact and legal pages, with sign-ups or demo requests tracked from the first day.', 'Add investor, careers, customer story and blog pages when you have something real to put on them, on templates your team can reuse without a developer.'],
  'figma-to-wordpress-designer-guide': ['Name colour, type and spacing styles or variables by role, and use auto layout everywhere, because both map straight onto global styles and Flexbox containers in WordPress.', 'Build buttons, cards, fields and navigation as components with every state, and design at least desktop and mobile frames for the homepage and each unique template.', 'Hand over SVG icons, full-resolution photos, web-licensed fonts, real content, documented interactions and a page and template list, so nothing has to be guessed.'],
  'industrial-website-product-catalogue': ['An industrial catalog website works best as web pages, one per product family, with a text spec table, drawings, a datasheet and a quote button.', 'Plan the category tree first and let buyers filter and search by the specifications and model numbers they actually use.', 'For most manufacturers a catalogue without prices, with an enquiry basket that sends one RFQ, works better than a full online shop.'],
  'domain-hosting-ssl-explained': ['The domain is your address, DNS points it to your hosting and your email, hosting stores and serves the website, and SSL encrypts the connection.', 'Each part renews separately, so put every renewal date in one place, turn on auto-renew and keep payment details current.', 'Register the domain, hosting and email in your business name with your own login, and give your developer access rather than ownership.'],
  'website-maintenance-cost-india': ['Website maintenance cost in India has two parts: the monthly maintenance plan and the yearly running costs of domain, hosting and licences.', 'The monthly price depends mostly on the type of site, the number of plugins and integrations, content-change time and how fast emergencies are handled.', 'Compare maintenance packages by written scope, backup and restore process and response times, not by the headline monthly charge.'],
  'landing-page-vs-website': ['A website builds trust and search traffic across many pages; a landing page focuses one audience on one offer and one action.', 'Send paid ad clicks to a landing page or tightly focused service page that matches the ad, not to your homepage.', 'Most businesses that advertise need both, with the landing page sharing the website\'s branding and contact details.'],
  'get-website-indexed-google-faster': ['Link every new page from pages Google already crawls often, such as the homepage, main menu and indexed posts, and keep it in a sitemap with an accurate lastmod.', 'Use Search Console\'s Request indexing only for your most important URLs, because the daily quota is small and repeated requests do not speed anything up.', 'Google indexes selectively on new sites, so fewer, fuller and unique pages on a fast server get indexed sooner than many thin ones; use IndexNow to tell Bing directly.'],
  'how-to-choose-wordpress-theme': ['Decide how the site will be built and edited first, Elementor, the block editor or a custom design, because that decides which kind of theme fits.', 'Check speed on a plain page, recent updates, support replies, reviews, required plugins and lock-in before you look at demo designs.', 'Use free themes from WordPress.org or premium ones from the developer, never nulled copies, and test any theme switch on a staging copy.'],
  'restaurant-website-online-ordering': ['Choose the ordering route your team can actually run: WhatsApp orders for small menus and regulars, a WooCommerce menu with online payment once manual replies start slipping.', 'Plan portions, add-ons, delivery areas, ordering hours and payment before anything is built, because these choices decide how every dish and checkout is set up.', 'Make sure every order is seen and confirmed quickly, by email, app alerts or WhatsApp, and give regulars a simple reason to order direct instead of through an app.'],
  'personal-brand-website-professionals': ['A personal brand website should say in one sentence who you help and with what, then back it up with credentials visitors can check, not adjectives.', 'Professional rules shape the copy: doctors, advocates and CAs should check their regulator\'s current guidance on advertising and testimonials before launch.', 'Give visitors one clear next step, such as a consultation request, a discovery call or a free self-assessment, and make it work on a phone in a couple of taps.'],
  'equipment-rental-website-guide': ['Organise the fleet the way buyers specify it, usually by capacity first, and give each capacity band or equipment type its own page with uses, price factors and FAQs.', 'For SEO, match "on rent" and "hire" searches with capacities and your city, keep your Google Business Profile complete, and only create area pages where you genuinely serve.', 'Make enquiring instant with a call-back button in the header, an enquiry button after every section, click-to-call, page-specific WhatsApp messages and a short form.'],
  'directory-website-wordpress': ['Plan the data first: a listing post type, taxonomies for how people browse, such as category and location, and custom fields for every detail a visitor needs.', 'Decide the search routes before choosing a theme or plugin, then give each route its own archive page with filters, sorting and a map where it helps.', 'Google indexes useful listings, not thousands of near-empty ones, so launch with fewer, complete listings, write real category intros and keep filter URLs out of the index.'],
  'lead-magnets-newsletter-small-business': ['A good lead magnet answers one question your buyers have before they hire you, such as a checklist, price guide or calculator, and is delivered the moment they ask.', 'Build it a short landing page with a clear consent line, deliver it on the thank-you page and by email or WhatsApp, and track sign-ups separately from enquiries.', 'Follow up with a few useful messages that lead naturally to your service, and only keep messaging people who clearly agreed to hear from you.'],
  'woocommerce-store-launch-checklist': ['Start the slow tasks early: payment gateway KYC, courier accounts, GST rates from your CA and policy pages can hold up a launch more than the build itself.', 'Run test orders for every path, including failed and abandoned payments, COD, out-of-state GST, coupons and refunds, before switching the gateway to live keys.', 'On launch day, place and refund one real low-value order, delete test orders, open the store to search engines, then check orders against the gateway daily for two weeks.'],
  'schema-markup-explained': ['Schema markup is structured data, usually JSON-LD, that tells search engines exactly what a page describes; it helps Google understand a page but guarantees neither rankings nor rich results.', 'Most small business sites need Organization or LocalBusiness, Service or Product, Article and BreadcrumbList; FAQ rich results are now limited and self-serving review stars are not shown.', 'Let one SEO plugin generate the core schema, add custom JSON-LD only for gaps, mark up only what is visible on the page, and test every template after changes.'],
  'multilingual-wordpress-website-hindi-english': ['Use a translation plugin such as Polylang, WPML or TranslatePress so each language gets its own URL, usually a folder like /hi/, with a language switcher.', 'Translate everything a visitor touches: menus, forms, confirmation emails, SEO titles and image alt text, and have a fluent speaker write or check the Hindi.', 'Let the plugin add hreflang tags, never redirect visitors by location, and start with the pages that bring enquiries before translating the whole site.'],
  'whatsapp-on-business-website': ['The free way to put WhatsApp Business chat on a website is a wa.me click-to-chat link behind a button, with your number in international format and an optional pre-filled message.', 'Place it beside your main calls to action and as a small floating button on mobile, with page-specific messages so you know what each chat is about.', 'You only need the WhatsApp Business API for automation, shared team inboxes or bulk notifications; track clicks in GA4 and reply quickly either way.'],
  'image-optimization-wordpress': ['Resize images to the largest size they are shown at, convert photos to WebP or AVIF and compress them with one tool, so no page ships multi-megabyte photos to phones.', 'Treat the main image at the top of the page differently: never lazy-load it, give it fetchpriority="high" and set its width and height, then lazy-load everything below the fold.', 'Fix an existing site by finding the heaviest images first, bulk-optimising with backups of the originals, and checking that sliders, CSS backgrounds and product galleries are covered too.'],
  'website-accessibility-basics': ['Accessibility means people can use your site whatever their eyesight, hearing, movement or device, and WCAG level AA is the usual target.', 'Most problems on small business sites are basic and fixable: low contrast, missing alt text, menus that fail with a keyboard, skipped headings and unlabelled form fields.', 'Automated checkers find only some issues, so add a keyboard test, a zoom test and a quick screen reader test, and build the fixes into your theme rather than patching pages.'],
  'website-brief-template': ['A website brief should cover your business, goals, audience, pages, features, content, examples, timeline and budget range, in plain language.', 'Features, page count, who writes content and integrations such as payments or CRM drive cost most, so describe those sections in the most detail.', 'Send the same brief to every developer you approach so quotes are comparable, and treat their questions as a sign of how carefully they read it.'],
  'on-page-seo-checklist': ['On-page SEO starts before writing: match the type of page Google already shows for the search, and give each search one page so your own pages never compete.', 'Get the visible basics right on every page: a unique title and description, one clear H1, a direct answer near the top, real experience and a clear next step.', 'Then check the technical layer: internal links in and out, compressed images with alt text, matching schema, a self-referencing canonical, no stray noindex, and fast loading on a phone.'],
  'contact-form-not-getting-enquiries': ['Submit your own form today and check the email arrives; many forms fail silently because WordPress sends unauthenticated email that lands in spam or nowhere.', 'Send form emails through SMTP from an address on your own domain, keep SPF and DKIM in place, and store every submission so nothing depends on email alone.', 'If the form works but nobody uses it, shorten it, put it where visitors decide, offer WhatsApp and phone alongside, and show proof and a reply time next to it.'],
  'essential-wordpress-plugins-business': ['Most business sites need one plugin, or a host feature, for each of SEO, security, backups, caching, images, forms with spam protection, email delivery and analytics.', 'Choose plugins that are recently updated, well supported and do one job well, and never run two plugins that do the same job.', 'The number of plugins matters less than their quality: delete what you do not use, avoid nulled copies, and update with a backup and a test.'],
  'website-traffic-dropped': ['Before changing anything, confirm the drop is real by comparing GA4 with Search Console and with the same weeks last year, since broken tracking and seasonality cause many false alarms.', 'Narrow it down by channel, pages, queries and the exact date, then match the pattern to a cause: a site change, an indexing block, a Google update, a hack or competitors.', 'Check Manual actions, Security issues and the Pages report in Search Console early, and fix the cause you find rather than rewriting or deleting pages in a panic.'],
  'ai-search-optimization-website': ['AI Overviews and AI assistants mostly draw on pages that search engines can crawl and index, so the foundation is the same as good SEO: clear, trustworthy pages that answer questions directly.', 'Write quotable answers, keep your business details identical everywhere, add accurate structured data, and check that robots.txt and security tools don\'t block the crawlers you want.', 'llms.txt is an optional, unofficial proposal with unproven benefit; measure what you can through Search Console, GA4 referrals and asking new enquiries how they found you.'],
  'website-for-interior-designers-architects': ['Make the project page the heart of the site: the brief, the site as found, key decisions, materials, your exact scope and real photos, captioned so visitors can match your work to their taste.', 'Large images can stay sharp and fast if they are exported at sensible sizes, served in modern formats and loaded only when needed.', 'Ask enquiry type, project stage, budget and timeline so you know what each lead needs before you call, and keep design partner and careers enquiries on separate forms.'],
  'website-for-gyms-fitness-studios': ['A gym website is mostly a timetable, a set of plans and a trial offer, so build it so staff can update each one in minutes.', 'Trainer profiles should name each certification and the body that issued it, and facilities should be shown in real photos at real times of day.', 'Avoid body-transformation guarantees; describe programmes, coaching and support instead, and use member stories only with consent and context.'],
  'website-for-travel-agencies': ['Give every package its own page with a day-by-day itinerary, hotels or hotel category, meals, transfers, inclusions and exclusions, price basis, dates and cancellation terms.', 'Most trips are customised, so make the trip enquiry form the main action: destination, dates, departure city, travellers, hotel category and budget, followed up quickly on WhatsApp.', 'Mention registrations and memberships such as Ministry of Tourism recognition, IATA or TAAI only if genuinely held, and back them with reviews, a real office and clear payment and refund terms.'],
  'website-for-dentists': ['Each major treatment deserves its own page that answers the patient\'s real worries: pain, number of visits, recovery and how cost is worked out.', 'State each dentist\'s degrees, university and council registration precisely, and keep claims, offers and before-and-after photos within current professional guidance.', 'An emergency page, a clinic tour and an easy booking route on every page do more for appointments than a polished slider.'],
  'portfolio-website-freelancers-creatives': ['A portfolio website you own is where serious clients check you out: show your best 6–12 pieces, explain your thinking in case studies and make the next step obvious.', 'Say who you work with and what you offer, add an about page with a real photo and real testimonials, and ask for project type, budget and timeline on the form.', 'Keep image-heavy portfolios fast with resized WebP images and lazy loading, and target your niche and location so the right clients find you on Google.'],
  'website-for-event-wedding-planners': ['Instagram shows your taste, but the website is where couples and companies check your process, read full event stories and send a proper enquiry.', 'Organise the portfolio by event, each with a short story, the scale and credited vendors, rather than one gallery of hundreds of mixed photos.', 'You can guide budgets without a price list by explaining what drives cost and asking for a budget range in the enquiry form.'],
  'wordpress-backup-restore-guide': ['A complete WordPress backup has two parts, the files (mainly wp-content and wp-config.php) and the database, and you need both to restore a working site.', 'Follow the 3-2-1 rule: host backups plus an automatic plugin or service backup stored off the server, such as UpdraftPlus to Google Drive, BlogVault or Jetpack VaultPress Backup.', 'Restore through your backup tool or hosting panel, or manually with File Manager and phpMyAdmin, and test a restore on staging every few months so you know it works.'],
  'woocommerce-product-page-optimization': ['Answer four questions on the first phone screen: what it is, the full price, when it will arrive and whether the store can be trusted.', 'Use consistent photos, clear variation swatches, specifications as attributes and a one-line delivery, COD and returns summary right beside Add to cart.', 'Keep the page fast, add honest reviews, and use GA4 views, add-to-carts and purchases per product to decide what to change next, one change at a time.'],
  'woocommerce-seo-guide': ['Map one main search term to each category and product, then give category pages real content, because they usually have the best chance of ranking for broader searches.', 'Use one SEO plugin, check that only one set of Product schema appears on each product page, and keep filter, sort and variation URLs canonicalised to the main page.', 'Keep temporarily out-of-stock products live, redirect discontinued ones to the closest alternative, and keep the store fast with caching that excludes cart and checkout.'],
  'get-more-google-reviews': ['Most happy customers will leave a review if they are asked personally, at the right moment, with a direct link, so make asking part of every handover, visit or delivery.', 'Don\'t offer anything in exchange, don\'t ask only the customers you expect to be happy, and never post reviews yourself; Google\'s policies forbid all three, at the time of writing.', 'Reply to every review, good or bad, and show genuine reviews on your website with a link to your profile, so a steady flow of reviews keeps working for you.'],
  'internal-linking-explained': ['Contextual internal links with descriptive anchor text tell Google and visitors how your pages relate.', 'Group pages into hubs and spokes, and link new pages from your strongest existing pages so they get found.', 'There is no fixed link count; link where it helps the reader and fix orphan pages and broken links regularly.'],
  'technical-seo-audit-wordpress': ['A WordPress SEO audit checks whether Google can crawl, index and understand your site, then looks at speed, on-page SEO, structured data and content quality.', 'Fix blockers such as a site-wide noindex, robots.txt blocks, hacked pages and broken templates before title and description tweaks.', 'Write every finding into a short report with the pages affected, impact, owner and status, and run a quick audit after any redesign, migration or traffic drop.'],
  'choose-domain-name-business': ['In .com vs .biz, choose .com (or .in for India-focused businesses): people remember and trust it more, and .biz is often mistyped as .com.', 'A good business domain is short, easy to say and spell, matches your business name and works as an email address.', 'Check trademarks, existing businesses and the domain\'s history before buying, and register it in your own account with auto-renew on.'],
  'website-for-export-businesses': ['An overseas buyer cannot visit before the first order, so product pages need full specifications, packing details, MOQ and lead times stated accurately.', 'Explain trade terms such as MOQ and Incoterms in plain words, and show only the registrations and certificates you actually hold, with dates.', 'Translated pages with hreflang, fast hosting for overseas visitors and an enquiry form built around trade questions turn international searches into usable leads.'],
  'why-elementor-sites-slow': ['Most slow Elementor sites are slow because of how they were built: nested containers, add-on packs, animations, full-size images and a heavy theme underneath.', 'Turn on Elementor\'s performance settings one at a time, remove add-ons and widgets you do not use, and rebuild only the heaviest sections with flat containers.', 'A slow Elementor editor is a separate problem, usually long pages, low PHP memory, weak hosting or a plugin, and page caching does nothing for it.'],
  'headless-wordpress-small-business': ['Headless WordPress keeps WordPress for managing content but builds the public website as a separate front end that fetches content through an API.', 'It can be fast and flexible, but you pay for two systems, lose most front-end plugins and visual editing, and need a developer for layout changes.', 'Most small businesses get the speed they want from a well-built traditional WordPress site with good hosting, caching and a CDN, or a simple static site.'],
  'measure-website-roi': ['Website ROI is the profit from customers the website brought in, minus what the website cost, divided by that cost; the hard part is counting the leads, not the maths.', 'Track every enquiry route (forms, calls, WhatsApp clicks, bookings, orders) as GA4 key events, and ask offline leads how they found you.', 'Lead value comes from your own close rate and average profit per customer, so keep a simple lead sheet and review cost per lead every month.'],
  'website-for-diagnostic-labs': ['The test page is the core of a lab website: what the test is for, sample type, preparation, reporting time and price if you publish it.', 'Home collection booking works best when it shows the areas and time slots you can really serve, and asks only for what the phlebotomist needs.', 'Report download usually means linking or embedding your lab software\'s patient portal, so check what your vendor supports before planning the page.'],
  'write-about-page-that-builds-trust': ['Open with who you help and what you do for them, then keep the story short and tell visitors who is behind the business, with real photos.', 'State only credentials you can prove, such as registrations, memberships and real projects, and back them with case studies and genuine reviews.', 'Finish with a clear next step, such as a call, WhatsApp or a quote form, because the About page is often visited just before someone decides to enquire.'],
  'write-service-pages-that-convert': ['Give every main service its own page, named the way customers search, because each service is a separate search with its own questions.', 'Answer questions in the order buyers ask them: what it is and who it is for, what is included, how it works, what affects the price, proof, FAQs and how to start.', 'Use the customer\'s words, real proof and one clear call to action repeated down the page, and keep every service page\'s text unique even when the layout is shared.'],
  'collect-display-customer-testimonials': ['Ask happy customers soon after a good outcome, with two or three specific questions, and get written permission for the exact wording, name and photo you will publish.', 'Show testimonials word for word next to the decisions they support, such as service pages, forms and landing pages, rather than only on a testimonials page.', 'Never invent, buy or reword testimonials to change their meaning, and do not mark them up as review stars, which Google does not show for reviews of your own business.'],
  'write-case-studies-business-website': ['Tell each story in a fixed order: the client, the problem, your approach, what you delivered and the outcome, then a quote and a clear next step.', 'State results only as far as you can verify them, with the client agreeing to publish; if there are no numbers, describe what visibly changed.', 'Give every case study its own page with a descriptive title, link it to the service and industry pages it supports, and get permission before naming anyone.'],
  'update-wordpress-safely': ['Before updating, take a backup you know how to restore, read the changelog for major versions and test big updates such as WooCommerce, page builders and core on staging.', 'Update one thing at a time at a quiet hour, usually plugins, then the theme, then core, and test pages, forms and checkout while logged out after each important update.', 'If an update breaks something, use recovery mode or rename the plugin folder to get back in, roll back that one update or restore the backup, and pause its auto-updates.'],
  'staging-sites-explained': ['A staging site is a private copy of your website for testing updates, redesigns and new features before visitors see them.', 'Your host\'s one-click staging (Hostinger, SiteGround, Softaculous on cPanel hosts) is the easiest option; the WP Staging plugin works almost anywhere.', 'Keep staging password-protected and out of Google, stop it emailing customers, and never push a staging database over a live WooCommerce store without a plan for new orders.'],
  'website-for-logistics-transport-companies': ['Give each service its own page, FTL, part load, warehousing, last mile, because each is bought by different people who ask different questions.', 'Show coverage as the lanes and regions you actually run, describe owned and attached fleet honestly, and connect tracking in whatever way your system supports.', 'Use a quote form that captures origin, destination, load and timing, and treat driver recruitment as a second audience with its own simple, mobile-first page.'],
  'website-for-salons-spas': ['Write the service menu with durations and starting prices, because clients plan around time as much as cost and your booking system needs both.', 'Let clients choose a stylist or therapist when booking, and give bridal packages and memberships their own pages with clear inclusions and terms.', 'Show your own work with client consent, say plainly how you keep tools and rooms clean, and link the site to your Google Business Profile reviews.'],
  'website-for-construction-companies': ['Decide first whether you sell to home buyers as a builder or to clients and consultants as a contractor, because the two audiences need different websites.', 'Project pages with photos from each stage of work, scope, value band and role prove capacity better than a gallery of finished buildings.', 'A tender and prequalification section with current documents ready to download saves procurement teams time and shows you are organised.'],
  'website-for-furniture-businesses': ['Sell ready-stock pieces with prices and a cart, and handle made-to-order work through a quote flow that asks for sizes, materials and room photos.', 'Every product page needs exact dimensions, materials, finishes and care, plus a measuring guide so buyers know the piece will fit through the door and into the room.', 'Furniture sites are image-heavy, so resize and compress photos and lazy-load galleries, or the pages that should sell will load too slowly on a phone.'],
  'website-for-jewellers': ['Show the price breakdown buyers expect: metal weight, purity, the rate used, making charges, stone charges and tax, with the rate coming from a source your store controls and a visible update time.', 'Sell lightweight and everyday pieces online, and use reserve-to-try, store appointments and WhatsApp video calls for bridal and high-value collections.', 'Build trust with accurate photos, purity and hallmark details stated in hedged, checkable terms, clear exchange and buyback policies, insured delivery and a secure checkout.'],
  'website-for-it-software-companies': ['Say in one sentence who you help and with what, and organise the site around that rather than a long list of technologies.', 'Case studies need client permission or careful anonymising, and they work best when they show the problem, your team\'s role and how the engagement ran.', 'Overseas buyers want the delivery model explained plainly: engagement types, overlap hours, communication, security and who owns the code.'],
  'website-for-d2c-food-brands': ['A food buyer cannot taste before ordering, so product pages must answer every label question: ingredients, nutrition, allergens, shelf life, storage, net quantity and your FSSAI details.', 'Ship what survives the journey: check serviceable pin codes and transit times, pack for heat and breakage, and keep short-shelf-life products to zones you can reach quickly.', 'Repeat orders are where a D2C food brand makes its margin, so plan subscriptions or reorder reminders, trial packs, genuine reviews and festival gifting from the start.'],
  'website-for-fashion-boutiques': ['Answer the fit question on every product page with a size chart in centimetres and inches, garment measurements, fit notes and the model\'s height and size worn.', 'Set up sizes and colours as proper WooCommerce variations with stock per size, and keep lookbooks and collections linked to products so a styled photo turns into an order.', 'Use Instagram for discovery and WhatsApp for styling questions, but send both to product pages, and keep COD, exchange and returns rules short, clear and the same everywhere.'],
  'website-for-pharmacies': ['The rules on selling medicines online in India are not settled, so take legal advice before adding a cart for medicines, and treat prescription drugs with particular care.', 'For many local pharmacies the practical route is an enquiry-led site: customers share a prescription request, the pharmacist reviews it, and the order is confirmed by phone or WhatsApp.', 'Over-the-counter, wellness and device ranges can often be sold online more simply, alongside a delivery area checker, a store locator and a named pharmacist to contact.'],
  'website-for-car-dealers-workshops': ['Used car dealers, authorised new car dealers and independent workshops need different websites, so decide which you are before planning pages.', 'For a used car dealer the stock listing is the product: real photos, full details, condition notes and an enquiry button on every car.', 'Workshops should name the brands they service without implying an authorisation they do not hold, and use a booking form that captures the car and the problem.'],
  'website-for-printing-packaging-companies': ['Build the site as a catalogue: one page per product type with real sample photos, styles, materials, print processes, finishes, MOQ and applications, plus pages that prove your machinery and capacity.', 'Make quoting easy with an RFQ form that asks for size, quantity, material, finish and delivery city, accepts artwork files safely, and lets buyers request samples.', 'Publish artwork guidelines and dielines, state MOQs and proof approval rules plainly, and claim certifications or sustainability only where you genuinely hold them.'],
  'website-for-security-facility-management': ['Write the site to answer a vendor evaluation: services, licences, statutory registrations, supervision and references, each on a page a procurement team can check.', 'Show a PSARA licence, labour registrations or ISO certificates only if you hold them, with numbers, states and validity dates kept current.', 'Run two separate journeys, one for clients sending tenders and RFQs and one for job seekers, because both arrive every day and want completely different things.'],
  'wordpress-vs-webflow': ['Webflow suits design-led marketing sites where a team wants hosting, updates and security handled for a subscription, and can live within its CMS and plan limits.', 'WordPress suits sites that need ownership, a choice of hosting, Indian payment gateways, lots of content or specific features, and it has a far larger pool of developers in India.', 'Both can rank well and load fast; the bigger differences are how you pay, how easily you can move later and who will look after the site.'],
  'website-ready-for-google-ads': ['Before launch, give every ad group a matching landing page that loads fast on a mid-range phone, and test it on mobile data.', 'Set up and test conversion tracking for forms, calls and WhatsApp clicks with real test enquiries, so no click is counted twice or missed.', 'Check that forms deliver, policy pages and business details are in place, and someone is ready to answer leads during the hours your ads run.'],
  'website-copywriting-mistakes': ['Most weak website copy fails the same way: it is vague, talks about the business instead of the customer, and leaves the next step unclear.', 'Replace claims with specifics you can back up, answer the questions that stop people buying, and write in the words customers use when they search.', 'Test your copy by reading it aloud, checking it on a phone and asking whether a competitor could paste it onto their site unchanged.'],
  'woocommerce-abandoned-cart-recovery': ['WooCommerce has no built-in cart recovery: a plugin or email platform captures checkout details and sends reminders with a link that restores the cart.', 'Treat failed and pending payments separately from abandoned carts, and fix checkout friction first, because reminders cannot rescue a broken checkout.', 'Send two or three helpful reminders, keep discounts for the last one if at all, get opt-in for WhatsApp, and tag reminder links so you can measure recovered sales.'],
  'woocommerce-shipping-setup-india': ['Enter packed weights and box dimensions for every product, because couriers charge the higher of actual and volumetric weight and missing weights break rate rules.', 'Order zones from most specific to least, such as local pin codes, your state, remote regions and the rest of India, since WooCommerce uses the first zone that matches.', 'Allow COD only on the shipping methods and pin codes your courier supports, connect an aggregator or courier for labels and tracking, and test real pin codes before launch.'],
  'local-landing-pages-without-doorway-pages': ['SEO doorway pages are near-identical pages built to rank for many city or keyword variations that send everyone to the same place; Google\'s spam policies treat them as spam.', 'A city or area page is fine when it covers a place you genuinely serve and says things true only of that place: the team that covers it, local jobs and reviews, coverage and response times.', 'If you have nothing local to say, one strong service page with a clear list of areas beats a thin page per city, and existing doorway pages are best merged and redirected.'],
  'privacy-policy-cookie-basics-india': ['Any Indian site with forms, orders, analytics or a pixel needs a plain-language privacy policy explaining what it collects and why.', 'List every third-party tool that receives visitor data, from chat widgets and maps to couriers and CRMs, and update the list when tools change.', 'Cookie banners and Consent Mode v2 matter mainly for EU and UK visitors; Indian sites still need honest consent wording on forms.'],
  'wordpress-user-roles-explained': ['WordPress roles are bundles of capabilities: Administrators control everything, Editors manage all content, Authors publish their own posts, Contributors submit drafts and Subscribers only manage their profile.', 'Give each person the lowest role that fits, remember that Authors cannot edit pages, and use Shop Manager rather than Administrator for WooCommerce staff.', 'Keep administrators to the owner and the current developer, protect Editor and Shop Manager accounts with 2FA, and remove access the day someone leaves.'],
  'uptime-monitoring-explained': ['An uptime monitor checks your site from outside every few minutes and alerts you by email, SMS or app when it stops responding properly.', 'A basic status check misses many failures, so add a keyword check on a key page, plus SSL certificate and domain expiry alerts.', 'Free tools such as UptimeRobot, Better Stack and Jetpack Monitor cover most small business sites; shorter intervals and SMS alerts suit stores and sites running ads.'],
  'website-speed-indian-mobile-networks': ['Indian visitors often browse on budget Android phones over 4G connections that vary by hour and place, so latency, JavaScript and page weight matter more than an office Wi-Fi test suggests.', 'Test the way customers browse: PageSpeed field data, Chrome DevTools with Slow 4G and CPU throttling, and a real mid-range phone on mobile data in a private tab.', 'Set a page weight budget, host close to your visitors, cut heavy scripts and fonts, and make calls, WhatsApp, forms and checkout work even on a weak connection.'],
  'logo-favicon-brand-basics-website': ['Get your logo as an SVG plus PNG versions, in horizontal, stacked, icon-only and reversed forms, and keep the original design files in your business name.', 'One simple square icon of at least 512 pixels covers the favicon and phone home-screen icons; WordPress generates the smaller sizes from it.', 'Set a 1200 by 630 social share image and an organisation logo in your SEO plugin, and use the same name, logo and colours everywhere customers see you.'],
  'website-accessibility-older-users': ['Older visitors need larger body text, strong contrast and big, well-spaced buttons, and the site must respect the bigger text size many of them set on their phones.', 'Use plain, familiar words, a short menu and short forms, and always offer a tap-to-call number and WhatsApp next to the form.', 'Remove auto-playing media, rotating sliders and pop-ups, show the details that build trust, and test the site by watching an older relative complete real tasks.'],
  'regain-website-access-old-developer': ['Start with the domain: whoever controls the registrar account controls DNS, and with it the website, the email and Search Console verification.', 'Accounts in your business name can be recovered from the provider with proof of ownership; accounts in the developer\'s name need their cooperation, so ask politely and in writing first.', 'Once you are back in, change every password, remove old administrators and keep a record of each login in your name, so the next handover takes minutes.'],
  'stop-contact-form-spam': ['Identify the kind of spam first: bots fall to honeypots, time checks and Turnstile, while pitches typed by real people need Akismet or keyword filters.', 'Layer light defences that are verified on the server, and avoid puzzles, country blocks and silent filters that turn real customers away.', 'Store form entries so nothing filtered is lost, strip visitor text from auto-replies, and send a test enquiry after every change.'],
  'business-email-deliverability-spf-dkim-dmarc': ['SPF lists who may send for your domain, DKIM signs each message, and DMARC tells receivers what to do when a message fails, so you need all three.', 'Keep one SPF record per domain, copy exact values from each provider, and start DMARC at p=none, moving to quarantine only when every real sender passes.', 'Send website form emails through an authenticated SMTP login, such as a domain mailbox or a Gmail app password, rather than the server\'s PHP mail function.'],
  'faq-page-seo': ['FAQs help SEO when they answer real customer questions directly, in the words people search, on the page where the question comes up.', 'Put most FAQs on the service, product or sales page they belong to; keep a separate FAQ page for general questions about ordering, delivery and policies.', 'On a sales page, answer the objections that stop people buying, keep each answer short and link to the detail.'],
  'website-for-insurance-financial-advisors': ['Show each registration exactly as it was issued, only if you hold it, and never let a distributor or agent role read like a fee-based advisory licence.', 'Calculators and articles build trust when their assumptions are visible and nothing on the page promises or implies a return.', 'A needs-based enquiry form that asks about life stage and goals, not income proofs or policy numbers, gets better conversations and keeps sensitive data off your website.'],
  'website-for-real-estate-agents-brokers': ['India has no shared MLS feed of the kind many US agent sites use, so choose a listing method you can keep current: manual entry, spreadsheet import or a CRM feed.', 'Resale brokers and new-project channel partners need different sites; state area measures, RERA numbers and availability precisely on every listing.', 'Show your RERA agent registration where it applies, write locality guides only for areas you work, and make every listing one tap away from a WhatsApp message.'],
  'common-wordpress-errors-fixes': ['Most WordPress errors come from a handful of causes: plugin or theme code, server limits such as memory and PHP version, the database, configuration files and SSL or DNS.', 'For any error, stop making changes, note what changed last, take a backup, then read the recovery mode email or the error log before guessing.', 'Use the quick-reference table to match the message to its usual cause, then follow the dedicated guide for that error.'],
  'ssl-certificate-errors-fix': ['Most SSL errors come down to five causes: no certificate, an expired one, one that does not cover the domain, an untrusted one, or pages loading files over plain http.', 'Read the exact error code first, check the certificate details and test from another device, so you know whether the problem is the site or the visitor\'s device.', 'Prevent repeats with auto-renewing certificates, expiry monitoring, one preferred domain version with redirects, and an SSL check after every migration or redesign.'],
  'what-is-a-cdn': ['A CDN is a network of servers in many cities that keeps copies of your site\'s files and serves each visitor from the nearest one, while your hosting stays the origin.', 'It helps most when visitors are far from your server or spread across regions, and it does nothing for a slow admin, a heavy theme or uncached pages.', 'Set it up with DNS records copied carefully, cart, checkout and admin pages bypassed, and caches purged after updates, then test forms and checkout.'],
  'google-maps-on-website': ['For one location, the free embed from the Share button in Google Maps is enough; the Maps JavaScript API is only worth it for store locators or custom styling.', 'A map iframe is heavy, so show a static image or address card first and load the real map only when the visitor taps it.', 'Customers and Google rely more on a clear address, timings, phone and a directions link than on the map itself, so get those right first.'],
  'website-analytics-metrics-that-matter': ['Start with key events, such as enquiries, calls, WhatsApp clicks and orders, then look at which sources and landing pages produce them.', 'Use Search Console for what people searched and which pages earn clicks, and treat page views, time on site and follower counts as context, not results.', 'A 30-minute monthly routine comparing key events, sources, landing pages and search queries with last month and last year beats a detailed report nobody reads.'],
  'mobile-first-design-explained': ['Mobile-first design means planning the phone layout first and adding to it for bigger screens, because that is where many visitors decide whether to call or leave.', 'Google mainly uses the mobile version of your pages for indexing, so content, links and structured data must be on mobile, not only on desktop.', 'Big tap targets, short forms with the right keyboards, a sticky call and WhatsApp bar and a light page on a mid-range phone over 4G matter more than desktop polish.'],
  'website-for-home-services': ['Design for a worried person on a phone: the call and WhatsApp buttons should be visible on every screen, with your hours and response time beside them.', 'Build area pages only where you can say something true and specific about working there; otherwise list your areas on one page and in your Business Profile.', 'Explaining visiting charges, what is extra and how technicians are verified removes the two biggest doubts customers have before letting someone in.'],
  'website-for-cleaning-services': ['Publish the cleaning checklist for each package, because customers compare what is included before price.', 'Price by package, room count or area, and let customers book a slot online that matches how your teams work.', 'Describe staff checks accurately, name your products and give each service area its own useful page.'],
  'website-for-packers-movers': ['Customers arrive worried about fake movers, so lead with details they can verify: registered name, GST number, a real address, your own trucks and genuine reviews.', 'Ask for what changes the price, such as inventory, floors, lift access and dates, and list inclusions and extras before you quote.', 'Explain insurance and claims plainly, mention approvals such as IBA only if you genuinely hold them, and write route pages only for routes you really run.'],
  'website-for-hospitals': ['Design for the most urgent visitor first: the emergency number, ambulance contact and directions should be reachable in one tap from every page.', 'A doctor directory with qualifications, departments and OPD timings, kept accurate by a named person, is usually the most used part of a hospital site.', 'Insurance, TPA and cashless lists, accreditation and package prices change often, so show them only as accurately as you can maintain them, with a date.'],
  'website-for-physiotherapy-clinics': ['Build the menu around the pain or injury people search for, and give each condition a page that explains the assessment, the treatment and when to see a doctor instead.', 'Describe the first assessment, home visits and session packages in practical detail, and let the therapist recommend the number of sessions after assessing the patient.', 'State therapist qualifications precisely, check current council guidance on titles, and talk about recovery as goals and progress rather than promises.'],
  'website-for-eye-clinics-opticians': ['Treat the clinic and the optical store as two front doors on one site: careful, factual medical pages on one side, and a catalogue that sells like a shop on the other.', 'State doctor and optometrist credentials precisely, explain tests and surgery in plain language without promised outcomes, and check current medical advertising guidance before launch.', 'Explain lens options without upselling, handle prescriptions with care, offer separate appointment types for each service, and design for visitors who cannot see well.'],
  'website-for-veterinary-pet-clinics': ['Put emergency information first and make it exact: the hours you really take emergencies, the number to call, and where owners should go when you are closed.', 'Give each species you treat its own section, say honestly which animals you refer elsewhere, and let your vets set the vaccination schedule shown on the site.', 'State each vet\'s degrees, university, year and council registration precisely, and check the licensing rules before selling medicines through the website.'],
  'website-for-bakeries-cake-shops': ['Separate ready-to-order cakes, which can be bought straight from the menu, from custom cakes, which start with a detailed form, a quote and an advance payment.', 'Make the delivery date, time slot, pin code and daily capacity part of the order itself, so the website never accepts a cake your kitchen cannot bake or deliver on time.', 'Label eggless options, allergens and FSSAI details clearly, show real photos of your own cakes, and plan festival pre-orders before the rush starts.'],
  'website-for-florists-gift-shops': ['The person paying is rarely the person receiving, so checkout must collect both sets of details, the card message and a delivery slot, and keep prices out of the parcel.', 'Build pages around occasions and recipients, show cut-off times and the earliest delivery slot on every product, and state your substitution policy for seasonal flowers.', 'Plan Valentine\'s Day, Mother\'s Day, Rakhi and Diwali weeks ahead, with pre-orders, slot limits and campaign landing pages, and give corporate buyers their own quote route.'],
  'website-for-wedding-venues-banquet-halls': ['Give every hall, lawn and terrace its own page with capacities stated by layout, because "up to 1,000 guests" means nothing until families know whether that is seated or standing.', 'Explain packages by what they include and what costs extra, and let families check a date quickly without pretending to show a live calendar you do not keep.', 'Treat the site visit as the goal: real photos and video of each space, partner and policy details, then a fast route from enquiry to a booked visit.'],
  'website-for-coworking-spaces': ['Show plans side by side with what each includes, and make booking a tour the easiest action on every page.', 'Day passes and meeting rooms sell best when members can book and pay online with UPI or cards without calling.', 'Give each centre its own page with real photos, address, timings and amenities instead of one generic page.'],
  'website-for-hostels-pg-accommodation': ['Describe each room type the way residents compare them: sharing, bathroom, cooling, furniture, what the rent includes and which beds are free.', 'Set out rent, deposit, notice period and extra charges, or say clearly what is on request, and write food plans and house rules out in full.', 'List only the safety measures you really have, give honest commute times, and make booking a visit or a video tour the next step on every page.'],
  'website-for-driving-schools': ['Organise the site around the learner, from nervous beginners and licence holders who never drove to scooter learners and busy professionals, and point each to the right course.', 'State exactly what each package includes and how slots are booked, rescheduled and counted, and introduce the instructors and cars learners will meet.', 'Explain learner\'s licence and test steps in general terms with links to the official portal, never promise a licence, and mention women instructors only if you genuinely have them.'],
  'website-for-music-dance-academies': ['Give each instrument, vocal style or dance form its own course page with age groups, levels, batch timings and what a beginner learns in the first months.', 'Make the trial class the main goal of the site, with a short form that asks for the student, the course and preferred days rather than just a phone number.', 'Mention grade exams only if you genuinely prepare students for them, film recitals with parental consent, and introduce every teacher by training and teaching style.'],
  'website-for-home-tutors-online-teachers': ['Parents check three things before booking a tutor: whether you teach their child\'s subject and board, whether they can trust you, and how classes, timings and fees work.', 'Make a demo class the main action, with a short booking form for class, board, subject, mode and preferred time, and a clear note on whether the demo is free.', 'State qualifications and results precisely and only with consent, explain how home and online sessions keep children safe, and target searches by subject, class and area.'],
  'website-for-handicraft-artisan-brands': ['Tell the story of the craft and the people who make it with their consent and in specific, checkable terms, and use GI names, handloom marks or fair trade claims only where they are genuinely held.', 'Explain what varies between handmade pieces and what does not, photograph texture and scale clearly, and give exact materials, dimensions, care and lead times on every product.', 'Treat retail, overseas and trade buyers as separate routes: a store for single pieces, clear shipping and breakage terms for fragile items, and a wholesale page with MOQs and a quote form.'],
  'website-for-agriculture-businesses': ['Build product pages around crops, seasons and regions, so a farmer or retailer can see what suits their field before they call.', 'Make the dealer locator and WhatsApp the main routes to buy, and put key pages in Marathi, Hindi or whichever languages your buyers actually read.', 'Keep dosage and performance wording inside the approved label and current seed, fertiliser and pesticide rules, and never promise yields.'],
  'website-for-chemical-pharma-manufacturers': ['Build the catalogue around how buyers search: product name, synonyms, CAS number and grade, with specifications in a table on each product page.', 'Decide which documents are free downloads and which are sent on request, and list only certifications and registrations you hold, with scope and validity.', 'Use an RFQ form that asks for grade, quantity, packing, destination and documents required, so your sales team can quote without a round of emails.'],
  'website-for-textile-manufacturers': ['Give every fabric its own page with the specifications a sourcing team copies into its sheet: composition, construction, GSM, width, finish, tests, MOQ and lead time.', 'Publish your sampling process step by step, from swatch cards to lab dips and bulk approval, and let small buyers order swatches or sample lengths online.', 'Show certifications only with their scope, certificate number and validity, and name the brands you supply only with their written permission.'],
  'website-for-hardware-building-materials': ['Organise the catalogue by category and by brand, the two ways customers ask at the counter, and show only brands you are authorised to sell.', 'Sell small, stable-price items online if it suits you, and keep volatile or bulky materials such as steel, cement and plywood on rate requests or quotes.', 'Give contractors a fast route for material lists and bulk orders, state delivery areas and unloading terms exactly, and keep your Google Business Profile in step with the site.'],
  'google-search-console-reports-explained': ['Performance, Pages, URL Inspection and Sitemaps do most of the work for a small business; Manual actions and Security issues should simply stay empty.', 'Each report answers a different question: how you appear in search, whether pages are indexed, how Google sees one URL, and whether speed, HTTPS or structured data need fixing.', 'A short monthly routine across the reports catches indexing problems, lost pages and new opportunities long before they show up as lost enquiries.'],
  'ai-tools-website-content-responsibly': ['Google\'s guidance, at the time of writing, judges content by how helpful it is rather than how it was made, but mass-producing pages mainly to rank breaks its spam policies whatever the method.', 'Use AI for outlines, first drafts from your own notes and tidy rewrites, then add what only you know: real examples, photos, local detail and the questions customers actually ask.', 'Check every fact, price, rule and number before publishing, never let AI write reviews, testimonials or case studies, and keep customer data out of AI tools.'],
  'website-maintenance-vs-management': ['Website maintenance keeps a site secure, backed up, updated and working; website management uses the site to grow the business through content, SEO, campaigns and conversion improvements.', 'Every business site needs maintenance; management makes sense when the website is a main source of leads or sales and someone has time and budget to improve it.', 'Providers use the words loosely, so judge any plan by its list of tasks, the hours or requests included and the monthly report, not by its name.'],
  'wordpress-multisite-when-needed': ['WordPress Multisite runs many sites from one installation and suits one team managing many similar sites, such as franchise outlets, branches or departments.', 'For a few unrelated sites, a multilingual site or several locations of one business, a single site or separate installations is usually simpler and safer.', 'Google treats every site in a network as a separate site, so each one needs its own SEO settings, sitemap, Search Console checks and genuinely unique content.'],
  'website-security-headers-explained': ['Five headers are quick, low-risk wins: X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options set to SAMEORIGIN, Referrer-Policy, a simple Permissions-Policy and HSTS once HTTPS works everywhere.', 'Content-Security-Policy is the most powerful and the easiest to get wrong on WordPress, so run it in report-only mode and fix every violation before enforcing it.', 'Add headers in one place, test them with securityheaders.com and your browser, and remember they harden a clean site but never replace updates, 2FA and backups.'],
  'conversion-rate-optimization-basics': ['Conversion rate optimization means getting more enquiries, calls, WhatsApp messages or orders from the visitors you already have, starting with tracking those actions properly in GA4.', 'Find the leaks before changing anything: GA4 shows which pages and devices underperform, and Microsoft Clarity\'s heatmaps and recordings show what visitors actually do there.', 'On a low-traffic site, make clearly better changes and compare before and after rather than A/B testing small tweaks, and judge results by lead quality as well as numbers.'],
  'redesign-website-tight-budget': ['Spend a small redesign budget on the pages that bring enquiries, usually the homepage, top service pages and contact page, and phase everything else.', 'Keep the platform and URLs where you can, adapt a quality lightweight theme, cut the number of unique templates and prepare content yourself to reduce cost without cutting quality.', 'Never save money on redirects, backups, mobile testing, form testing, licensed software or owning your domain and hosting; those cuts cost far more later.'],
  'seasonal-festival-campaigns-website': ['Give each festival one permanent page, such as a Diwali gifts page, and update it every year instead of creating a new URL that has to start from zero each season.', 'Prepare weeks ahead: offers and coupons scheduled, order-by dates on every page, gift guides by budget and recipient, and the site tested for speed and checkout before traffic rises.', 'Tag every campaign link and track enquiries, WhatsApp clicks and orders, then review what worked while it is fresh so next year starts from evidence, not memory.'],
  'website-content-calendar': ['Pick three to five themes tied to the services you sell, then place topics in the months when customers actually search for them, including festivals and seasons.', 'Choose a publishing pace you can keep for a whole year, and keep a slot each month for updating an older post as well as writing new ones.', 'Run the calendar from one spreadsheet with the topic, target service page, owner, dates and status, and review it each month against Search Console.'],
  'video-on-business-website': ['Video helps most where seeing beats reading: short explainers, customer testimonials, product demos and tours of your premises, placed beside the text they support.', 'Host videos on YouTube or Vimeo rather than your web hosting, and embed them with a click-to-play preview image so the player loads only when someone taps it.', 'Add captions and a short written summary, keep autoplaying background videos off key pages, and use a phone, good light and a clip-on mic to film on a budget.'],
  'website-for-taxi-car-rental': ['Organise the site by trip type, local, outstation, airport and events, because each customer worries about different things and needs different booking fields.', 'Show what makes up a fare, such as driver allowance, tolls, parking and state taxes, whether you publish fixed fares, give estimates or quote on request.', 'Show your own cars and verified drivers, describe permits in general terms, and write route pages only for routes you drive regularly, with details that are specific to each.'],
  'website-for-immigration-visa-consultants': ['Organise services by visa category and destination, and say plainly which decisions rest with the immigration authorities rather than with you.', 'Show a licence or registration only if your firm or adviser genuinely holds it, with the number and a link to the public register where one exists.', 'Split professional fees from government and third-party charges, publish a refund policy, and add a scam warning page that tells clients how you will and will not contact them.'],
  'website-for-medical-equipment-suppliers': ['Write for everyone who signs off a purchase: the biomedical engineer wants specifications, the purchase team wants documents and a quote form, and the clinician wants to know how it fits their work.', 'Publish only the registrations, licences and certificates you actually hold, with numbers, validity and the products they cover, and check current medical device rules first.', 'Give after-sales service its own pages, with AMC and CMC terms, engineer locations and spares support, because service often decides the order.'],
  'website-for-authors-content-creators': ['Social platforms and bookstores control your reach and your customer data; your own website and email list are the parts of your audience you keep.', 'Give readers, brands, event organisers and journalists separate routes: book or content pages, a newsletter sign-up, a media kit with a collaboration form, and a speaker page.', 'Sell signed copies, merchandise and digital products directly where it makes sense, and keep links to bookstores and platforms for everything else.'],
  'migrate-blogger-to-wordpress': ['Export your Blogger content as a backup file, import it into WordPress on staging, then pull every image into the media library so nothing still loads from Blogger.', 'Matching the Blogger post URL pattern in WordPress permalinks avoids most redirects; pages, labels and anything else that changes need 301 redirects to the closest new page.', 'A custom domain moves cleanly with a DNS change, while a blogspot.com address cannot send server redirects, so keep the old blog live and plan for a slower handover.'],
  'migrate-shopify-to-woocommerce': ['Export products, customers, orders, content and every URL from Shopify before you start, and keep the store open until the new one is verified.', 'Setting the WooCommerce product base to /products/ and the category base to collections keeps most URLs, and a pattern redirect handles nested collection URLs.', 'Rebuild payments, COD, shipping and GST invoices for India, test real orders on a phone, then switch the domain in a quiet sales period.'],
  'xml-sitemaps-explained': ['An XML sitemap lists the URLs you want search engines to crawl; it helps Google find pages, but it does not make Google index or rank them.', 'List only canonical, indexable pages that return a 200 status, keep lastmod honest, and let one plugin generate the sitemap rather than two.', 'Submit the sitemap index once in Search Console, then use the Pages report filtered to submitted pages to see which listed URLs Google has left out and why.'],
  'robots-txt-explained': ['Robots.txt controls which URLs crawlers may visit, not which pages appear in Google; to keep a page out of search results, use noindex and leave it crawlable.', 'Most WordPress sites need only a short file that blocks the admin area, allows admin-ajax.php and lists the XML sitemap, without blocking CSS, JavaScript or uploads.', 'Check the live file after every launch or migration, test important URLs with URL Inspection, and decide on AI crawlers deliberately rather than by accident.'],
  'canonical-tags-explained': ['A canonical tag tells Google which of several near-identical URLs you prefer, but it is a strong hint rather than an order, so redirects, internal links and your sitemap must agree with it.', 'Most pages should have a self-referencing canonical, which WordPress and SEO plugins add automatically; paginated pages, language versions and genuinely different filter pages should point to themselves.', 'Check "Duplicate, Google chose different canonical than user" in Search Console and fix common mistakes such as canonicals pointing to an old domain, the homepage, a redirected URL or page 1.'],
  'duplicate-content-explained': ['The duplicate content penalty is mostly a myth: Google groups duplicate URLs and shows one, unless the copying is meant to deceive or manipulate results.', 'The real costs are split ranking signals, the wrong URL shown in results and wasted crawling, all fixed by making redirects, canonicals, links and sitemaps agree.', 'Fix in order: one preferred domain version, merged competing pages, canonicals for necessary duplicates, noindex for thin archives, then rewritten copied text.'],
  '301-vs-302-redirects': ['Use a 301 when a page has moved for good, and a 302 only when the original URL will genuinely come back, such as during short maintenance or a test.', 'A 301 tells Google to replace the old URL with the new one in results; a long-running 302 keeps Google guessing and can leave the old URL indexed.', 'Avoid redirect chains, redirecting everything to the homepage and forgetting to update internal links, sitemaps and canonicals after a redirect.'],
  'keep-visitors-engaged-website': ['Visitors stay when the first screen confirms they are in the right place, the page loads quickly on a phone, and the content is easy to scan.', 'Every page needs an obvious next step that fits what the visitor wants next, such as a related guide, a case study, a calculator or WhatsApp, linked from inside the content.', 'Measure engaged sessions and key events by landing page in GA4 rather than chasing bounce rate or time on site, because the goal is enquiries, not longer visits.'],
  'how-long-does-seo-take': ['SEO has no fixed timeline: technical fixes show as Google recrawls, while competitive searches take much longer.', 'New sites with few links usually start with specific long-tail and local searches before broad terms.', 'Judge progress by impressions and clicks for relevant queries in positions 1 to 20, not by a promised date.'],
  'seo-vs-google-ads': ['Google Ads buys visibility from the day a campaign starts and stops when spending stops; SEO earns free clicks but takes months to build.', 'Start with Ads when you need enquiries soon or want to test demand; prioritise SEO when you can invest for the medium term and buyers research first.', 'Most small businesses do best with both: a technically sound site with strong service pages, focused ads on your most profitable services, and enquiries tracked by channel.'],
  'seo-red-flags-scams': ['Guaranteed rankings, secret methods, bulk backlinks and reports you cannot verify are the clearest SEO red flags.', 'A fair SEO retainer covers technical fixes, content, local SEO and honest reporting from your own Search Console data.', 'Keep your Google accounts in your own name, ask for sample reports and avoid long lock-in contracts without exit terms.'],
  'ethical-link-building-small-business': ['Good backlinks come from real relationships: suppliers, partners, associations, local media and customers who already know you.', 'Useful resources such as guides, calculators and local data earn links that paid schemes cannot match, without the risk.', 'A small monthly routine of outreach, mentions and fixes builds authority steadily; avoid bought links and link exchanges at scale.'],
  'business-directories-citations-india': ['A small set of listings matters: maps platforms, a few general local directories, the platforms for your industry, and the associations you belong to.', 'Write one master version of your name, address and phone before listing anywhere, claim existing listings before creating new ones, and clean up duplicates and old details.', 'Skip packages that submit you to hundreds of directories and invoices for search engine listing; judge paid listings only on the enquiries they bring, tracked in one sheet.'],
  'handle-negative-reviews': ['Reply to every negative review calmly and briefly: thank, acknowledge, apologise where it is fair, and move the conversation to a phone call or email.', 'Only reviews that break Google\'s policies, such as fake, off-topic, abusive or conflict-of-interest reviews, can be flagged; an honest bad experience stays, so your reply is what counts.', 'Balance the occasional bad review with a steady flow of genuine ones from customers you ask, never paid for, filtered or written for you.'],
  'website-for-property-management-companies': ['Give owners and tenants separate paths from the homepage, because one is choosing whom to trust with an asset and the other wants a home or a repair.', 'Compare packages in one table, explain the owner journey from first call to first rent, and show a sample of the monthly report owners will receive.', 'Speak directly to NRI owners, and describe agreements, police verification and tax points in general terms, pointing owners to their lawyer or CA for specifics.'],
  'write-product-descriptions-that-sell': ['Open with who the product is for and the main benefit, then give scannable details, a specifications table, care and delivery notes, and answers to common doubts.', 'Write your own copy instead of the manufacturer\'s text, use the words customers search with once or twice naturally, and keep facts accurate.', 'Put specifications into WooCommerce attributes so they show as a table and power filters, and start with your best sellers when the catalogue is large.'],
  'business-email-options': ['Pick business email by the tools your team already uses: Gmail and Docs suit Workspace, Outlook and Excel suit Microsoft 365, and Zoho suits tight budgets.', 'Keep email separate from website hosting so a hosting problem or migration never takes your inbox down.', 'Every service that sends as your domain, including billing and form tools, must be covered by one SPF record plus DKIM.'],
  'website-speed-test-tools-explained': ['PageSpeed Insights shows two things: real-user field data from the Chrome UX Report at the top and a simulated Lighthouse lab test below; decisions should rest on the field data.', 'Scores vary between runs and tools because of server response, caching, test location, device simulation and third-party scripts, so run tests several times and compare like with like.', 'Use Search Console for site-wide Core Web Vitals, GTmetrix or WebPageTest waterfalls to find the slow file, and aim to pass Core Web Vitals rather than to score 100.'],
  'does-a-local-shop-need-a-website': ['For most local shops, a complete Google Business Profile, WhatsApp Business and genuine reviews come first, because that is where nearby customers look.', 'A website earns its place when you want to be found for specific products or brands, take delivery or bulk orders, sell to businesses, or look more established than competitors.', 'A simple site of one to a few pages, with WhatsApp, click-to-call, a map and details that match your Google profile, is usually enough to start.'],
  'domain-seo-scam-emails': ['Only the registrar you bought your domain from can renew it, so invoices from any other company are sales pitches or scams.', 'Check every renewal or suspension notice by logging into your registrar or host directly, never through the email link.', 'Keep domain, hosting and email logins in the business name with two-step verification to cut the damage from phishing.'],
  'website-for-dermatology-skin-clinics': ['Patients search by concern, such as hair fall or dark patches, so map each concern to condition pages and then to the treatments a dermatologist may suggest.', 'Give every procedure a plain fact panel covering who performs it, sessions, downtime, aftercare and who it does not suit, before any persuasion.', 'Publish before-and-after photos only with written consent, consistent lighting and honest captions, after checking current medical advertising and ad platform rules.'],
  'website-for-ayurveda-wellness-centres': ['Separate day treatments from residential programmes, because a local patient booking a consultation and an overseas guest planning a retreat need different pages.', 'State doctors\' BAMS and postgraduate degrees with university, year and registration, and describe each therapy and programme in concrete terms.', 'Avoid cure claims and disease lists, check the current advertising rules for Indian systems of medicine, and keep visa and medical advice off the booking pages.'],
  'website-for-fertility-clinics': ['Design for someone reading in private and often anxious: calm pages, plain language, and nothing on screen or in a message preview that gives their situation away.', 'Explain IVF, IUI, ICSI, egg freezing and male fertility tests step by step, without success-rate headlines, guarantees or baby photos used as proof.', 'Ask for the minimum in booking forms, keep advertising pixels off treatment pages, and check the current ART rules before writing about registration, donors or eligibility.'],
  'website-for-pest-control-companies': ['Give each pest and each kind of property its own page, because a family with cockroaches and a restaurant facing an audit ask different questions.', 'Make an inspection easy to book from every page, and set out AMC plans so visits, pests covered, exclusions and reports can be compared.', 'Describe chemical safety in exact, modest words, never claim approvals you do not hold, and show only your own job photos, with permission.'],
  'website-for-ev-dealers': ['First-time EV buyers need explanations more than brochures: give every model a page that explains range, charging time and battery warranty in plain words, with the test basis stated.', 'Never publish on-road prices or subsidy amounts you cannot keep current; use an on-road price form, link subsidy information to official sources and date every figure you show.', 'Turn the site into a sales and service tool: test ride booking, a charging guide, a running cost calculator with visible assumptions, and service and battery health bookings.'],
  'website-for-mobile-laptop-repair': ['Structure the site as a matrix of devices and faults, with a page for each repair people actually search for, rather than one long list of everything you fix.', 'Give estimates as ranges you set, explain part options and diagnosis honestly, and get approval before any work beyond the estimate.', 'Earn trust with clear data-handling promises, warranty terms in your own words, repair status updates and a complete Google Business Profile for near me searches.'],
  'website-for-laundry-dry-cleaning': ['Publish a real price list as a web page, priced the way each item is actually charged: per kg for wash and fold, per piece for dry cleaning, per pair or per size for shoes, curtains and carpets.', 'Keep the pickup form short, check the area first, cap bookings per slot, and send order updates on WhatsApp from pickup count to delivery and payment.', 'Give monthly plans and bulk clients such as hostels, salons and hotels their own pages, and write a plain damage and missing-item policy backed by inspection at pickup.'],
  'website-for-catering-services': ['Design the enquiry form first: event type, date, guest count, venue, service style, cuisine and dietary needs, and a budget range give you enough to quote without a long phone call.', 'Show menu packages people can compare, and decide openly whether per-plate prices are published, shown as a starting price or given on request, with what the price includes.', 'Back the food with proof: tastings for larger bookings, a gallery of past events labelled by type and scale, hygiene practices you actually follow and your FSSAI details.'],
  'website-for-tiffin-meal-subscriptions': ['Start with a trial meal and prepaid weekly or monthly plans sold as normal products, and add automatic renewals only once your volume justifies the extra setup.', 'Publish this week\'s menu as a web page, list delivery areas and times you can actually keep, and make pausing and skipping simple with a clear cut-off.', 'Show your FSSAI details, real kitchen photos and honest food claims, and use WhatsApp for menus and delivery updates only with customers who have opted in.'],
  'website-for-trekking-adventure-operators': ['Treat each trek page as the product: grade on a scale you publish, a day-by-day itinerary, terrain, highest point, inclusions and exclusions, and the real way to reach the base.', 'Sell fixed departures as batches whose seat count drops automatically when a deposit is paid, with balance, cancellation and weather terms written out before anyone pays.', 'Describe safety by what your team actually does and carries, hedge permits and rules that change, and give weekend, Himalayan and corporate or school groups their own routes through the site.'],
  'website-for-overseas-education-consultants': ['Treat every country and course page as having an expiry date: link to official sources, show when it was last checked and review it before each intake.', 'Let an eligibility checker sort students towards the right counsellor, never promise admission or a visa, and call a university a partner only when you hold an agreement.', 'Ask the few questions that decide fit, such as intake, level, test status and budget band, so counsellors call the most ready students first.'],
  'website-for-recruitment-agencies': ['Give employers and candidates separate journeys from the homepage, each with its own pages, forms and calls to action, so hiring enquiries never get lost among CVs.', 'Publish every live job as its own page with JobPosting structured data and a closing date, and take filled roles down promptly so candidates and Google only see real openings.', 'Treat CVs as sensitive personal data: collect them through a secure form with a clear consent line, limit who can open them, delete them on a schedule and warn candidates about fake offers.'],
  'website-for-event-rental-businesses': ['An event rental website works best as a catalogue with an add-to-quote list, so customers send one request for everything their event needs.', 'Ask for the event date, venue and setup time first, because availability and logistics decide the quote more than the item list does.', 'Publish your deposit, damage, cancellation and setup rules on one terms page and link it from every quote, so there are no arguments on the event day.'],
  'website-for-sports-academies': ['Parents choose an academy on batch fit, coach, facility safety and fees, so those belong on the first screen.', 'Make the trial session the main call to action and confirm it with the batch, kit list and arrival time.', 'A multi-centre academy needs one page per centre with its own address, batches and trial form routed to that coordinator.'],
  'website-for-preschools-daycare': ['Lead with what parents check first: safety, who looks after the children, facilities, daily routine and timings, described as you actually run them rather than with vague claims.', 'Make the main action a school visit: a short enquiry form with the child\'s age, programme and a preferred visit slot, followed up quickly on WhatsApp.', 'Publish children\'s photos only with written parental consent, avoid pairing names with faces, and keep daily photos and CCTV in private parent channels, never on public pages.'],
  'website-for-tailoring-services': ['A tailoring website should follow the real order journey: design reference, measurements, price and date, trial fitting, then delivery.', 'Design photo uploads, a measurement visit request and a clear alterations list remove most of the back-and-forth that slows orders on WhatsApp.', 'Bridal and festival orders need their own page with lead times and booking cut-offs, because these customers plan weeks or months ahead.'],
  'news-magazine-websites-wordpress': ['Plan categories, authors and editorial roles before choosing a theme, because the archive grows faster than the design.', 'Caching, image handling and careful ad placement keep a large WordPress news site fast on Indian mobile networks.', 'Google News inclusion is decided by Google, so clear bylines, dates, contact details and policies matter more than any plugin.'],
  'one-page-vs-multi-page-website': ['A one-page website suits one offer, one audience and one action, such as an event, a single service, a launch or a campaign, or a quick start on a small budget.', 'Choose a multi-page website when you offer several services, serve several areas or audiences, or want to be found on Google for more than your name, because each page can target its own search.', 'You can start with one page and grow: plan sections that can become their own pages later, and keep the homepage URL and section links working when you do.'],
  'first-90-days-after-website-launch': ['Spend the first week proving everything works and is measured, then the first month getting key pages indexed and fixing what Search Console reports.', 'Months two and three are for trust and content: your Google Business Profile, genuine reviews, a few useful posts and improvements based on real search data.', 'Keep backups, updates and monitoring running from day one, and expect impressions to grow before clicks and enquiries do.'],
  'write-meta-titles-descriptions': ['Lead each meta title with the page\'s main search term and keep it near 50 to 60 characters so it survives on mobile.', 'For Indian shoppers, mention COD, free shipping thresholds or easy returns in descriptions only where the page offers them.', 'Write language-specific titles and descriptions only for pages whose content is actually in that language.'],
  'website-navigation-structure': ['Plan the page hierarchy around what visitors come to do, then build a short main menu of five to seven plain labels with one clear call to action.', 'Keep important pages within two or three clicks of the homepage, use the footer and breadcrumbs for secondary routes, and make the mobile menu work with a thumb.', 'Menus are only part of navigation: clean URLs that follow the structure and contextual links between related pages help visitors and Google find everything.'],
  'breadcrumbs-explained': ['Breadcrumbs are the trail of links near the top of a page that shows where it sits on your site and lets visitors move up a level in one tap.', 'They add internal links to your section pages, make your structure clear to Google and, with matching BreadcrumbList schema, can appear in search results in place of the URL.', 'On WordPress, switch them on in one source only, such as Rank Math, Yoast or WooCommerce, place them above the page heading, and test the markup after any change.'],
  'helpful-404-pages': ['A helpful 404 page returns a real 404 status, explains the problem and offers search, key links and contact options.', 'Soft 404s are pages that look missing or empty but return 200, and Search Console reports them separately.', 'Redirect old URLs that have links or traffic to the closest matching page, and leave genuinely dead URLs as 404s.'],
  'wordpress-comments-enable-or-disable': ['Most business websites are better with WordPress comments disabled; keep them only where real readers discuss posts and someone moderates daily.', 'Turning off comments needs three steps: Discussion settings for new posts, bulk edit for existing posts, and handling media attachment pages.', 'If you keep comments, require approval, use an anti-spam tool, disable links in comments and set old posts to close automatically.'],
  'add-blog-to-existing-website': ['Put the blog in a subfolder such as yourdomain.com/blog/ on the same platform as your site where you can; it keeps everything one site to manage, measure and link.', 'Match the design, set up a few categories that mirror your services, and handle sitemaps, Search Console and SEO settings before the first post goes live.', 'Start with a handful of posts that answer the questions customers ask before they buy, and link every post to the service page it relates to.'],
  'repurpose-website-content-social-media': ['Break each article into parts first, such as the core idea, a checklist, tips, a myth and a FAQ answer, then turn each part into the format that suits a platform.', 'Make every piece useful on its own, and link back to the full article with UTM-tagged links so GA4 shows which platforms bring visitors and enquiries.', 'Follow a simple weekly routine for one article, reshare evergreen pieces every few months, and drop formats that never bring anyone back to the website.'],
  'choose-website-colours-fonts': ['Build a small palette by role, with a primary, an accent for buttons, and neutrals for text and backgrounds, starting from your logo rather than colour psychology.', 'Check every text and button colour against WCAG contrast ratios; a brand colour that fails with white text needs a darker shade or dark text instead.', 'Use one or two font families with only the weights you need, body text of at least 16 pixels, and set colours and fonts once as global styles.'],
  'website-launch-checklist': ['Test content, mobile layout, forms, WhatsApp and calls on real phones before launch, not just on a desktop preview.', 'Turn off the discourage search engines and coming soon settings on launch day, then submit your sitemap in Search Console.', 'Keep a fresh backup, HTTPS, legal pages and your own logins in place before going live.'],
  'prepare-photos-for-website': ['Shoot real photos in soft natural light at the shape they will be used, landscape for banners and square for products, and take more than you think you need.', 'Resize each photo to roughly the largest size it is shown at, save it as WebP or a compressed JPEG, and give it a descriptive file name and alt text.', 'Get consent from anyone recognisable, then send full-quality files to your developer in folders by page, with a short sheet of captions and notes.'],
  'hire-developer-vs-diy-website': ['DIY suits simple, low-stakes sites when you have time to learn; hiring pays off when the website brings enquiries or sales, needs real features or has rankings to protect.', 'The biggest DIY cost is your time and the problems you cannot see, such as forms that fail silently, slow pages and missing SEO basics, not the subscription.', 'A common middle path is a developer-built foundation you edit yourself, with help hired only for technical work, and every account kept in your name either way.'],
  'write-homepage-that-converts': ['The first screen must say what you do, who it is for and what to do next, with one primary call to action, and it must pass that test on a phone.', 'A reliable order is hero, quick proof, services, why you, process, work, testimonials, FAQs and a final call to action, each linking to a deeper page.', 'Repeat one primary action through the page, use only proof you can back up, and judge changes by enquiries rather than opinions.'],
  'show-prices-on-website': ['Most service businesses benefit from some price guidance, such as a range, a starting-from price or packages, even when every job is quoted individually.', 'A price needs context next to it: what is included, what is not, whether GST is extra and what moves the cost up or down.', 'If you are unsure, add price guidance to one page or landing page and compare the number and quality of enquiries over a couple of months.'],
  'diy-website-audit': ['With a phone, a laptop and free tools you can check first impressions, mobile use, speed, contact paths, Google visibility, security, content, trust and analytics in under an hour.', 'Score each area from 0 to 2 as you go; anything that stops enquiries, such as a broken form or a wrong phone number, gets fixed first, whatever the total.', 'A few low scores usually mean targeted fixes or a refresh; low scores across mobile, speed, trust and Google visibility usually point to a redesign.'],
  'ga4-events-explained': ['GA4 records every interaction as an event; key events are the few you mark as business outcomes, such as enquiries, calls, WhatsApp clicks and purchases.', 'Enhanced measurement covers page views, scrolls, outbound clicks and downloads, but enquiries need their own events, best fired on a successful submission or thank-you page.', 'Use clear, consistent event names, mark only real outcomes as key events, and test every event in DebugView and Realtime before trusting the reports.'],
  'utm-tags-explained': ['UTM tags are labels added to a link so Google Analytics knows which source, medium and campaign sent a visitor, which matters most for WhatsApp, email, QR codes and social links.', 'Good UTM tagging means lowercase, consistent names, medium values GA4 recognises, one shared spreadsheet, and never tagging links between pages of your own website.', 'In GA4, switch Traffic acquisition to session source, medium or campaign and add key events, so you compare campaigns by enquiries rather than visits.'],
  'thank-you-pages-forms': ['A lead form thank you page redirect confirms the enquiry arrived, says honestly what happens next, offers a faster route such as WhatsApp, and gives you a reliable conversion to track.', 'Redirect only after a successful submission, keep the page out of Google with noindex rather than robots.txt, and give each important form its own thank-you URL.', 'Count each enquiry once: use a generate_lead key event set to once per session in GA4, set Google Ads to count one conversion, and test with a real submission.'],
  'wordpress-caching-explained': ['WordPress cache means saved copies of pages, files, queries and compiled PHP; page caching from your host or one plugin gives the biggest speed gain.', 'Server-side caching (LiteSpeed, Nginx, Varnish) is usually faster than plugin-only caching; use one page cache, never two.', 'Never cache cart, checkout, account or logged-in pages, clear caches in order (plugin, server, CDN, browser) and test while logged out.'],
  'woocommerce-gst-invoices-india': ['Enable taxes, calculate tax on the shipping address, create a tax class per GST rate, and add CGST and SGST for your home state with IGST for every other state.', 'WooCommerce has no HSN field, GSTIN checkout field or GST invoice by default, so add them with a maintained invoice plugin or custom fields and test the PDF.', 'Rates, HSN codes, invoice format, shipping tax and e-invoicing depend on your products and turnover, so confirm every setting with your CA before launch.'],
  'woocommerce-cash-on-delivery-india': ['Enable COD under WooCommerce payments, limit it to the shipping methods your courier supports, and add rules for order value, pin code or product with a plugin or a little code.', 'Confirm COD orders by WhatsApp, SMS or a call before dispatch, hold unconfirmed ones, and use a COD fee, a prepaid incentive or a partial advance to filter non-serious buyers.', 'Act quickly on failed delivery reports, reconcile courier COD remittances against your orders every week, and tighten rules where refusals keep happening.'],
  'website-ownership-checklist': ['Ownership means the account is in your business name on an email you control; access is what you grant developers through their own logins.', 'Use role addresses such as admin@ as account owners so a departing employee does not take the domain or Google accounts with them.', 'Keep a dated ownership record of every account, its owner and who has access, and review it whenever staff or developers change.'],
  'website-for-nutritionists-dietitians': ['Describe each consultation format, online, in clinic or by phone, with its length, what happens in it and what the client receives afterwards.', 'State your qualifications exactly as awarded, name the body behind any credential, and keep every result claim free of guarantees.', 'Use recipes and articles to show how you work, and publish before and after photos only with written consent and a note that results vary.'],
  'website-for-psychologists-counsellors': ['State qualifications and any registration exactly as they are, and explain what you do and do not offer, because the reader is deciding whether you are safe to contact.', 'Keep booking forms minimal and private: no case history, discreet notifications, and tracking scripts kept away from pages about specific concerns.', 'Every page needs a short notice that the practice is not an emergency service, pointing to emergency services and national helplines, and the site should promise no outcomes.'],
  'update-php-version-wordpress': ['Old PHP versions stop getting security fixes and newer plugins drop support for them, so plan an upgrade rather than waiting for your host to force one.', 'Before switching, take a backup, update WordPress, plugins and theme, replace abandoned plugins and test the new version on staging; PHP Compatibility Checker is only a first pass.', 'Switch in hPanel or cPanel, check that extensions such as mysqli, curl, mbstring, intl, zip and gd are enabled for the new version, test key pages and forms, and switch back if anything breaks.'],
  'wordpress-database-optimization': ['Take a tested backup before any cleanup; database changes cannot be undone with the browser back button.', 'Large autoloaded options from old plugins usually slow a site more than revisions or spam, so check the autoload size before deleting anything else.', 'Limit revisions, clear expired transients on a schedule and remove leftover plugin tables so the database stays lean without frequent manual cleanups.'],
  'website-design-contract-checklist': ['A website contract or proposal should name every page and feature, list what is excluded, and say who supplies content and how many revision rounds are included.', 'Payment milestones, change requests, ownership of the site and accounts, the support period and how either side can end the project cause most disputes, so write them down.', 'It does not need to be long, but it should be in writing and accepted by both sides; have a lawyer review anything high-value or unusual.'],
  'wordpress-form-plugins-compared': ['All the common form plugins handle a standard contact or quote form; the differences are stored entries, spam tools, conditional logic, file uploads, multi-step forms and integrations, and which of those are free.', 'No form plugin fixes email delivery on its own, so pair any of them with SMTP, stored entries and spam protection, then send a test enquiry from your phone.', 'Choose by the forms you actually need and who will maintain them, and check the current plans of each plugin, because what is free and what is paid changes.'],
  'website-for-astrologers-vastu-consultants': ['Treat each consultation as a product with a format, duration, fee, what the client must send beforehand and what they receive afterwards.', 'Avoid guaranteed outcomes such as assured marriage, cures or business success; describe what a consultation involves and let clients judge.', 'Content in Hindi or your regional language, evergreen guides rather than thin daily horoscopes, and genuine reviews build more trust than volume.'],
  'website-for-photographers': ['Organise the portfolio by specialism and show complete stories, but export images at display size, serve WebP and lazy-load everything except the first image so galleries stay fast on phones.', 'Deliver work through private proofing galleries with favourites, downloads and an expiry date, kept out of search and away from your main hosting if storage is tight.', 'Use an enquiry form that asks for date, city, venue, events and coverage, and support it with image SEO: descriptive file names, alt text, captions and a story for every shoot.'],
  'website-for-coaches-consultants': ['Visitors decide in seconds whether a coach is for them, so the homepage headline should name who you help and the change you help them make.', 'Give each offer its own page with format, duration and who it is not for, and make the discovery call the single next step from every page.', 'Proof works when it is specific and permitted: client stories with consent, talks you have given, and no income or outcome promises.'],
  'website-for-wholesalers-distributors': ['A distributor website should help trade buyers find products fast, check terms and send a bulk enquiry or order without a phone call.', 'Choose between public prices, prices on request and a dealer login based on how much your pricing varies by customer.', 'Quick order forms, reorder lists and WhatsApp hand-offs suit Indian retailers who reorder the same items regularly.'],
  'website-for-colleges-universities': ['Give every programme its own page with eligibility, duration, curriculum, fees or fee structure, careers and an enquiry button, because programme pages match what students actually search for.', 'Build admissions as a funnel, from programme page to enquiry, counsellor follow-up, campus visit, application and fee payment, with dates staff can update in one place.', 'State approvals, affiliations, accreditations and placement figures only exactly as held and verifiable, and keep mandatory disclosure, anti-ragging and grievance pages current.'],
  'website-for-elder-care-home-nursing': ['The reader is often an adult son or daughter in another city or country, so write for someone who cannot visit before deciding and needs proof of process.', 'Separate what nurses, attendants and physiotherapists do, and describe vetting, training and supervision as the steps you actually follow.', 'A care plan enquiry that asks the right questions, clear service areas and an explained routine for family updates do more for trust than any slogan.'],
  'website-for-sweet-shops-mithai': ['Plan the website around the festival calendar, with gift box and pre-order pages ready weeks before each season rather than days.', 'Let shelf life decide delivery: fresh milk sweets within your own delivery radius, dry fruit sweets and namkeen by courier further away.', 'Give corporate Diwali buyers their own page and form, and choose between a full online checkout and WhatsApp ordering based on how your counter actually works.'],
  'website-chatbot-worth-it': ['Most small business sites get more from a clear WhatsApp button and a good form than from a chatbot.', 'A chatbot helps when you get many repeat questions or enquiries outside working hours, but it adds scripts, cost and privacy duties.', 'If you add an AI bot, limit it to your own approved content, give it a clear hand-off to a human, and review its conversations.'],
  'keyword-research-small-business': ['Start with the words customers use on calls and WhatsApp, then expand them with Search Console, Google autocomplete, People also ask and Keyword Planner ranges.', 'Collect local modifiers, city spellings and Hinglish or Marathi variants, map every keyword group to one page by intent, and prioritise searches close to a buying decision that you can realistically win.', 'Keep a simple keyword map so two pages never target the same search, and revisit it every few months with fresh Search Console data.'],
  'search-intent-explained': ['Search intent is the goal behind a query; Google ranks the page type that best serves that goal, so a great page of the wrong type rarely ranks.', 'Read intent from the current results: the page types, formats and angles on page one tell you what Google thinks searchers want.', 'Give each intent its own page, such as a guide for learning, a comparison for choosing and a service page for hiring, and link them together.'],
  'topic-clusters-pillar-pages': ['In WordPress, build the pillar as a page, usually the service page you want to rank, and the cluster articles as posts that link to it in the body text.', 'Plan the cluster from real searches first, give each article one question, and avoid two pages targeting the same query.', 'Contextual links do most of the work; categories, breadcrumbs and a table of contents on the pillar support them.'],
  'update-old-blog-posts': ['Start with posts that are losing clicks, sit just off page one or get impressions without clicks in Search Console, and give priority to posts that bring enquiries.', 'Decide whether each post should be updated, merged, redirected or removed, then refresh it in place: answer first, correct the facts, cover current questions and add internal links.', 'Keep the URL, show an honest updated date, request indexing, and compare the same length of time before and after so you know whether the refresh worked.'],
  'google-algorithm-updates-small-business': ['Core updates reassess how helpful and relevant pages are across the web, while spam updates target sites breaking Google\'s spam policies, so each needs a different response.', 'Before blaming an update, match your Search Console dates to the Search Status Dashboard, wait for the rollout to finish and rule out tracking, site changes and seasonality.', 'Recovery comes from genuinely better pages and removing spam tactics, usually over months and often not until a later update, so nobody can promise a date.'],
  'write-blog-posts-that-rank': ['Start from a question customers actually search, check what Google already ranks for it, and outline a post that answers it more clearly and more specifically than those pages.', 'Add what only your business knows, such as real examples, local detail and photos of your work, then handle headings, internal links, images and the title before publishing.', 'Publishing is the midpoint: share the post, link to it from related pages, and update it once Search Console shows which searches it appears for.'],
  'woocommerce-returns-refunds-policy': ['A good returns policy states what can be returned or exchanged, the window from delivery, the condition required, how to ask, who pays return shipping, and how and when refunds are paid.', 'Issue refunds from the WooCommerce order screen so stock, totals and emails stay correct; refunds go back automatically only if your gateway plugin supports it.', 'For COD orders, collect bank or UPI details through a secure form, record the transaction reference, and treat RTO parcels separately from customer returns.'],
  'woocommerce-product-variations': ['Product-type variations come from choosing Variable product in the Product type dropdown, adding attributes used for variations, then generating variations.', 'Each variation can have its own price, SKU, stock and image; a variation without a price will not show on the product page.', 'Use global attributes for options shared across products, keep combinations to what you really stock, and add swatches and size charts to help buyers choose.'],
  'woocommerce-inventory-management': ['WooCommerce has solid built-in inventory management: turn it on under Settings, Products, Inventory, then set quantity, SKU and thresholds on each product or variation.', 'Most stock problems come from setup: manage stock left unticked on variations, orders stuck in pending payment, or backorders allowed by accident.', 'If you also sell in a shop, on marketplaces or keep stock in Tally, pick one system as the master, use identical SKUs everywhere and keep a small buffer for sync delays.'],
  'woocommerce-coupons-discounts': ['WooCommerce has three coupon discount types, percentage, fixed cart and fixed product, and free shipping is a separate tick box that any of them can add.', 'Pick the type by the offer: percentage for brand or category sales, fixed cart with a minimum spend to raise order value, fixed product for one product line.', 'Set an expiry, usage limits and individual use, check the discount against your margin and test the code on a real cart before you share it.'],
  'google-merchant-center-woocommerce': ['Connect WooCommerce to Google Merchant Center with the free Google for WooCommerce extension or a feed plugin, then set shipping and returns to match your site.', 'Most disapprovals come from prices, GST or stock that do not match the product page, missing shipping details, poor images or missing contact and policy pages.', 'Free listings cost nothing, and the same clean product data later powers Shopping and Performance Max ads once conversion tracking is in place.'],
  'own-website-vs-marketplaces': ['Marketplaces bring ready buyers and logistics, but you pay fees, compete on price and keep little customer data.', 'Your own store gives control over brand, margins and repeat customers, but you must bring the traffic yourself.', 'Most growing Indian brands do both: marketplaces for reach, their own store for loyalty, bundles and better margins.'],
  'website-down-what-to-do': ['First confirm the site is down for everyone, not just your network, by testing on mobile data and with an online down checker.', 'The error message tells you which layer failed: DNS errors point to the domain, timeouts and suspension pages to hosting, certificate warnings to SSL, and PHP or database errors to the site itself.', 'Check in this order: domain and DNS, hosting status and resource limits, SSL, then recent changes to the site; restore a backup only once you know the domain and hosting are fine.'],
  'domain-expired-what-to-do': ['An expired domain is rarely lost straight away: most registrars give a grace period to renew at the normal price, then a redemption period where the owner can restore it for an extra fee.', 'When a domain expires, the website and the email on that domain stop working, and SSL, payment callbacks and password-reset emails can fail with them.', 'Turn on auto-renew, keep the registrar account in your business name with a recovery email on a different domain, and check that renewals actually went through.'],
  'transfer-domain-to-another-registrar': ['A transfer changes the company you pay to register the domain; your website and email stay online as long as the DNS records keep working throughout.', 'Copy every DNS record and prepare it first, then unlock the domain, get the EPP code and start the transfer at the new registrar.', 'Check eligibility before you start: registrars can refuse a transfer soon after registration, a previous transfer or an owner change, and close to expiry.'],
  'wordpress-admin-slow': ['The dashboard is never page-cached, so every admin screen runs plugins and database queries live, which is why it can crawl while the public site feels fast.', 'Measure before changing anything: Query Monitor shows slow queries, slow requests to outside servers and which plugin causes them.', 'The usual fixes are removing or replacing heavy plugins, slowing Heartbeat, cleaning autoloaded data, adding object caching and moving to hosting with enough resources.'],
  'deceptive-site-ahead-warning-fix': ['To remove the deceptive site warning, find the flagged URLs in Search Console\'s Security issues report, delete the phishing pages and how the attacker got in, then request a review.', 'The warning will not fade while the bad pages remain, and a review submitted before the cleanup is finished usually fails.', 'After Google clears the site, check Edge and antivirus blocklists separately, change every password and keep updates, backups and monitoring running.'],
  'secure-wordpress-login': ['Unique passwords and two-factor authentication stop most login attacks; passkeys go further where supported.', 'Limit login attempts, add a bot check and protect XML-RPC and application passwords, the other doors into a site.', 'Give every person their own account with the lowest role they need, and remove access when the work ends.'],
  'compare-website-quotes': ['Website quotes differ because each developer prices a different job: design, number of layouts, content, features, testing and support.', 'To compare website quotes, send the same brief to everyone, put each quote into one sheet line by line and ask the same follow-up questions.', 'Compare the three-year cost, including hosting, renewals, licences and maintenance, rather than the build price alone.'],
  'fixed-price-vs-hourly-website-projects': ['Fixed price suits most website projects with a clear scope, such as a new site, a redesign or a landing page, because you know the total before work starts.', 'Hourly billing suits work nobody can scope in advance, such as diagnosing a slow or broken site, but always agree an estimate and a cap.', 'A common, fair setup is a fixed quote for the build and a monthly plan or fixed-price batches for changes after launch, all written into the agreement.'],
  'give-website-feedback-developer': ['Send one consolidated list from one decision-maker, where each point names the page, the device and browser, what you see and what you expected, with a screenshot.', 'Explain the problem rather than only the fix, mark each point as must-fix, should-fix or later, and keep bugs separate from new requests.', 'Review on your phone as well as a computer, and send everything for a round together by the agreed date so each revision round counts.'],
  'website-bug-vs-change-request': ['A bug is when the site does not do what was agreed; a change request is when you now want it to do something different, and the signed scope settles which is which.', 'A good bug report gives the page link, steps, expected and actual result, device and browser, and a screenshot or screen recording.', 'Agree warranty length, urgency levels and response times in writing before launch so grey-area issues are settled by the contract rather than by argument.'],
  'outsource-wordpress-development-india': ['To outsource WordPress development to India safely, choose a partner by live work and communication, start with one paid test task and agree scope, ownership and an NDA in writing.', 'Agencies can resell WordPress development under their own brand when the partner works white-label, stays invisible to the end client and follows the agency\'s QA list.', 'Compare partners on process, response times and handover quality, not just the hourly or fixed price.'],
  'web-designer-vs-web-developer': ['A web designer decides how a site looks and how visitors move through it; a web developer makes it work; a WordPress developer is a developer who specialises in WordPress.', 'For a small business site, one person who designs and builds well is often enough; for fixes, speed, security, WooCommerce or custom features you need a developer.', 'Judge either by their live work: designers by how polished and on-brand it looks, developers by how fast, mobile-friendly and reliable the sites are.'],
  'what-does-wordpress-developer-do': ['A WordPress developer builds, customises and fixes WordPress sites; some specialise in themes or plugins, others in complete business websites and stores.', 'A web designer decides how a site looks and works for visitors; a WordPress developer turns that into a working, fast, secure site, and many people do both.', 'Hire one when your site drives enquiries or sales, needs features you can\'t add yourself, or is slow, broken or hacked, and judge them on similar live work and a clear scope.'],
  'elementor-pro-worth-it': ['Elementor Pro is worth it when your site needs custom headers, footers and post templates, WooCommerce layouts, dynamic listings, or forms and popups in one tool.', 'For a simple brochure site, free Elementor on a theme with a header and footer builder, plus a form plugin, is often enough.', 'Pro is a yearly licence: buy it in your business name, budget for renewals, and remember that removing it later means rebuilding anything made with Pro features.'],
  'best-theme-for-elementor': ['With Elementor, the theme mainly decides who builds the header, footer and templates, and how much code loads.', 'Hello Elementor suits sites built fully in Elementor Pro; Astra, GeneratePress, Kadence and Blocksy suit mixed setups.', 'Avoid heavy multipurpose and nulled themes, use a child theme for code changes and test any switch on staging.'],
  'figma-to-wordpress-approaches': ['The best Figma to WordPress approach depends on who edits the site afterwards, how strict the design is, and how much speed and long-term maintenance matter.', 'Elementor suits marketing teams who want visual editing; block themes and custom themes suit stricter designs, better speed and fewer plugin dependencies.', 'Whichever approach you choose, insist on design tokens, reusable components, responsive checks against the Figma frames and a handover guide.'],
  'website-for-saas-startups': ['Keep the marketing site and the product apart, usually on the main domain and an app subdomain, so marketing can change pages without touching the product.', 'Pricing, feature and use-case pages do most of the selling, and the sign-up button should hand visitors to the app with their plan and campaign source intact.', 'Docs, a changelog and honest comparison pages answer questions that come after the first visit, and they often bring in better search traffic than the homepage.'],
  'restaurant-menu-on-website': ['Build the menu as a real web page with dish names, prices and markers as text, and keep a PDF only as a printable extra.', 'Give every dish the same details in the same order, with dietary and allergen labels supplied by your kitchen, and photos only for the dishes that sell the restaurant.', 'Point QR codes, your Google Business Profile and every outlet to stable menu addresses, and give one person the job of keeping prices current everywhere.'],
  'website-for-cloud-kitchens': ['Keep delivery apps for discovery, and use your own website and WhatsApp to turn repeat customers into direct orders you keep more of, with a clear reason to order direct.', 'Choose the ordering flow that matches your team: WhatsApp orders for small menus, WooCommerce ordering with payment, delivery areas and kitchen hours for higher volumes.', 'Put every brand menu on real web pages with prices, veg markers and allergens, show your FSSAI details, and check Google rules before creating profiles for virtual brands.'],
  'real-estate-project-microsite': ['A project microsite should answer a buyer\'s first questions fast: configurations with carpet area, floor plans, location, price or price on request, RERA details and how to book a visit.', 'Most projects work best as a page on the developer\'s main site plus focused ad landing pages; a separate microsite earns its place for a large launch with its own budget.', 'Capture leads with short forms, a gated brochure, site-visit booking, tap-to-call and WhatsApp, and track every one by campaign so you know which ads bring visits.'],
  'hotel-booking-engine-channel-manager': ['A booking engine takes direct bookings on your website; a channel manager keeps rates and availability in sync across OTAs and the booking engine.', 'The PMS runs the property itself, and in a good setup the PMS, channel manager and booking engine share one live inventory through real two-way sync.', 'Use OTAs for discovery and your booking engine for repeat and returning guests, with fair rate parity and a clear reason to book direct.'],
  'ngo-website-donations-80g': ['Donors give when the website answers their doubts: who you are legally, what their money will do, whether a tax benefit applies and what happened after they gave.', 'Mention 80G, 12A, FCRA or CSR registration only if the organisation genuinely holds it, collect PAN only from donors who want 80G, and confirm receipts and foreign donation rules with your CA.', 'Transparency pages, simple volunteer forms and impact stories shared with written consent build the trust that brings repeat and monthly giving.'],
  'solar-company-lead-generation': ['Match each lead source to the right page: search ads and Google Maps bring buyers who are ready to compare, while social ads and articles bring people who need more convincing.', 'Qualify with a few questions that change your proposal, such as property type, PIN code, roof type and bill range, and judge campaigns by surveys booked rather than form fills.', 'Speed of follow-up decides many solar sales, so reply instantly, call as soon as you can, use WhatsApp for documents and track every lead through to survey and order.'],
  'website-for-advocates-bar-council-rules': ['The Bar Council of India rules forbid advocates from soliciting work or advertising, but permit a website that gives factual information; check the current rules before launch.', 'A compliant site sticks to facts: name, contact details, enrolment, qualifications and areas of practice, with an entry disclaimer and no testimonials, rankings or success claims.', 'Describe practice areas in neutral, informative language, and treat Google reviews, paid ads and SEO phrases like "best lawyer" with the same caution as the website itself.'],
  'website-for-homestays-bnbs': ['Keep your OTA listings; the website is there to convert guests who find you on a platform and then search your name, plus repeat guests and referrals.', 'Answer the questions guests otherwise message about: each room in detail, house rules, the exact route in, meals and what is nearby.', 'Choose the simplest booking method you can run reliably, from WhatsApp enquiries to a booking plugin with calendar sync or a full channel manager.'],
  'online-appointment-booking-website': ['Pick the booking method by how you work: a request form or WhatsApp for a single practice, an embedded scheduler for consultants, a WordPress booking plugin for staff and services, or your clinic or salon software\'s own widget.', 'Set services, durations, buffers, holidays and booking windows carefully, and keep phone and walk-in bookings in the same calendar to avoid double bookings.', 'Reminders with an easy reschedule link, plus deposits for first visits or long appointments, do more to cut no-shows than anything else.'],
  'remove-malware-wordpress-step-by-step': ['Back up the infected site, reset every password and key, then replace core, plugins and themes with clean copies.', 'Check uploads, mu-plugins, .htaccess, wp-config.php, the database and cron jobs, and close the entry point.', 'Only request a Google review once the site is fully clean, then remove spam URLs and keep monitoring.'],
  'fix-wordpress-redirect-hack': ['A WordPress hacked redirect is almost always malware that targets mobile visitors from Google while hiding from logged-in admins, so test the way a real visitor arrives.', 'Redirect malware hides in .htaccess, wp-config.php, index.php, theme and JavaScript files, the database, fake plugins, mu-plugins and cron jobs, so check every location rather than trusting one scan.', 'Removing the visible code is not enough: replace core, plugins and themes, remove backdoors, change every password, purge every cache and close the entry point, or the redirect comes back.'],
  'fix-japanese-keyword-seo-spam-hack': ['The Japanese keyword hack creates cloaked spam pages that only Google sees, so confirm it with site: searches and the URL Inspection live test rather than by visiting your site.', 'Remove any Search Console owner or sitemap the hacker added, including their verification token, then delete the spam generator and every backdoor at the same time.', 'Let spam URLs return 410 or 404, never redirect or block them, use the Removals tool for brand searches, and expect a large hack to take weeks or months to clear.'],
  'find-remove-wordpress-backdoors': ['A backdoor is hidden code or access that lets an attacker back in after a cleanup, and a missed one is the usual reason malware returns days later.', 'Check core and plugin files against official checksums, then look where backdoors hide: PHP in uploads, mu-plugins, fake plugins, wp-config includes, .htaccess and .user.ini, cron jobs, rogue admins and the database.', 'Remove every backdoor in one session, replace files rather than editing them, then change all passwords and salts, or the attacker simply uses the one you missed.'],
  'wordpress-site-sending-spam-emails': ['Spam from a hosting account usually comes from a malicious mailer script, a stolen mailbox or SMTP password, or an abused form, and the mail logs and message headers tell you which.', 'Ask your host to pause outgoing mail, keep a sample of the spam, fix the cause and change every mailbox password before you request delisting from Spamhaus or any other blacklist.', 'Send website email through authenticated SMTP with SPF, DKIM and DMARC in place, so abuse is easier to spot and spoofed mail is easier for receivers to reject.'],
  'woocommerce-checkout-skimmer-malware': ['A checkout skimmer is injected JavaScript that copies what customers type or shows a fake card form, and it can affect stores that use a hosted payment page too.', 'Inspect the checkout as a logged-out customer with the Network tab open, list every script domain, and search the database and JavaScript files, because skimmers often live in settings rather than PHP files.', 'If you find one, pause card payments, keep evidence, tell your payment gateway and take legal advice on reporting and customer notices, then clean the store and limit checkout scripts with a Content Security Policy.'],
  'why-wordpress-sites-get-hacked': ['Most WordPress hacks come through outdated or abandoned plugins and stolen or reused passwords, not WordPress core.', 'Leftover staging sites, old admin accounts and shared hosting folders give attackers easy side doors.', 'A short monthly routine of updates, access reviews and off-site backups prevents most small business hacks.'],
  'nulled-themes-plugins-risks': ['Nulled WordPress themes and pirated plugins are premium products with licence checks removed, often with hidden malware added.', 'Even a clean nulled copy gets no official updates or support, so known security holes stay open.', 'Check every premium theme and plugin against an official licence, replace unlicensed copies and scan for leftover backdoors.'],
  'wordpress-malware-scanners-compared': ['Remote scanners such as Sucuri SiteCheck only see public pages, plugin scanners such as Wordfence check files and the database from inside WordPress, and server-side scanners see the whole hosting account.', 'No scanner catches everything: cloaked spam, database injections, new malware and premium plugin files are common blind spots, so trust symptoms and Google\'s reports over a clean result.', 'For most business sites, one plugin or host scanner on a schedule, an occasional remote check and Search Console alerts are enough, as long as someone acts on the results.'],
  'hosting-suspended-malware': ['A malware suspension means the host found malicious files somewhere in the account, so every site in it must be cleaned, not just one.', 'Close the entry point and change every password before asking for reinstatement, or the account is likely to be suspended again.', 'Keep an off-server backup, registrar and DNS logins and the host\'s abuse contact outside the hosting account so recovery can start the same day.'],
  'this-site-may-be-hacked-google': ['The label appears when Google finds hacked content, often spam pages or redirects, and is removed after cleanup and a successful review.', 'Lock attackers out, find every spam page and backdoor, clean completely, then request a review in Search Console.', 'While the label is up, tell regular customers how to reach you and keep paid ads and social links pointing to clean pages.'],
  'wordpress-file-permissions': ['For most WordPress sites, folders should be 755, files 644 and wp-config.php tighter at 600 or 440 where your host allows it; 777 is never the right answer.', 'Ownership matters as much as the numbers: if WordPress asks for FTP details or cannot create folders, the files usually belong to the wrong user, which chmod 777 does not fix.', 'You can check and reset permissions in cPanel File Manager, an SFTP client such as FileZilla or over SSH, and you should recheck them after a hack or a migration.'],
  'wordpress-firewall-waf-explained': ['A plugin firewall such as Wordfence runs on your server and understands WordPress, while a DNS-level firewall such as Cloudflare or Sucuri filters traffic before it reaches your hosting.', 'The rules that matter most on WordPress protect the login, XML-RPC, checkout and forms, and rate limiting on those few URLs stops most bot floods without affecting real visitors.', 'Every firewall blocks something legitimate sooner or later, so find the exact rule in the logs and add a narrow exception rather than switching the firewall off.'],
  'harden-wp-config-php': ['Add DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT, keep debugging off on the live site, force HTTPS for the admin area and refresh the salts after a hack or when someone with access leaves.', 'DISALLOW_FILE_MODS also blocks dashboard updates and automatic updates, so use it only when updates are handled by WP-CLI, deployments or a maintenance routine.', 'Changing the table prefix or moving wp-config.php up a folder adds little real protection; deleting leftover backup copies of the file and setting tight permissions matter more.'],
  'recover-rankings-after-hack': ['Clean the site fully and close the entry point before asking Google for a review, or the hack and warning return.', 'Spam URLs should return 404 or 410 so they drop out of the index; redirecting them to your homepage slows recovery.', 'Track recovery in Search Console by branded searches, indexed page counts and clicks on your real pages, not by a fixed date.'],
  'hostinger-wordpress-setup-guide': ['Choose web, WordPress or cloud hosting rather than the Website Builder, check the renewal price, and keep the account and domain in your business name.', 'After installing WordPress in hPanel, switch on SSL, set a current PHP version and configure LiteSpeed Cache before you build a single page.', 'Keep your own off-site backup alongside the host\'s, set up email records carefully, and use staging for changes once the site is live.'],
  'hostinger-website-slow-fix': ['A slow WordPress site on Hostinger is usually hitting its plan\'s CPU, memory or process limits, or not being served from the LiteSpeed cache, so check hPanel\'s resource usage and caching before blaming the host.', 'Use the LiteSpeed Cache plugin with the server cache, one CDN and a current PHP version, clean up the database and heavy plugins, and change optimisation settings one at a time, testing after each.', 'If the server responds quickly but pages are still slow, the cause is the page itself (images, page builder bloat, third-party scripts), and upgrading the plan won\'t fix it.'],
  'migrate-website-to-hostinger': ['For a Hostinger site migration without downtime, copy and test the site on Hostinger before you change DNS, then switch the domain and keep the old host for a week or two.', 'Use hPanel\'s migration tool for a simple site, a migration plugin for a medium one, and a manual files-and-database move for large or complex sites.', 'Recreate email DNS records before switching, then check SSL, forms, redirects and speed once the domain points to Hostinger.'],
  'hosting-renewal-price-increase': ['Hosting renewal shock happens because the first term was an introductory discount; the renewal is the regular price, often plus add-ons and 18% GST.', 'Before paying, check what is on the invoice, remove add-ons you do not use, ask for a retention offer and compare the total cost over three to five years.', 'If you move, start well before the renewal date so the site, email and DNS can be moved and tested without downtime.'],
  'monthly-website-maintenance-plan': ['Website maintenance packages are worth paying for only when updates are tested, backups are off-site and restorable, and someone checks forms and checkout afterwards.', 'Compare packages and monthly fees by the tasks written into the scope, including content-change minutes and emergency response times, not by the package name.', 'The monthly report should show what changed and what was checked, so you can tell whether the plan is doing its job.'],
  'domain-hosting-renewal-checklist': ['A WordPress site\'s domain renewal price is set by the registrar, not WordPress; a .com or .in typically renews for about ₹500 to ₹1,200 a year.', 'Check the renewal price before buying, since first-year discounts and "free domain" offers end, and keep the domain in your own business account.', 'List every renewal with its date, switch on auto-renew with a card that won\'t expire, and send renewal emails to an address someone reads.'],
  'hostinger-business-email-setup': ['Create the mailbox in hPanel, then make sure MX, SPF, DKIM and DMARC records point at Hostinger, copying the exact values hPanel shows for your domain.', 'Use IMAP, not POP, on phones and Outlook so every device sees the same mailbox, and keep one SPF record that lists every service sending mail for your domain.', 'Send WordPress form emails through your Hostinger mailbox with an SMTP plugin, using the same address as the sender, then test that enquiries reach the inbox, not spam.'],
  'fix-wordpress-critical-error': ['A WordPress fatal error is what triggers "There has been a critical error on this website", usually from a plugin, theme, PHP version change or memory limit; your content is almost always safe.', 'Start with the recovery mode email WordPress sends to the admin address; if it never arrives, turn on WP_DEBUG_LOG and read the fatal error in wp-content/debug.log.', 'No log access? Rename the plugins folder in File Manager or SFTP to find the culprit, switch to a default theme, then check PHP version and memory before restoring a backup.'],
  'wordpress-changes-not-showing': ['Most changes that do not show are held back by a cache, so check the page as a visitor would, then clear the plugin, server, CDN and browser caches in that order.', 'If clearing caches does not help, check you edited the right thing: a template, a synced pattern, a translation, a child theme or a staging copy instead of the live site.', 'Changes that show only on desktop, only for you or only for a while each point to a different layer, which tells you where to look first.'],
  'elementor-not-loading-fix': ['When Elementor is stuck on loading, the cause is usually around Elementor rather than in it: low PHP memory, a plugin conflict, a server firewall rule, or caching and CDN scripts.', 'Diagnose before changing things: check the browser console and Network tab, Elementor\'s System Info, and whether the editor opens in Safe Mode.', 'If only one page won\'t load, suspect that page\'s data and restore a revision or backup; if every page fails, work through memory, plugins, server rules, caching and PHP version in order.'],
  'elementor-layout-broken-after-update': ['Most layouts that break after an Elementor update are fixed by regenerating Elementor\'s CSS and data, clearing every cache and making sure Elementor and Elementor Pro are on matching versions.', 'If that does not fix it, look for an add-on, theme or custom CSS that has not caught up, and check whether containers, features or global settings changed with the update.', 'Rolling back is a temporary fix; test major Elementor updates on staging with a backup first so the live site never breaks.'],
  'edit-website-content-wordpress': ['Find out whether each page uses the block editor or Elementor first, because that decides where and how you edit it.', 'Edit content from your own Editor account, preview before you save, and use revisions or Elementor History to undo mistakes.', 'Leave global styles, permalinks, theme files, plugin updates and new layouts alone, or try them on a staging copy first.'],
  'premium-wordpress-themes-guide': ['A premium theme licence buys you updates and support for a period, not just the files, so buy it from the developer or an established marketplace in your business name.', 'Check what the demo installs, which bundled plugins it depends on and how much of your content would be locked into the theme before you pay.', 'If you would spend weeks fighting a theme to match your design, a lightweight theme with Elementor or a custom build is usually the better investment.'],
  'update-premium-theme-safely': ['Premium themes only update automatically once the licence or purchase code is activated; otherwise download the latest ZIP from the vendor and upload it with "Replace current with uploaded".', 'Before updating, take a full backup and check whether anyone edited the theme files directly, because those edits are overwritten; move them into a child theme first.', 'Update the theme, then its bundled plugins, then test key pages, forms and checkout, ideally on staging first when the version jump is large.'],
  'custom-wordpress-theme-development': ['A custom theme is written for one website and contains only what that site needs, so it suits unusual designs, strict speed targets and locked-down editing.', 'Most business sites are better served by a lightweight theme with Elementor or the block editor, because they are quicker to build and easier to change yourself.', 'If you do go custom, keep content structures in a plugin, insist on documentation and version control, and make sure you own the code.'],
  'custom-wordpress-plugin-development': ['Build a custom plugin for what is unique to your business, such as pricing rules, calculators or integrations, and use established plugins for common jobs like SEO, forms and backups.', 'A well-built plugin sanitises input, escapes output, checks permissions, loads code only where needed and is tested on staging before it touches the live site.', 'Cost depends on features, integrations, edge cases and testing, so get a written scope, and make sure you own the code and receive it in version control.'],
  'wordpress-child-theme-explained': ['A child theme inherits everything from its parent and holds only your changes, so parent theme updates no longer wipe your custom code.', 'You need one only for template, PHP or substantial CSS changes; small CSS tweaks and snippets can live in Additional CSS or a code snippets plugin.', 'Create it with a style.css header and a functions.php that loads the parent styles, then test the switch on staging because menus and widgets may need reassigning.'],
  'block-themes-full-site-editing': ['A block theme builds the whole site, including headers, footers and templates, from blocks, with design settings in theme.json and editing in the Site Editor.', 'Changes made in the Site Editor are saved in the database, not the theme files, so agencies need a clear rule for where template changes live.', 'Choose a block theme for lean, structured sites with a stable design system; choose Elementor when non-technical clients must rearrange layouts themselves.'],
  'custom-gutenberg-blocks': ['Most custom Gutenberg block requests can be met with a pattern or synced pattern; build a real block only when content needs structure, logic or a locked layout.', 'ACF blocks suit PHP teams and server-rendered sections, while native blocks built with block.json and React give the best editing experience for interactive pieces.', 'Plan the block library like a design system: name blocks clearly, restrict what editors can change with theme.json and locking, and document every block at handover.'],
  'custom-post-types-fields': ['A custom post type is a separate kind of content such as Doctors, Projects or Courses, and custom fields store its fixed details like fees, location or duration.', 'Plan the content model on paper first: list each type, its fields, its taxonomies and how pages should display and filter it.', 'Register post types in a plugin, not the theme, so the content survives a redesign, and give archives and single pages proper titles, descriptions and schema.'],
  'plugin-vs-custom-code-wordpress': ['Use a maintained plugin for common needs, a snippet for a few lines, and a custom plugin for features specific to your business.', 'Functionality belongs in a plugin and design in the theme, so features survive a redesign.', 'Decide by asking who maintains it, what it loads, what it costs over years and what happens if you remove it.'],
  'website-for-farm-stays-agritourism': ['Farm stay guests book an experience, not just a room, so the website should show daily activities, food, seasons and what families and groups actually do.', 'Separate day visits, overnight stays and group or school packages, each with clear inclusions, timings and a simple booking or enquiry route.', 'Directions, road conditions, mobile network notes and honest photos prevent disappointed guests and reduce repetitive phone calls.'],
  'website-for-landscaping-gardening-services': ['Landscaping sells visually, so a project gallery organised by type, with before and after photos, does most of the persuading.', 'Separate pages for design and execution, maintenance contracts, terrace and balcony gardens and nursery sales let each kind of client find their next step.', 'A site-visit form that asks for location, space type, size, photos and budget range qualifies enquiries before anyone travels across the city.'],
  'website-for-water-purifier-ro-service': ['Decide whether the website leads with service or with sales, because the homepage, main button and most important pages change depending on which brings your income.', 'Spell out AMC plans visit by visit and part by part, and let customers book or renew without a phone call.', 'Say exactly what your relationship with each purifier brand is, and describe what a technology does rather than promising health outcomes.'],
  'website-for-modular-kitchen-companies': ['Organise designs by layout and style, label real installations versus 3D renders, and add materials, city and finish notes to every project.', 'Make the design consultation the main call to action, with a short form, a WhatsApp option for floor plans and an honest explanation of what drives price.', 'Win local searches with separate pages for kitchens and wardrobes, a complete Google Business Profile and city pages built on genuine projects.'],
  'website-for-glass-aluminium-fabricators': ['Give each product type, such as sliding windows, partitions, shower enclosures and facades, its own page with real installations and plain explanations.', 'Help buyers choose between aluminium and uPVC and between glass types, then make a site measurement visit the main call to action.', 'Show completed projects by type and area, offer a separate path for builders and architects, and keep warranty and service details clear.'],
  'website-for-steel-fabrication-companies': ['Fabricators sell capability, so give each process its own page with honest limits on materials, thickness, size and weight, and say what is outsourced.', 'List only certifications you currently hold, with scope and expiry, and describe your inspection process and the documents you supply.', 'Build an RFQ form that accepts drawings securely and asks for material, quantity, finish, scope and delivery, then acknowledge every submission quickly.'],
  'website-for-painting-contractors': ['Homeowners and facility managers want proof, so before-and-after galleries and clear service pages matter more than a fancy home page.', 'Make the site visit or quote request easy: a short form, a WhatsApp button and a phone link on every mobile page.', 'Local SEO wins painting jobs: a page for each main service, the areas you cover, and a complete Google Business Profile with real photos.'],
  'website-for-waterproofing-companies': ['Organise the site around the problems owners search for, such as terrace leakage, bathroom seepage and wall dampness, not around chemical brand names.', 'Make booking a site inspection the main action, with WhatsApp, a short form and a clear note on what the visit involves and whether it is charged.', 'Real before-and-after photos, warranty terms explained in plain words and a Google Business Profile do more for trust than generic claims.'],
  'website-for-ac-repair-services': ['A website for AC repair services must catch urgent summer searches and still sell installations and AMC plans through the year.', 'Say which brands you service without implying authorisation, and explain what decides repair and gas refill charges.', 'Booking slots that match real capacity, a small set of genuine area pages and steady reviews do more than dozens of thin pages.'],
  'website-for-cctv-security-installers': ['Homes, shops and offices search for CCTV installation in their area, so clear service pages, local landmarks and a quick site survey form matter more than technical jargon.', 'Separate pages for homes, shops, offices, societies and warehouses, plus an AMC page, help each buyer find their situation and help you rank for more searches.', 'Show real installations with owner permission, list the brands you genuinely work with, and never publish clients\' camera views or layouts that could compromise their security.'],
  'website-for-it-hardware-computer-dealers': ['Retail and business buyers want different things, so give each a clear path from the homepage: stock and store visits for one, quotes and service for the other.', 'Hardware prices move too often for a fixed price list, so many dealers show specifications with price on request and sell only stable items online.', 'State brand authorisation exactly as your partner agreement allows, and explain warranty, AMC and GST invoicing plainly, because these decide business orders.'],
  'website-for-signage-companies': ['A signage company website should lead with a project portfolio organised by sign type, because buyers want to see similar work before they ask for a quote.', 'Detailed quote forms with size, location, quantity, photo upload and deadline fields bring better enquiries than a bare contact form.', 'Separate pages for each sign type and for multi-location B2B rollouts help you rank for specific searches and reach brand and retail buyers.'],
  'website-for-housing-societies-rwas': ['A society website gives residents one official place for notices, rules, documents and requests, so important information stops getting lost in WhatsApp groups.', 'Decide early what is public and what sits behind a member login, and keep personal details such as flat owners\' phone numbers out of public pages.', 'Register the domain, hosting and every account in the society\'s name with two committee members as admins, so the site survives each change of committee.'],
  'website-for-pharma-franchise-companies': ['A PCD pharma franchise website is a B2B tool: its job is to show the product range clearly and turn interested distributors into qualified enquiries.', 'A searchable product catalogue with composition, pack and division, plus an enquiry form asking state, district and experience, does most of the work.', 'Keep medical claims out, show licence details only as they genuinely stand, and check current drug advertising rules with your regulatory adviser.'],
  'website-for-yoga-studios-teachers': ['Give each yoga style its own page with a clear level, what a class feels like and who should check with a doctor first, plus a "Your first class" page for beginners.', 'Treat online batches, workshops, retreats and teacher training as separate offers with their own pages, dates, inclusions and refund rules, and send class links only after booking.', 'Describe certifications exactly as held, never claim yoga cures a condition, and keep the timetable as editable text rather than an image or PDF.'],
  'woocommerce-maintenance-checklist': ['Split store maintenance by frequency: a few minutes daily on orders and payments, a weekly test order, monthly updates on staging, and quarterly restores, audits and cleanup.', 'Reconcile gateway settlements against orders regularly, because a payment that succeeded at the gateway but left the order pending is easy to miss.', 'Back up stores more often than brochure sites and never restore or push a full database over a live store without first saving the orders placed since.'],
  'woocommerce-speed-optimization': ['WooCommerce performance optimization is mostly hosting, caching, images, plugins and database, in that order, measured on product, category, cart and checkout pages.', 'Cache product and category pages but never cart, checkout or account pages; use object caching and a CDN to speed up what cannot be page cached.', 'Match the fix to the symptom: slow server response points to hosting and database, while slow loading after the first byte points to images, scripts, fonts and theme.'],
  'woocommerce-security-checklist': ['Start with general WordPress hardening, then add the store layer: a checkout where card details are entered with the gateway, not on your server, and two-factor login for every admin and shop manager.', 'Card-testing bots, skimmers and fake orders target the checkout, so add bot protection there, verify every payment in the gateway dashboard and check the checkout page for scripts you did not add.', 'Every plugin that touches checkout, orders or payments runs on your most sensitive page, so keep that list short, buy only from official sources and update with a test order afterwards.'],
  'website-for-car-wash-detailing': ['Describe each package honestly, including what ceramic coating and PPF do not do, and state warranties exactly as the product brand provides them.', 'Make booking match how you work: WhatsApp or a form for a single bay, live slots with service durations once volume grows, and separate details for doorstep jobs.', 'Sell with your own before-and-after work, shown with the owner\'s consent and number plates blurred, plus a separate page for each main service for local search.'],
  'website-for-pet-shops-grooming': ['Build the site around what you actually offer, with separate paths for buying supplies and booking grooming or boarding.', 'WooCommerce suits shops that need bookings and the store in one place; Shopify suits supply-led shops wanting a simpler store.', 'Show real photos, clear pricing rules and safety practices, and keep your Google Business Profile in step with the site.'],
  'website-for-toy-stores': ['Toys are mostly bought by parents and gift-givers, so organise the store by age, type, interest and budget rather than by product name.', 'Clear age guidance, materials, safety marks the products genuinely carry, box contents and battery details reduce doubts, returns and support calls.', 'Gift wrapping, messages and honest delivery dates win birthday and festival orders; WooCommerce and Shopify both handle this, along with Indian payments and COD.'],
  'website-for-beauty-cosmetics-brands': ['A beauty store sells through detail: full ingredient lists, skin-type guidance, shade help, batch and expiry information and clear usage steps.', 'Shopify suits most D2C beauty launches; WooCommerce suits brands that want deep content, custom logic or full ownership of the stack.', 'Keep product claims within what your labels and paperwork support, and check current cosmetics labelling and advertising rules before launch.'],
  'website-for-makeup-artists': ['A makeup artist website turns Instagram interest into confirmed bookings by showing a curated portfolio, clear packages and an easy date-check enquiry.', 'Organise the portfolio by look and occasion, use your own unedited-looking photos, and ask for date, city, occasion and number of people in the form.', 'Local SEO, a complete Google Business Profile and genuine client reviews matter more than a long blog for most artists.'],
  'website-for-tattoo-studios': ['People choose a tattoo studio on the artist\'s portfolio and on hygiene, so show real work by artist and style and explain your safety routine clearly.', 'A consultation booking form that collects the idea, placement, size and reference images saves time and filters serious clients from casual enquiries.', 'Local SEO, genuine Google reviews and a fast, image-optimised portfolio usually bring more bookings than a large site with many thin pages.'],
  'website-for-video-production-companies': ['A video production website should lead with a short, fast-loading showreel and make it obvious what kinds of videos you make and for whom.', 'Case studies that explain the brief, your approach and the deliverables sell better than a wall of embedded videos with no context.', 'A project brief form that asks about video type, timeline and budget range brings better enquiries than a plain contact form.'],
  'website-for-djs-live-bands': ['Clients book performers on what they can see and hear, so short live clips organised by event type matter more than posters or studio tracks.', 'Clear packages with what is included, plus a short enquiry form asking date, city, event and guest count, filter serious clients and cut back-and-forth.', 'Keep the site fast on mobile, link it from Instagram and Google Business Profile, and send enquiries straight to WhatsApp so peak-season leads are answered quickly.'],
  'website-for-artists-art-galleries': ['Organise the portfolio by series or medium, with titles, sizes, materials and availability on every artwork.', 'WooCommerce suits artists who want portfolio, blog and shop in one flexible site; Shopify suits print-led stores wanting less upkeep.', 'Handle commissions, shipping of fragile work and international buyers with clear written terms before taking payment.'],
  'website-for-book-publishers': ['A publisher website needs a searchable catalogue, clear author pages and a submissions process that sets expectations, before it needs anything fancy.', 'Selling direct works on WooCommerce or Shopify with UPI, cards and COD, but shipping weights, ISBN fields and pre-orders need planning up front.', 'Each book page is a small SEO landing page: unique description, author bio, sample pages and structured data help readers find it on Google.'],
  'website-for-corporate-gifting-companies': ['Corporate buyers want to see products, branding options, minimum quantities and lead times quickly, then send a bulk quote request without friction.', 'A catalogue with a quote request flow usually works better than a plain shop cart, though WooCommerce or Shopify can combine both for smaller orders.', 'Plan festive campaigns such as Diwali well in advance, with hampers, deadlines and a clear process for logo approval and delivery to many addresses.'],
  'website-for-home-decor-stores': ['Decor shoppers browse by room and by style as often as by product type, so give each of those routes real pages, not just filters.', 'Plan colours, sizes and sets as variants before uploading, and pair a styled lifestyle photo with plain product shots on every page.', 'Fragile packing, a returns policy written for colour and size mismatches, and simple gifting options remove most of the doubts that stop a decor order.'],
  'website-for-corporate-training-companies': ['HR and L&D buyers shortlist training partners on programmes, trainer credibility, delivery format and proof, so give each programme its own clear page.', 'An enquiry form that asks about team size, location, format and timeline helps you qualify leads and reply with a relevant proposal.', 'Downloadable brochures, honest case studies and LinkedIn-friendly content support a long B2B buying cycle better than a single services page.'],
  'website-for-software-training-institutes': ['Give every course its own detailed page with syllabus, tools, duration, batch timings, mode and a demo class or counselling button.', 'Keep batch dates current and make demo bookings and WhatsApp enquiries easy, because students compare several institutes in the same week.', 'Be careful and honest with placement and certification claims; show real projects, trainer profiles and student work with permission instead.'],
  'website-for-ielts-spoken-english-institutes': ['Organise the site by what the learner wants to achieve, such as study abroad, a job interview or workplace confidence, rather than by your internal course codes.', 'A free demo class or level check with a short booking form is the most useful enquiry route an English institute can offer.', 'Refer to exam names factually, avoid official logos and words like authorised unless you hold an agreement, and never promise a band score.'],
  'featured-snippets-how-to-win': ['Featured snippets usually go to pages that already rank on page one and answer the question directly in the first lines under a matching heading.', 'Format answers the way Google shows them: a 40 to 60 word paragraph, a numbered list for steps, or a table for comparisons.', 'Target long-tail questions your Indian customers actually ask, such as costs, timelines and how-tos with a city or local angle.'],
  'long-tail-keywords-explained': ['Long-tail keywords are longer, more specific searches, such as a service plus a city or a problem, and they usually come from people closer to buying.', 'You can find them free from Google autocomplete, People also ask, Search Console queries and the questions customers already ask you on WhatsApp and calls.', 'Give each important long-tail topic one focused page or section that answers it fully, rather than stuffing many phrases into a single page.'],
  'seo-friendly-urls': ['Use the Post name permalink setting and keep slugs short, lowercase and descriptive, ideally with the main phrase people search for.', 'Changing a URL that already ranks is risky: only do it for a clear reason, and always add a 301 redirect from the old address.', 'For WooCommerce and Shopify, keep product and category URLs stable, because they collect links, shares and ad history over time.'],
  'image-seo-guide': ['Original photos with descriptive file names, natural alt text and relevant nearby text are far more likely to appear in Google Images and Lens than stock images.', 'Use real image elements in modern formats, compressed and sized responsively with width and height set, and never lazy-load the main image at the top of the page.', 'Add images to your sitemap through an SEO plugin, set a strong featured image for Discover and link previews, and track image clicks in Search Console.'],
  'voice-search-local-seo': ['Voice assistants mostly answer local questions from your Google Business Profile, so complete and consistent listings matter more than any website trick.', 'Answer the real questions customers ask, in the words they use, including Hinglish and regional phrasing, in short FAQ-style paragraphs.', 'A fast mobile site, consistent name, address and phone details and basic schema help voice results, but voice traffic cannot be measured separately.'],
  'google-business-profile-posts': ['Google Business Profile posts, offers and photos show real activity and give searchers a reason to call, visit or click through to your website.', 'A simple weekly routine of one useful post and a few fresh photos is more effective than occasional bursts of activity.', 'Link each post to the matching page on your website, with clear tracking, so you can see which posts actually bring enquiries.'],
  'eeat-explained-small-business': ['E-E-A-T stands for Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness and Trustworthiness; it is a way Google describes quality, not a single score you can switch on.', 'Small businesses show it through real details: who you are, real work and photos, clear contact information, honest claims, reviews and accurate content.', 'Trust matters most, especially for health, money and legal topics, so start with an about page, contact details, policies and proof of real work.'],
  'website-trust-signals-checklist': ['Visitors decide quickly whether a business is real, so clear contact details, a physical address, real photos and named people do much of the work.', 'For online stores, visible policies, GST details where applicable, secure checkout, COD clarity and genuine reviews reduce hesitation before payment.', 'Trust is also technical: HTTPS without warnings, fast pages, working forms and no broken links tell visitors the business is looked after.'],
  'backlink-audit-disavow': ['A toxic backlink audit starts with Search Console links data and a manual action check, not a third-party toxicity score.', 'Disavow only links you or a past agency built through paid or scheme tactics, or links named in a manual action.', 'A genuine backlink cleanup service explains every link it disavows; avoid offers that promise ranking jumps from removal alone.'],
  'competitor-seo-analysis': ['Your search competitors are the businesses that keep appearing for your customers\' searches from your area, which may not be your offline rivals.', 'Compare Google Business Profiles first, then the pages that actually rank, looking at page type, depth, proof and mobile speed rather than design.', 'Turn findings into a short plan grouped by effort, never copy text or tactics that break Google\'s guidelines, and repeat the check every few months.'],
  'service-area-business-seo': ['A service-area business can rank locally without a shopfront by hiding its address in Google Business Profile and listing the areas it actually serves.', 'City or area pages help only when each has genuinely local content, such as jobs done, response times and local conditions; copy-paste pages do not.', 'Reviews that mention the service and area, consistent contact details and fast mobile pages matter more than chasing every suburb.'],
  'multi-location-business-website': ['For most multi-branch businesses one website with a separate, genuinely useful page per branch is easier to manage and rank than many separate sites.', 'Each branch page needs its own address, phone, hours, map, photos, team and local details, and must match that branch\'s Google Business Profile exactly.', 'Build branch pages from a structured WordPress template with custom fields, so details, schema and listings stay consistent when a branch moves or closes.'],
  'search-console-page-indexing-errors': ['Not every "Why pages aren\'t indexed" reason is a problem: first decide whether each URL should be in Google at all.', 'Crawled or discovered but not indexed are usually quality, duplication or internal linking issues, not technical errors you can switch off.', 'Fix real errors (5xx, 404s in the sitemap, accidental noindex or robots blocks) first, then validate the fix and check the report monthly.'],
  'orphan-pages-fix': ['To find orphan pages, compare every URL your site has with the URLs a crawler reaches by following links.', 'Fix each orphan page by linking it from relevant pages, merging it, redirecting it or removing it.', 'Most orphans come from redesigns, menu changes and forgotten landing pages, so check after every big change.'],
  'seo-reporting-what-to-track': ['A useful SEO report starts with enquiries and sales from organic search, then explains them with Search Console clicks, queries and landing pages.', 'Compare the same period year on year where seasons matter, and look at trends over three months rather than reacting to one month.', 'Keyword ranking lists, authority scores and raw traffic totals are context at best; ask what changed, why, and what happens next month.'],
  'respond-to-website-enquiries-fast': ['Route every form, call and chat to a phone someone watches, with a named owner and a backup for each part of the day.', 'Use auto-replies that set an honest expectation, especially after hours, then follow with a personal reply from a short template.', 'Log time received and time of first reply for every enquiry, and review it monthly to see where leads wait.'],
  'connect-website-forms-to-crm': ['Email notifications alone get lost; sending every form entry to a CRM or sheet gives you one list you can assign, follow up and report on.', 'Use the form plugin\'s own add-on where one exists, a webhook or automation tool for everything else, and map fields carefully, including source and consent.', 'Always keep a copy of entries in WordPress, test the connection after updates, and collect only the personal data you actually need.'],
  'multi-step-forms-lead-qualification': ['Multi-step forms work when each step is short, easy questions come first and contact details are asked for last.', 'Ask only qualifying questions that change what you do next, such as service, timeline, location or budget range.', 'Route answers to the right person, CRM stage or WhatsApp follow-up, and track drop-off per step before changing anything.'],
  'whatsapp-marketing-small-business': ['Start with the free WhatsApp Business app on a dedicated number, a complete profile, catalogue and quick replies.', 'Message only people who opted in, keep broadcasts short and useful, and remove anyone who asks to stop.', 'Link WhatsApp to your website and Google Business Profile with click-to-chat buttons, and track which pages start chats.'],
  'exit-intent-popups': ['An exit-intent popup only helps when it offers something genuinely useful at the moment a visitor is about to leave, such as a guide, a callback or a first-order offer.', 'Desktop exit intent works by mouse movement; on mobile you need different triggers, and full-screen popups that block content can hurt both visitors and search performance.', 'Show it once per visitor, keep the form to one or two fields, exclude checkout and enquiry pages, and measure leads and sales rather than popup views.'],
  'google-reviews-on-website': ['You can show Google reviews on your website with a plugin or widget that pulls them automatically, or by quoting a few manually with a link to your profile.', 'Choose a lightweight option, place reviews near the decision points, and always link to your Google Business Profile so visitors can verify them.', 'Do not add review star schema for your own business reviews pulled from Google, and never edit, invent or selectively misrepresent reviews.'],
  'payment-gateways-india-compared': ['Compare payment gateways in India on how your customers actually pay, the full fee structure, settlement timing, KYC fit, international support and plugin quality.', 'Fees change and vary by method and business type, so check each provider\'s current pricing page and agreement rather than relying on blog comparisons.', 'Test the gateway end to end on your own site, including a real UPI payment, a failed payment and a refund, before switching it on for customers.'],
  'woocommerce-subscriptions': ['WooCommerce needs a subscription plugin for recurring products, and the gateway integration must support automatic renewals with that plugin, so confirm both together before you build.', 'In India, automatic charges generally run on a mandate the customer approves once, so many stores combine automatic renewals with prepaid plans or emailed renewal links.', 'Easy pausing, honest renewal reminders, a clear cancel option and a GST invoice for every renewal keep subscribers longer and cause fewer payment disputes.'],
  'sell-digital-products-wordpress': ['WooCommerce downloadable products suit a few files or a mix of digital and physical products, while dedicated download plugins suit software with licence keys and LMS plugins suit courses.', 'Serve files through the store rather than public links, require login where it makes sense, set download limits, and make sure delivery emails reach the inbox.', 'Digital products can be taxed and refunded differently from physical goods, so agree GST treatment with your CA and publish a clear refund policy before launch.'],
  'call-tracking-small-business': ['Start simple: track clicks on call and WhatsApp buttons in analytics, and ask every caller how they found you, before paying for tracking numbers.', 'Dynamic tracking numbers show which page, ad or keyword led to a call, but they need consistent business details for local SEO.', 'Recording calls and storing caller data brings consent and privacy duties, so check current rules and tell callers clearly.'],
  'google-ads-conversion-tracking-setup': ['Decide which actions really matter, such as enquiries, calls and purchases, and mark only those as primary conversions so Google Ads optimises for real leads.', 'Track form submissions on the thank-you step, calls and WhatsApp clicks as separate actions, and WooCommerce purchases with order value on the order-received page.', 'Test every conversion with Tag Assistant and a real test enquiry before spending, and check consent settings, duplicate counting and the conversion window.'],
  'meta-pixel-conversions-api': ['The Meta Pixel tracks actions in the browser, while the Conversions API sends the same events from your server, so using both gives Meta a more complete picture.', 'Shopify and WooCommerce both have official or well-supported integrations; the key is to send each event once with a matching event ID for deduplication.', 'Test every event in Events Manager, track only the actions that matter to your business, and be clear with visitors about the tracking you use.'],
  'woocommerce-product-reviews': ['Enable reviews and required star ratings under WooCommerce settings, show the verified owner label, and check how verified-only reviews work with guest checkout.', 'Ask for a review after delivery, not after payment, with a one-tap link by email, WhatsApp (with opt-in) or a parcel card, and send one reminder at most.', 'Publish honest negative reviews with a calm reply, show ratings near the price and in category listings, and only mark up reviews that genuinely appear on the page.'],
  'customize-woocommerce-emails': ['Start with WooCommerce\'s built-in email settings: logo, colours, footer text and which emails go to customers and admins.', 'For deeper changes use the block email editor or a customiser plugin, or override templates in a child theme, never in the plugin folder.', 'A beautiful email is useless if it lands in spam; send through SMTP with SPF, DKIM and DMARC, and test every order status.'],
  'woocommerce-multi-vendor-marketplace': ['WooCommerce vendor onboarding should take every seller through the same steps: application, KYC, approval, store profile, first product review, agreement and a test order with a refund.', 'WooCommerce plus a multi-vendor plugin such as Dokan, WCFM Marketplace, MultiVendorX or WC Vendors can run a marketplace, but decide the commission, payout and tax model before you pick the plugin.', 'Hold vendor earnings until the return window closes, ask your CA about GST registration, TCS and invoicing early, and start on hosting with object caching and a staging site.'],
  'lazy-loading-explained': ['Lazy loading delays offscreen images, videos and iframes until the visitor scrolls near them, which saves data and speeds up the first view.', 'Never lazy-load the main image visible on first load, because it delays Largest Contentful Paint; load it eagerly and give it high priority.', 'Set width and height on every image, replace heavy YouTube and map embeds with click-to-load facades, and avoid stacking several lazy-load plugins.'],
  'web-fonts-performance': ['Web font performance on a business website depends mostly on how many font files load and whether text can appear before they arrive.', 'Use one or two families, only the weights you use, WOFF2 files on your own domain, font-display: swap and a preload for the main above-the-fold font only.', 'Set a simple font budget, check it after every theme, plugin or design change, and test on a real phone over mobile data.'],
  'third-party-scripts-slow-website': ['Every chat widget, tracking pixel, embed and app script loads code from another server, and together they often slow a site more than its own theme and images.', 'Audit what loads, keep only scripts with a clear owner and purpose, and delay or lazy-load the rest so they do not block the first view or the first tap.', 'On Shopify, apps are the main source of third-party code, so remove unused apps and check the theme for leftover snippets.'],
  'landing-page-ab-testing': ['A/B testing only gives trustworthy answers with enough conversions, so low-traffic sites should test big changes such as the offer or form, not button colours.', 'Set up conversion tracking first, change one main thing per test, run it for full weeks and decide the success measure before you start.', 'When traffic is too low for testing, use heatmaps, recordings, customer questions and before-and-after comparisons to improve the page instead.'],
  'website-heatmaps-session-recordings': ['Heatmaps show patterns across many visitors, while session recordings show individual journeys; use both to find where people get stuck.', 'Start with your highest-value pages, such as the home page, a key service or product page and the enquiry or checkout page, and watch a reasonable sample before changing anything.', 'Mask form fields, mention the tool in your privacy policy and avoid recording sensitive pages, then turn findings into small, tested changes.'],
  'referral-program-website': ['A referral programme works when the reward is simple, sharing is easy on WhatsApp and both the referrer and the new customer get something.', 'WooCommerce and Shopify both support referral links or codes through plugins or apps; service businesses can run it with a form and a tracking sheet.', 'Write clear terms, check for self-referrals and fake accounts, and track referred sales so you know whether the programme is worth running.'],
  'fix-lcp-largest-contentful-paint': ['LCP measures when the biggest visible element, usually a hero image or heading, finishes rendering, and good is 2.5 seconds or less for most real visitors.', 'Split LCP into server response, resource load delay, load time and render delay, then fix the largest part first instead of installing another speed plugin.', 'The most common WordPress fixes are page caching, a correctly sized and preloaded hero image that is never lazy-loaded, and less render-blocking CSS and font loading.'],
  'fix-cls-layout-shift': ['Most CLS on WordPress comes from content without reserved space: images, embeds, ads and sliders need set dimensions or an aspect ratio before they load.', 'Fonts, cookie banners, sticky headers and delayed scripts cause most of the rest: preload the main font with a matched fallback, show notices as overlays, and keep headers a fixed height.', 'Find the exact shifting elements with PageSpeed Insights and the Chrome DevTools Performance panel, fix the shared template, then validate the fix in Search Console.'],
  'fix-inp-interaction-to-next-paint': ['INP measures how quickly a page responds visually after a tap, click or key press, and it is usually hurt by heavy JavaScript running on the main thread.', 'Find the slow interactions first using field data in Search Console and PageSpeed Insights, then fix the scripts behind them rather than guessing.', 'The biggest wins on WordPress usually come from removing unneeded plugins and third-party scripts, delaying non-essential JavaScript and simplifying heavy menus, sliders and popups.'],
  'choose-wordpress-maintenance-provider': ['Compare website maintenance packages by what is actually done each month, how updates are tested, where backups live and how fast emergencies are handled, not by the plan name.', 'Website maintenance charges vary with site type, change hours and support hours, so ask every provider to quote the same scope in writing.', 'Keep the domain, hosting and plugin licences in your own name, insist on monthly reports and make sure you can leave with your backups and access.'],
  'wordpress-cron-explained': ['WP-Cron only runs when someone visits the site, so on quiet or heavily cached sites scheduled tasks run late or not at all.', 'The dependable fix is DISABLE_WP_CRON in wp-config.php plus a server cron job in hPanel or cPanel, tested afterwards.', 'Use WP-CLI, WP Crontrol and WooCommerce\'s Scheduled Actions screen to see what is due, what ran and what failed.'],
  'wordpress-memory-limit-errors': ['The "allowed memory size exhausted" error means one page request needed more PHP memory than allowed; the file named is a clue, not always the culprit.', 'Setting WP_MEMORY_LIMIT to 256M and raising the PHP limit in your hosting panel fixes many cases, but only up to the cap your host allows.', 'If normal pages need far more memory, find the heavy plugin, builder page or task on staging instead of raising the limit again and again.'],
  'wordpress-vs-squarespace': ['Squarespace is quick and tidy for simple portfolio-style sites, while WordPress gives full ownership, more flexibility and a far larger pool of Indian developers and plugins.', 'For Indian payments, GST invoices, regional shipping and local integrations, WordPress with WooCommerce or Shopify is usually the more practical choice.', 'If selling online is the main goal, compare Shopify too; if content, leads and long-term control matter most, WordPress is the safer base.'],
  'wordpress-vs-google-sites': ['Google Sites is free, simple and reliable, which makes it fine for event pages, internal sites, quick tests and stopgaps.', 'It has no blog, payments or bookings and very little SEO control, so it struggles once your website needs to bring in enquiries from Google.', 'If you start on Google Sites, connect your own domain from day one, so you can move to WordPress later without losing your address.'],
  'in-vs-com-domain': ['For customers mainly in India, .in and .com both work; choose the shorter, clearer name rather than a twisted .com.', 'Exporters, SaaS and global brands should prefer .com, because .in tells Google and buyers the site is India-focused.', 'If you can, register both in your own account, use one everywhere and 301-redirect the other on https.'],
  'mixed-content-warnings-fix': ['A mixed content warning means an HTTPS page still loads images, scripts or styles over HTTP.', 'The lasting WordPress mixed content fix is updating the URLs at the source: database, theme files and builder settings.', 'An .htaccess HTTPS redirect or upgrade-insecure-requests header helps, but does not replace fixing the old links.'],
  'too-many-redirects-error-fix': ['ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS in WordPress means two settings disagree about the final address and keep redirecting each other.', 'Check cookies, Cloudflare SSL mode, the WordPress Address and Site Address, .htaccess and redirect plugins, in that order.', 'If wp-admin is locked, set the site URLs in wp-config.php and rename the plugins folder through your hosting file manager.'],
  'wordpress-not-sending-emails-smtp': ['WordPress sends mail through your hosting server by default, which is often blocked or marked as spam, so form and order emails quietly go missing.', 'The reliable fix is to send through SMTP or a transactional email service, using an address on your own domain with SPF, DKIM and DMARC set up.', 'After the fix, test every email type, keep a mail log, and recheck after hosting, domain or plugin changes.'],
  'ecommerce-website-cost-india': ['The platform, catalogue complexity, design and checkout needs drive most of an online store\'s build cost.', 'Payments, shipping rules and integrations add development time, and some add monthly or yearly fees.', 'Budget for running costs such as hosting, licences, gateway fees and maintenance, not just the build.'],
  'cheap-website-risks': ['A cheap website can be fine for a simple brochure need, as long as you own the domain, hosting and logins.', 'Very low prices usually mean nulled themes, no backups, no SEO basics and no handover, which turn into costs later.', 'Check ownership, licences, what happens after launch and who fixes problems before paying for any low-cost website.'],
  'website-refresh-vs-redesign': ['A refresh improves the site you have (content, photos, buttons, speed) while the platform, structure and URLs stay; a redesign changes the design, structure and often the theme or platform.', 'Choose a refresh when the site works on phones, updates safely and still matches what you sell; choose a redesign when it fails on phones, runs on an abandoned theme or no longer fits the business.', 'Whichever you choose, record a baseline, work on staging, keep URLs where possible and redirect any that change, so the pages that already rank keep their place.'],
  'dpdp-act-website-basics': ['Most business websites handle personal data through forms, analytics, chat widgets and payments, so it is worth mapping exactly what you collect and where it goes.', 'Practical steps such as collecting less, clear notices at each form, honest consent, security and a way to handle requests are sensible regardless of the final rules.', 'The Act and its rules are being phased in, so check the current official text and take advice from a lawyer for your own obligations; a developer handles the technical side.'],
  'website-terms-and-conditions': ['Terms and conditions set the rules for using your site and buying from you; they sit alongside, not instead of, a privacy policy and a refund or returns policy.', 'Online stores on WooCommerce or Shopify need clearer terms than brochure sites, covering pricing, payment, delivery, cancellations and returns.', 'Templates are a starting point only; have a lawyer review the final wording for your business, because this guide is not legal advice.'],
  'website-handover-checklist': ['A proper handover gives you working access to every account, your own Administrator login, a launch backup you control, documentation and training.', 'Log in to each account yourself, change shared passwords and confirm every licence is in your business name before you sign off.', 'Agree the handover list and support terms in writing before work starts, so launch day is a checklist rather than a negotiation.'],
  'stock-photos-vs-real-photos': ['Real photos of your team, premises, products and work build more trust than generic stock, especially for local service businesses.', 'Stock photos are fine for backgrounds, concepts and blog headers, as long as you hold a proper licence and avoid overused images.', 'Good real photos do not need an expensive shoot: plan a shot list, use natural light, and compress images before uploading.'],
  'google-tag-manager-basics': ['Google Tag Manager lets you add and change tracking such as GA4, Google Ads conversions and Meta Pixel from one place, without editing theme code each time.', 'Install the container once, then build tags with clear triggers, test every change in Preview mode and publish with a short version note.', 'Avoid double tracking from plugins and GTM, respect consent, and keep the container tidy with clear names and limited access.'],
  'change-domain-name-without-losing-seo': ['To change domain without losing SEO, keep the old domain, 301-redirect every old URL to its matching new URL and file a Change of Address in Search Console.', 'Build the redirect map before launch from a full crawl plus your top pages in Search Console, and never send everything to the new homepage.', 'Expect a short wobble; a steep, lasting drop usually points to missing or wrong redirects, so test them and watch both properties for several months.'],
  'website-for-furniture-appliance-rental': ['A rental website must show monthly rent by tenure, refundable deposit, delivery areas and what happens at the end of the term, all before checkout.', 'WooCommerce handles rental logic, deposits and custom checkout fields flexibly; Shopify can work with subscription and rental apps for simpler models.', 'Write clear terms on damage, maintenance, relocation and early closure, collect only the documents you need, and keep personal data secure.'],
  'website-for-auto-parts-dealers': ['Parts buyers need to know a part fits their vehicle, so fitment search by make, model, year and variant is the core of an auto parts website.', 'Label genuine, OEM and aftermarket parts honestly, show part numbers, and only claim dealership or brand authorisation you actually hold.', 'Separate retail and trade buyers: garages and mechanics need login pricing, quick reorder and WhatsApp ordering that feeds the same system.'],
  'website-for-dairy-milk-delivery': ['A milk delivery website is really a subscription manager, so design it around what subscribers do each evening: change, skip, pause and top up.', 'Publish a clear cut-off time, the areas and slots you serve, and how the prepaid wallet or monthly bill works before anyone signs up.', 'Describe your farm and sourcing in specific, checkable terms, show your FSSAI details, and avoid regulated or unprovable words.'],
  'website-for-digital-marketing-agencies': ['Decide who the agency is for before designing anything, because the homepage headline, service pages and case studies all hang off that one sentence.', 'Case studies need written client permission and real context; a short, honest story with modest figures persuades more than a wall of logos.', 'If you resell websites built by a development partner, be clear with yourself and your clients about who builds what, and never pass off a partner\'s portfolio as your own.'],
  'website-for-study-centres-libraries': ['Students choose a library within a short distance and visit before paying, so the site should show exact location, plans, timings and a quick way to book a visit.', 'Seat availability is only worth showing if it is accurate; otherwise offer a waitlist and confirm on WhatsApp.', 'Rules, safety for late hours and real photos of the hall at study time persuade serious aspirants and their parents more than a list of amenities.'],
  'website-for-marriage-bureaus': ['Decide whether you are a personal matchmaking service or a profile listing before building anything, because the two need very different privacy and moderation work.', 'Describe verification by listing what you check and what you cannot check, rather than claiming every profile is verified.', 'Get consent from the person in the profile, not just their parents, and give them a simple way to pause or delete their details.'],
  'website-for-speech-therapy-child-development': ['Parents search by concern, such as speech delay or sensory issues, so explain each therapy and who it helps in calm, plain language.', 'Show real therapist qualifications and registrations accurately, and never promise outcomes or timelines for a child.', 'Make the first assessment easy to book, keep enquiry forms minimal, and protect children\'s information and photos carefully.'],
  'website-for-3d-visualisation-studios': ['For a visualisation studio the website is the sales pitch, so organise the portfolio by project type and client need, not by date.', 'High-resolution renders and walkthroughs can be shown at full quality without a slow site by using modern image formats, responsive sizes, lazy loading and hosted video.', 'A quote form that asks for drawings, number of views, turnaround and output use lets you price accurately and reply quickly to builders, architects and overseas firms.'],
  'website-for-grocery-delivery-stores': ['A local grocery website must make repeat shopping fast: clear categories, forgiving search, saved lists and quick reorder.', 'Delivery rules need to be built in from day one: pin codes or areas served, slots, cut-off times, minimum order and substitutions.', 'Shopify suits a simple local store launched quickly; WooCommerce suits stores that need custom delivery logic or weight-based pricing.'],
  'test-structured-data-rich-results': ['Google\'s Rich Results Test is the main rich snippet testing tool: it shows which rich results a page is eligible for and lists errors and warnings.', 'Use the Schema Markup Validator to check all schema.org markup, and Search Console enhancement reports to monitor rich snippets across the whole site.', 'Valid markup makes a page eligible, not guaranteed: Google still decides whether to show a rich snippet based on quality, relevance and its own policies.'],
  'email-newsletter-small-business': ['An email list is a channel you own; a useful email once a month to people who asked for it can bring back past customers and move undecided ones to enquire.', 'Send from a proper email marketing tool on your own domain with SPF, DKIM and DMARC set up, never from a personal Gmail or Outlook inbox.', 'Grow the list with clear sign-up forms on your website, consent you can show, and content that answers customers\' real questions rather than pure promotion.'],
  'linkedin-b2b-website-traffic': ['LinkedIn builds trust with buyers, but enquiries happen on your website, so every profile, post and DM should point to a specific, relevant page.', 'Send LinkedIn visitors to focused landing pages with one clear action, and tag every link with UTM parameters so GA4 shows what actually works.', 'Consistent, useful posts from a real person beat company-page broadcasting; reply fast to website enquiries because B2B buyers compare several vendors.'],
  'category-tag-pages-seo': ['Category pages can rank well when they have a clear purpose, an intro and a sensible set of posts or products; most tag pages are thin and are better set to noindex.', 'Audit your taxonomy before changing settings: merge near-duplicate categories, delete tags used once, and redirect removed archives to the closest useful page.', 'For WooCommerce and Shopify stores, product category or collection pages are often the main ranking pages for commercial searches, so they deserve real content.'],
  'improve-wordpress-site-search': ['Default WordPress search only matches titles and content in a basic way, so it misses SKUs, attributes, custom fields and spelling variations that shoppers type.', 'A search plugin or hosted search service, plus synonyms for Indian spellings and Hinglish terms, fixes most "no results" problems without a rebuild.', 'Use your own search logs to find what visitors type and fail to find, then fix those terms first with synonyms, redirects or new content.'],
  'hreflang-multilingual-seo-india': ['Give each language its own URL, usually a subfolder like /hi/, and use valid codes such as hi, en-IN, hi-IN and x-default; a country code on its own, like IN, is not valid.', 'Hreflang must be reciprocal, point to canonical, indexable URLs and use one method (page head or XML sitemap); WPML, Polylang, TranslatePress and Weglot add it once translations are linked.', 'Human-reviewed translation and keyword research in Hindi, regional scripts and Hinglish matter more than the tags, and crawler checks now replace Search Console\'s retired International Targeting report.'],
  'website-for-cafes-coffee-shops': ['Most people check a cafe online to confirm the menu, prices, hours, location and the feel of the space before visiting, so put those on the first screen.', 'Use a text menu page instead of a PDF or Instagram image, with real photos, prices kept current and notes on dietary options.', 'Link the website, Google Business Profile and Instagram together, and use the site for what social media handles poorly: events, private bookings and selling beans.'],
  'website-for-hearing-aid-centres': ['Most visitors are family members researching for a parent, so explain the hearing test, trial and fitting process plainly and make booking easy by phone, WhatsApp or form.', 'Explain device styles, features and what affects price honestly, and avoid cure claims, invented outcomes or misleading brand authorisation.', 'Readable text, captions on videos and non-phone contact options matter more here than on almost any other kind of site.'],
  'website-for-homeopathy-clinics': ['Write condition pages that describe the concern and your consultation approach, never cure claims, because advertising rules for remedies and AYUSH practice apply to websites too.', 'State qualifications and registration exactly as they appear on the certificates: degree, institution, university, year, and the register you are on.', 'Build booking around two appointment types, a long first consultation and shorter follow-ups, with online consultation steps explained before payment.'],
  'youtube-video-seo-small-business': ['Write YouTube titles and descriptions around what customers actually search, including the city or service, and add chapters and accurate captions.', 'Embed each useful video on a relevant page of your own website with a written summary and VideoObject schema, so the page can earn the visit too.', 'Measure success by enquiries and calls from video viewers, not views alone, and link every video back to the matching service page.'],
  'instagram-to-website-enquiries': ['Send Instagram visitors to a page that matches the post, not your homepage, with WhatsApp and a short form near the top, and test it inside the Instagram app.', 'Use your bio link, Story link stickers, Highlights, pinned posts and captions as signposts to specific pages, and move serious DM conversations to WhatsApp politely.', 'Tag every link you share with UTM parameters and track form submissions, WhatsApp clicks and calls, so you know which posts bring enquiries and customers, not just likes.'],
  'google-ads-budget-small-business': ['Work out your Google Ads budget from profit per customer, close rate and landing page conversion rate, not from a round number.', 'Start with one or two profitable services in the area you actually serve, with conversion tracking tested before you spend seriously.', 'Scale only when cost per enquiry stays below your limit and lead quality is good; otherwise fix the page, keywords or offer first.'],
  'website-disaster-recovery-plan': ['Decide how much downtime and data loss you can accept, then set backup frequency and storage to match.', 'A backup only counts once you have restored it successfully, so test restores on a staging copy regularly.', 'Keep logins, contacts and a first-hour checklist somewhere you can reach even when the website and email are down.'],
  'wordpress-activity-logs': ['WordPress has no full change log built in: revisions cover content only, so an activity log plugin is needed to see logins, user, plugin and settings changes.', 'Activity logs, revisions, error logs and server logs answer different questions; check the right one first.', 'Give every person their own login, log the events that matter, set a retention period and have a named person review the log weekly.'],
  'website-accessibility-audit-checklist': ['Run an automated scan first, then do the manual checks for keyboard use, contrast, zoom, forms and screen readers, because tools catch only part of the problems.', 'Fix anything that blocks a task first, such as forms that cannot be submitted by keyboard or pop-ups that trap focus, before polishing smaller issues.', 'Many fixes are content edits you can make yourself, like alt text, headings and link wording; theme, menu and focus styles usually need a developer.'],
  'pincode-delivery-checker-woocommerce': ['A pincode checker on the product page answers three questions before checkout: do you deliver here, is COD available, and roughly when will it arrive.', 'The data can come from a simple uploaded pin code list or live from a courier or shipping aggregator, and each approach suits different stores.', 'Enforce the same rules at checkout, keep the list updated, and never promise exact delivery dates you cannot control.'],
  'woocommerce-wholesale-dealer-pricing': ['Write your pricing rules down first: who qualifies, which tiers exist, minimum quantities and how retail and trade customers differ.', 'Use user roles and an approval step so only verified dealers see trade prices, and capture GST details at registration.', 'Test every role, tier, coupon and invoice on staging, because pricing errors in a B2B store are expensive and easy to miss.'],
  'woocommerce-order-management-workflow': ['Agree what every order status means and who acts on it; add custom statuses such as Confirmed, Packed and Shipped only if each has a clear owner and next step.', 'Run one daily flow from payment check to dispatch: confirm COD orders, print slips in batches, create labels, add tracking and move orders to Shipped.', 'Give each person their own login with the narrowest role that works, check plugin compatibility before relying on HPOS, and log return and RTO reasons weekly.'],
  'membership-website-wordpress': ['Start with a clear reason people will pay every month or year, then plan simple membership levels and decide exactly which content each level unlocks.', 'Choose a membership plugin that supports your payment gateway in India, and remember that recurring payments by card and UPI have their own mandate rules.', 'Launch small with a few levels and solid content, keep logins, member data and the site secure, and add features once real members ask for them.'],
  'online-course-website-wordpress': ['An online course website with WordPress needs four parts working together: an LMS plugin, a payment gateway with UPI, secure video hosting and student accounts.', 'Host videos on a proper video platform rather than your web server, and plan for India-specific needs such as UPI, GST invoices and mobile-first students.', 'Start with one course, a clear sales page and a simple checkout, then add memberships, bundles or certificates once students are enrolling.'],
  'woocommerce-product-bundles-upsells': ['Upsells suggest a better version, cross-sells suggest an add-on, and bundles sell a set together; each belongs in a different place in the store.', 'WooCommerce linked and grouped products cover simple cases; a bundle plugin is needed for real kits with their own price, stock rules and options.', 'Price bundles honestly, check stock and GST handling on test orders, and judge offers by order value and margin, not by clicks.'],
  'website-for-electrical-contractors': ['Organise the site by the kind of buyer you serve, because a homeowner, a facilities manager and a plant engineer look for completely different proof.', 'Copy licence names, classes and numbers exactly from your certificates, since licensing differs by state and tender teams cross-check.', 'Projects with real scope details, a safety page that describes your actual system, and an RFQ form that accepts drawings do more selling than any slogan.'],
  'website-for-cold-storage-warehousing': ['Storage buyers shortlist on hard facts, so publish temperature bands, capacity units, chamber details and distances in a form they can compare quickly.', 'List only the licences, registrations and certifications you actually hold, with validity, and keep them current.', 'A space enquiry form that asks for commodity, quantity, temperature, period and handling needs lets your team quote in one reply.'],
  'website-for-tempo-traveller-bus-rental': ['Group organisers think in head count and luggage, so organise the fleet by seating capacity and give every vehicle type its own page with real photos.', 'Explain every part of the fare, including driver allowance, tolls, parking and permit charges, so your quote compares fairly with a low base rate elsewhere.', 'Corporate, school and wedding clients need different pages: contracts, safety details, billing and multi-vehicle coordination rather than a single trip quote.'],
  'website-for-fire-safety-companies': ['Fire safety buyers want to know what you supply, what you install and maintain, which areas you cover and how fast you respond, so make those clear on every service page.', 'Separate pages for products, installation, AMC and refilling, each with its own enquiry form, bring better leads than one long services page.', 'Be careful with compliance claims: describe what you do accurately and mention approvals, licences or certifications only if your company genuinely holds them.'],
  'website-for-recycling-ewaste-companies': ['A website for e-waste recycling companies needs two front doors: quick pickup booking for homes and shops, and a proof-led route for corporate buyers.', 'Show registrations, certificates and data destruction steps only as you genuinely hold and perform them, with exact, hedged wording.', 'A clear pickup form, an accepted-items list and local search basics turn visits into collection requests.'],
  'wordpress-backup-plugins-compared': ['Host backups are a useful first layer but should not be your only copy; keep at least one backup off the server in cloud storage you control.', 'Choose a plugin by what you need: free scheduled backups to Drive or S3, real-time backups for busy WooCommerce stores, or managed off-site backups with easy restores.', 'A backup you have never restored is a guess. Test a restore on staging every few months and after major changes.'],
  'website-for-ambulance-services': ['A private ambulance website has one main job: put a large, always-visible 24/7 call button in front of a frightened visitor on a phone.', 'List only the services, vehicles, equipment and areas you genuinely cover, and never imply you are the government 108 service.', 'Keep the site light, match your Google Business Profile details exactly, and track calls so you know which pages and ads bring enquiries.'],
  'website-for-specialist-doctors': ['Patients and their families compare specialists on specific experience, so show full degrees, registration, fellowships and the conditions you actually treat.', 'Give each major procedure or condition its own plain-language page covering who it is for, what happens, recovery and where it is done.', 'Make consultation, second-opinion and appointment requests easy on mobile, and keep claims factual and in line with current medical advertising rules.'],
  'website-for-kids-activity-classes': ['Parents decide on age suitability, timings, location and safety, so programme pages by activity and age group with a clear batch calendar matter most.', 'Make a trial class or camp enquiry the main call to action, with a short form, WhatsApp and a quick, personal follow-up.', 'Collect only the children\'s data you need, explain how it is used, and ask for consent before sharing photos of children online.'],
  'website-for-sports-bicycle-stores': ['Sports and bicycle store websites work best as a catalogue plus enquiry tool, with optional online sales for smaller items that ship easily.', 'Size and fit guides, frame size charts and a service booking form answer the questions that otherwise stop buyers from visiting or ordering.', 'Local SEO and a complete Google Business Profile bring nearby buyers; a clean WooCommerce or Shopify setup handles online orders when you are ready.'],
  'website-for-electronics-appliance-stores': ['Build the catalogue around real buying decisions such as room size, capacity and budget, and keep specifications, prices and stock accurate.', 'Word offers, EMI and warranty carefully: say who provides them, what conditions apply and that the brand or lender has the final terms.', 'Choose WooCommerce or Shopify based on how many products you list and whether you sell online, and give each store location its own genuine page.'],
  'website-for-perfume-fragrance-brands': ['Describe each fragrance in plain words, with notes, mood, longevity guidance and occasions, so buyers can choose without smelling it.', 'Discovery sets and trial sizes lower the risk of buying a full bottle online, and both WooCommerce and Shopify can sell them as bundles.', 'Check courier rules for shipping perfume before launch, and keep labels and claims accurate.'],
  'website-for-translation-services': ['A translation agency website should make language pairs, document types and turnaround options easy to find, with a quote form that accepts files.', 'Trust is everything: explain confidentiality, how files are handled and what certified or notarised translation means, without overstating credentials.', 'Separate pages for translation, interpretation and localisation, and for key language pairs, help you appear for specific searches.'],
  'website-for-civil-engineering-consultants': ['Engineering clients judge a consultancy on proof, so project pages with scope, role, constraints and photos matter more than a flashy homepage.', 'Separate pages for each service (structural design, audits, PMC, surveying) help you rank for what developers, architects and government buyers search.', 'Keep credentials accurate and current, make company profiles easy to download, and route enquiries to someone who replies quickly.'],
  'website-for-lift-elevator-companies': ['A lift company website should separate new installations, modernisation and AMC or breakdown service, because each buyer arrives with a different question.', 'Builders, societies and homeowners decide on trust, so show completed project types, safety practices and how service calls are handled, without inventing claims.', 'Clear quote forms that ask for floors, stops, shaft size and building type save site visits and help the sales team reply faster.'],
  'search-console-content-ideas': ['The Performance report shows the exact queries you already appear for; impressions with few clicks point to titles, pages or topics worth improving.', 'Filter by country (such as India) and look at positions roughly 5 to 30 first, because those pages are closest to earning real clicks.', 'Turn findings into a short list: improve an existing page when it matches the query, and write a new page only when the intent is genuinely different.'],
  'rank-for-your-business-name': ['If your site does not appear for its own name, check indexing first: Search Console, a noindex tag or a blocked robots.txt are the usual culprits.', 'Put the exact business name in the homepage title, H1, About page and structured data, and use the same name, address and phone everywhere.', 'A verified Google Business Profile, consistent directory listings and genuine mentions from other sites help Google connect your name to your website.'],
  'wordpress-seo-plugins-compared': ['Yoast SEO, Rank Math and All in One SEO all handle the basics well: titles, meta descriptions, XML sitemaps, canonical tags and basic schema.', 'Pick one plugin, configure it properly and keep it; running two SEO plugins together causes duplicate tags and confusing settings.', 'The plugin is a tool, not a ranking factor: content, internal links, speed and real reviews matter far more than which plugin you install.'],
  'website-for-uniform-manufacturers': ['Uniform buyers want to see sectors served, fabrics, customisation options, minimum quantities and lead times before they ask for a quote.', 'A catalogue with a bulk quote and sample request flow suits corporate, hospital and industrial orders, while an online store suits school uniforms sold to parents.', 'WooCommerce handles quote carts and school-specific stores flexibly; Shopify suits simpler fixed-price retail ranges with little upkeep.'],
  'website-for-borewell-drilling': ['Borewell customers search locally and decide fast, so the website should show services, areas covered and a one-tap call or WhatsApp button on every page.', 'Explain the process, what affects cost and what customers must arrange, instead of promising water or quoting figures you cannot guarantee.', 'Real site photos, genuine Google reviews and honest service area pages build more trust than generic stock images or copied city pages.'],
  'website-for-franchise-brands': ['Give prospective partners their own franchise section with a page per format, explaining space, role, staffing, territory and the agreement in plain language.', 'Break down investment into one-time, ongoing and working capital costs, label figures as indicative and never promise returns or payback periods.', 'Use a short multi-step application form, an accurate outlet locator and a clear warning about fake franchise offers to attract serious, safe enquiries.'],
  'website-for-tiles-sanitaryware-showrooms': ['Organise the catalogue the way buyers think, by room, look, size and finish, and give each product practical details such as coverage per box and where it can be used.', 'List only brands you are authorised to sell, and never call yourself official or exclusive unless the brand has confirmed it in writing.', 'Make the showroom visit the main call to action for homeowners, and give builders, contractors and architects a separate route for project quantities and BOQs.'],
  'website-for-mattress-sleep-brands': ['A website for mattress brands has to replace the showroom: explain feel, layers and who each model suits in plain words.', 'A size guide for Indian beds, honest trial and warranty terms, and clear EMI and delivery details remove most hesitation.', 'Shopify and WooCommerce both work; pick based on custom sizes, showroom stock and how much you want to manage yourself.'],
  'digital-pr-small-business': ['Digital PR works when you give journalists something useful: original data you really collected, expert comment, a local story or a practical resource.', 'Pitch a short, specific email to the right writer at Indian trade, city and industry publications, and never pay for links dressed up as news.', 'Coverage only pays off when your website is ready for the visitors and links it brings, so fix the landing page, About page and contact path first.'],
  'website-for-tour-guides-heritage-walks': ['Give each walk or tour its own page with route, duration, meeting point, inclusions, languages and a clear way to book or ask.', 'Simple booking with a deposit through UPI or a payment gateway, plus WhatsApp for questions, suits most independent guides and small walk companies.', 'Google Business Profile, honest reviews and fast mobile pages matter because most travellers find and book tours on their phones, often the same week.'],
  'website-microcopy-that-converts': ['Write microcopy for the moment the visitor is in: deciding, filling in, making a mistake, waiting, finding nothing or paying.', 'Every reassurance near a button must be literally true, and anything about price, refunds or reply times must match what actually happens.', 'For Indian audiences, plain Indian English usually works best, with familiar terms such as UPI, pin code and WhatsApp, and Hinglish only where it matches how you already talk to customers.'],
  'rfq-forms-b2b-websites': ['An RFQ form is not a contact form: it should collect just enough detail to prepare a quote or a focused first call, and nothing that does not earn its place.', 'Ask for product, quantity, specification, delivery location and timeline, allow drawing uploads, and leave extras such as full address for later.', 'Route each RFQ to the right salesperson with an instant acknowledgement, store it in a CRM or sheet, and reply quickly; speed often decides who wins the order.'],
  'website-for-cooking-baking-classes': ['Give every workshop its own page with the menu, level, date, fee, seats left and what to bring, and keep the calendar current.', 'Online booking with seat limits, UPI or card payment and automatic WhatsApp or email confirmations removes most of the back-and-forth.', 'Free recipes, real student bakes and a complete Google Business Profile help you get found for searches like "baking classes in {city}".'],
  'website-for-recording-studios': ['A website for recording studios should let clients hear your work, see the rooms and check the gear before they message you.', 'Separate pages for recording, mixing, mastering, dubbing and podcasts answer different questions from different clients.', 'Publish samples only with permission, and make slot booking rules and rate guidance clear to cut down long WhatsApp threads.'],
  'lead-scoring-small-business': ['Lead scoring simply adds points for fit and intent, based on what your own past customers had in common, so the most promising enquiries get called first.', 'Your website form can collect the signals that matter, such as location, service, timeline and budget range, without becoming long or off-putting.', 'Start with a spreadsheet and three bands, review the scores against actual sales every month or two, and never use a low score as a reason to ignore someone.'],
  'website-for-baby-products-brands': ['Parents buy baby products on trust, so product pages need full ingredients or materials, age guidance, safety information and real photos.', 'Avoid medical or absolute claims such as "100% safe" or "cures rashes"; check the current rules for cosmetics, toys and baby food labelling.', 'Both Shopify and WooCommerce suit Indian baby brands; choose based on catalogue, budget and who will manage the store, then set up UPI, COD and shipping properly.'],
  'website-for-inverter-battery-dealers': ['Organise the site around customer needs, such as home backup, shop backup and battery replacement, not just brand catalogues.', 'A simple backup or load guide, clear battery exchange information and a service request form answer the questions that otherwise come by phone.', 'Local pages, an accurate Google Business Profile and fast mobile pages help dealers win "inverter battery near me" searches.'],
  'image-compression-plugins-compared': ['ShortPixel, Imagify, EWWW Image Optimizer and Smush all compress images well; the right choice depends on WebP or AVIF needs, server limits and whether you prefer local or cloud processing.', 'Resize images to the size they display, choose sensible compression, serve next-gen formats and keep originals backed up before bulk optimising an existing library.', 'A compression plugin is one part of image speed: lazy loading, correct dimensions, a CDN and good hosting matter as much on Indian mobile networks.'],
  'website-for-wedding-choreographers': ['Answer the questions families ask first: can you teach non-dancers, how many sessions, where sessions happen, and whether you are free on their date.', 'Use click-to-play videos grouped by performance type, with rehearsal clips beside final performances, so the site stays fast on mobile data.', 'Make the enquiry form ask for the sangeet date, city, number of acts and dancers, and preferred format, so you can check availability and quote quickly.'],
  'website-for-pandit-puja-services': ['A website for pandits works best as one calm, clear page per puja, saying what is included and what the family must arrange.', 'Booking by date or muhurat, online puja steps and language options let families at home and abroad book with confidence.', 'Explain what decides the dakshina, avoid promises about outcomes, and collect only the personal details you need.'],
  'woocommerce-gift-cards': ['WooCommerce needs a plugin for gift cards and store credit, while Shopify includes gift cards on its plans, so the platform affects setup effort more than features.', 'Write clear terms for validity, partial use, refunds and lost codes before launch, and ask your CA how GST applies to gift card sales and redemptions.', 'Protect gift cards from misuse with random codes, payment checks on high-value orders and limits on how cards can be bought.'],
  'website-for-tractor-farm-equipment-dealers': ['State exactly what you are: an authorised dealer for a named brand and area, a multi-brand used tractor dealer, or an implement seller, and follow your manufacturer\'s rules for logos and offers.', 'Build a page for each model or implement family with real photos, brochure-sourced specifications and an on-road price enquiry, and word finance and subsidy information carefully.', 'Design for rural buyers: Hindi or the local language, click-to-call and WhatsApp first, light pages for patchy networks, and service and spares given as much space as sales.'],
  'website-for-event-organisers-ticketing': ['Keep event information on your own website even if a ticketing platform handles payment, because those pages keep their search value from one edition to the next.', 'Selling through WooCommerce gives you the buyer relationship and lower platform dependence, but you then own capacity limits, ticket delivery, check-in and sale-day load.', 'Plan the gate before the sale: how QR codes are scanned, what happens offline, and how duplicates and transfers are handled.'],
  'wordpress-plugin-conflicts-troubleshooting': ['Never hunt for a plugin conflict on a busy live site: take a backup, then reproduce the problem on staging or in Health Check troubleshooting mode.', 'Deactivate plugins in halves rather than one by one, then read the debug log and the browser console to confirm which plugin, file or script is failing.', 'Most conflicts are prevented by updating one plugin at a time on staging, avoiding overlapping plugins and removing anything abandoned or nulled.'],
  'website-for-gym-equipment-sellers': ['Gym equipment sellers usually need two flows: an online store for home fitness products and a quote route for full gym setups for gyms, hotels, societies and offices.', 'Both Shopify and WooCommerce work; Shopify suits sellers who want a managed platform, while WooCommerce suits those needing custom quote, B2B pricing or WordPress content.', 'Heavy and bulky items need clear delivery, installation, pincode, COD and warranty information on every product page, or buyers hesitate.'],
  'website-for-lab-scientific-equipment-suppliers': ['Lab buyers search by model number and compare specifications line by line, so a catalogue with consistent spec fields and good site search matters more than design flourishes.', 'State your relationship with each principal exactly, whether manufacturer, authorised distributor, dealer or service partner, and for which territory.', 'Quote forms, institutional buying information and clear installation, AMC and calibration pages are what turn a technical visitor into a purchase order.'],
  'rebranding-your-website': ['Good website rebrand SEO comes from changing as little as possible at once: keep URLs and content where you can, and 301-redirect anything that moves.', 'Update every mention of the old name, including titles, schema, Google Business Profile and listings, so Google connects the old and new brand.', 'Launch from a checklist, then watch Search Console for 404s, indexing and branded searches for the first few weeks.'],
  'website-for-drone-services': ['Give each drone service its own page with sample deliverables, because a wedding couple, a builder and a farmer group are asking very different questions.', 'State registrations, pilot certificates and insurance only as they genuinely stand, and tell clients that permissions depend on the location and the current rules.', 'Use an enquiry form that asks for location, job type, area or duration and deadline, so you can quote quickly without a long phone call.'],
  'website-for-aquarium-shops': ['Treat livestock and dry goods as two different businesses on one site: keep permanent species pages with a simple stock status, and sell tanks, equipment and food through a normal online store.', 'Offer live fish for store pickup or your own local delivery only, set up as a separate shipping class, and state your arrival policy plainly; courier shipping of live animals is a special case to check first.', 'Setup and maintenance services, species and care guides, a complete Business Profile and WhatsApp for photo questions bring the local enquiries that a stock list alone never will.'],
  'website-for-musical-instrument-stores': ['Organise the catalogue by instrument family first and brand second, with Indian classical instruments described in the terms their players use.', 'Demo videos of the actual instrument recorded in your shop, and an easy way to book a trial visit, do more than any amount of description.', 'Rentals, repairs and a link to classes give people reasons to come back, so give each one its own page with clear terms.'],
  'website-for-domestic-help-agencies': ['Families choose a placement agency on trust, so explain your verification, training and replacement process clearly before asking for an enquiry.', 'Build separate pages for each role you place, such as full-day maids, nannies, cooks and elder caretakers, because families search for the specific role.', 'Keep the enquiry short and mobile-friendly, offer WhatsApp, and handle worker and family personal data carefully with a clear privacy policy.'],
  'seo-for-pdfs-brochures': ['Google can index text-based PDFs, but a PDF rarely beats a proper web page, so the main content should live in HTML with the PDF as a download.', 'Optimise every PDF you keep: a descriptive file name, a real document title, selectable text, compressed images and links back to your site.', 'Scanned brochures without real text are almost invisible to search, and very large files frustrate mobile visitors on Indian networks.'],
  'meta-catalog-instagram-shopping-woocommerce': ['Connect WooCommerce through the official Facebook for WooCommerce plugin or a product feed, and Shopify through the Facebook and Instagram sales channel app.', 'Clean product data (titles, prices in INR, availability, images, brand and identifiers) decides whether items are approved and how ads perform.', 'Keep stock and prices in sync automatically, install the pixel and Conversions API, and check which shopping features Meta currently offers in India.'],
  'website-for-dog-trainers': ['Explain your training methods openly, because many owners worry about harsh techniques and choose trainers whose approach matches their values.', 'Create separate pages for puppy training, basic obedience, behaviour problems and home visits, since owners search for the specific problem they have.', 'Make a first consultation easy to book on mobile with a short form and WhatsApp, and show real dogs and owners only with permission.'],
  'hindi-devanagari-fonts-website': ['Always use Unicode Hindi text, never images of text or legacy non-Unicode fonts, so content can be searched, copied, read aloud and indexed.', 'Pick a Devanagari font with clear matras and conjuncts, pair it with a Latin font of similar weight, and set a slightly larger size and line height for Hindi.', 'Load only the Devanagari subset and weights you need, host fonts locally with a swap setting, and test on real Android phones.'],
  'woocommerce-product-filters': ['Pick filters from how your buyers actually decide, such as size, fabric, price or compatibility, and set up clean attributes before adding any plugin.', 'Filtered pages can slow a store and create thousands of crawlable URLs, so test speed and keep most filter combinations out of the index.', 'Shopify has built-in collection filters through Search and Discovery, while WooCommerce gives more control through blocks or a filter plugin.'],
  'contact-page-that-gets-enquiries': ['A contact page that gets enquiries offers the options your customers prefer, usually a short form, click-to-call and WhatsApp, with clear hours and reply times.', 'Keep the form short, ask only what you need to respond well, and send people to a thank-you page that confirms what happens next.', 'Track form submissions, calls and WhatsApp clicks separately, and make sure every enquiry reaches someone who replies quickly.'],
  'meet-the-team-page': ['A meet the team page builds trust by showing the real people behind the business, with genuine photos, roles and short bios focused on how each person helps customers.', 'Only list real people with their consent, state qualifications accurately, and update the page when someone joins or leaves.', 'If you work solo, say so honestly: an about page focused on your process and experience works better than inventing a team.'],
  'google-business-profile-photos': ['Cover the full set: a square logo, a representative cover photo, exterior shots from where customers arrive, interior, team, at-work, and real products or finished work.', 'Follow the current guidance from Google on formats and sizes, keep photos real and lightly edited, and add a few new ones whenever something changes rather than uploading hundreds at once.', 'Geotags and keyword file names have no confirmed ranking benefit; what helps is honest, current photos that match your website and win the click, the call and the visit.'],
  'website-for-plumbers': ['Most plumbing searches are urgent and on mobile, so the website must load fast and put click-to-call and WhatsApp buttons on every screen.', 'Separate pages for main services and the areas you genuinely cover help you appear for searches like leak repair or bathroom fitting in a specific locality.', 'Trust signals such as real job photos, reviews, clear visit charges explained in words, and honest service hours turn a quick visit into a call.'],
  'website-for-carpenters': ['Clients hire carpenters on proof of work, so a well-organised project gallery with short descriptions matters more than anything else on the site.', 'A quote request form that asks for room, measurements, material preference and photos saves site visits and filters serious enquiries.', 'Service pages for kitchens, wardrobes, beds and repairs, plus a strong Google Business Profile, help a local carpenter appear for the searches that bring work.'],
  'website-for-bike-rentals': ['Riders choose a bike rental on fleet, availability, tariff, deposit, documents and pickup location, so make all six clear on a phone.', 'An online booking flow with date and time selection, UPI payment for the advance and clear document requirements cuts down on calls and no-shows.', 'Local SEO, a strong Google Business Profile and pages for each pickup point or tourist destination bring riders searching nearby.'],
  'website-for-stationery-shops': ['A stationery shop website earns its keep on three jobs: school list kits before term starts, repeat office supply orders and local delivery or pickup.', 'WooCommerce suits quote-based office orders and large catalogues with many variations; Shopify suits a simpler store that needs to launch and run with little upkeep.', 'Local SEO, a complete Google Business Profile and WhatsApp ordering often matter more for a neighbourhood stationery shop than a huge online catalogue.'],
  'website-for-bookstores': ['An independent bookstore website should show what makes the shop different: curated lists, staff picks, regional language titles, events and personal service.', 'Both WooCommerce and Shopify can run a bookstore; the choice depends on catalogue size, how you manage stock and how much you want to customise.', 'Good search by title, author and ISBN, honest stock status, India-friendly payments and clear shipping rules matter more than a large catalogue.'],
  'website-for-plant-nurseries': ['Decide what you can deliver alive and how far before building anything, then let the website offer each plant only inside the zone where it travels well.', 'Photograph the plants you actually have, state height and pot size, and say plainly when a photo shows a typical plant rather than the exact one.', 'Builders, landscapers and housing societies buy in quantity and need an availability list and a quote route, not a shopping cart.'],
  'website-for-car-service-garages': ['Decide whether you are a multi-brand garage, a specialist or a quick-service centre, and lead with that, because it changes which pages matter.', 'Explain how estimates work and what happens at each stage of a job, from pickup to final invoice, so customers know there will be no surprise bill.', 'Describe insurance claim help and carmaker names in careful, factual terms, and offer only as many booking slots as your bays can keep.'],
  'website-for-loan-agents-dsa': ['Explain each loan type, the documents needed and how the process works in plain language, and name lenders only with permission.', 'Keep enquiry forms short, explain why each detail is asked, and never collect full documents or OTPs through a basic web form.', 'Avoid promises like guaranteed approval or lowest rates, and check current lender and regulatory guidance before publishing claims.'],
  'website-for-two-wheeler-dealers': ['Read your dealership agreement before planning the site, because it usually decides what you may say about models, prices, offers and the brand.', 'Treat new models and used bikes as two different catalogues, each with its own page layout, enquiry button and level of detail.', 'Test ride and service booking, careful finance wording and a page for every showroom and service point are what turn local searches into visits.'],
  'wordpress-auto-updates': ['Leave minor core updates, translations and small, well-maintained plugins on auto-update, but update major core releases, page builders, WooCommerce and your theme by hand after testing.', 'Auto-updates only roll back fatal errors, so broken layouts, silent form failures and expired premium licences still need daily backups, uptime monitoring, a monitored admin email and a weekly check.', 'Use staging for stores, major versions and PHP changes, check exactly what your host\'s managed updates cover, and keep a recent backup, because restoring it is the most reliable rollback.'],
  'choose-wordpress-developer-pune': ['Shortlist three or four Pune developers or studios, check their live sites on your own phone and ask what they personally built on each.', 'Compare written scopes line by line, and use a small paid first task when two options look equally good.', 'Keep the domain, hosting, WordPress admin login and Google accounts in your business name from day one.'],
  'website-content-calendar-small-business': ['Choose a publishing rhythm you can keep for a full year, usually one or two useful pieces a month, rather than an ambitious plan that stops after six weeks.', 'Plan festival and seasonal content four to eight weeks ahead, because people search before the occasion, and check the dates each year since many festivals move.', 'Give every month one refresh slot for an older post, and turn each new piece into Google Business Profile posts, social posts and WhatsApp updates.'],
  'woocommerce-wishlist': ['A wishlist helps stores with considered or gift purchases, such as jewellery, sarees, furniture and baby products, more than low-cost repeat buys.', 'Allow guest wishlists, make sharing on WhatsApp easy, and send back-in-stock or price-drop reminders only to shoppers who opted in.', 'Choose a lightweight plugin or Shopify app, check it works with caching, and track wishlist adds and resulting orders before keeping it.'],
  'wordpress-white-screen-of-death': ['The white screen is almost always a PHP error from a plugin, theme, memory limit or PHP version change, and the debug log usually names the culprit.', 'Work in a safe order: check recovery mode email, enable debug logging, disable plugins by renaming the folder, then switch theme and check memory.', 'Take a backup before changing files, and test updates on a staging copy so a white screen never reaches a live business site again.'],
  'error-establishing-database-connection-fix': ['Check causes in a fixed order: is the database server up, are the four details in wp-config.php right, do they work outside WordPress, and is the hosting account hitting limits.', 'Damaged tables and backup restores come last, and both need a fresh backup first because they change data rather than settings.', 'A short, specific message to your host and uptime monitoring with a keyword check turn a long outage into a short one.'],
  'website-for-footwear-stores': ['Footwear sells online when buyers trust the fit: clear size charts in UK/India sizes, foot-length guidance and honest notes on how each style runs.', 'Plan variants, stock by size and a simple exchange process before launch, on Shopify or WooCommerce, because size swaps are a normal part of the business.', 'COD, pin code checks and fast mobile product pages matter for Indian shoppers, alongside UPI and card payments.'],
  'website-for-saree-ethnic-wear-stores': ['Saree website development is mostly about trust: honest drape, border and pallu photos plus exact fabric, weave and length details.', 'Blouse stitching, occasion collections and WhatsApp-assisted selling need to be planned into the store, not bolted on later.', 'UPI, COD rules, clear returns and fast image-heavy pages decide whether visitors from beyond your city actually buy.'],
  'website-for-watch-stores': ['Authorised dealer status is your strongest trust signal, so state it brand by brand and branch by branch, exactly as your agreements allow.', 'Organise the catalogue by brand, then collection, then reference, and photograph your own stock so buyers can judge size, finish and condition.', 'Warranty and authenticity language should repeat what the brand or your own written policy actually says, not stronger promises, and high-value pieces sell best through a booked visit.'],
  'local-seo-pune-businesses': ['Local SEO in Pune starts with a complete Google Business Profile, consistent contact details and steady genuine reviews, before any blog or area pages.', 'Pune and Pimpri-Chinchwad are large and spread out, so area pages only help when they hold real, unique information about work you do in that locality.', 'Track calls, WhatsApp clicks and form enquiries, not just rankings, so you know which searches actually bring business.'],
  'not-showing-on-google-maps': ['First confirm whether the listing is missing, unverified, suspended or simply ranking low, because each problem has a different fix.', 'Most visibility issues come from verification, the wrong primary category, address or service area problems, or a thin profile with few reviews.', 'A website with the same name, address and phone number, plus genuine local pages and reviews, strengthens the listing over time.'],
  'woocommerce-order-notifications-sms-whatsapp': ['Email remains the record for every order; WhatsApp and SMS are for short, timely updates such as confirmation, dispatch and delivery.', 'Automated WhatsApp messages need the WhatsApp Business Platform with approved templates, and business SMS in India needs DLT registration first.', 'Collect clear opt-in at checkout, send only messages customers expect, and test every order status before going live.'],
  'seo-pricing-models-india': ['SEO in India is usually sold as a monthly retainer, a one-time audit, a fixed project, hourly consulting or a performance deal, and each suits a different situation.', 'Cost depends on competition, site size and condition, how much content and technical work is included, and who actually does the work.', 'Judge a quote by its scope, deliverables and reporting, not by the monthly figure, and be wary of guaranteed rankings or link packages.'],
  'website-payment-terms-milestones': ['Pay a modest advance, then tie every later payment to something you can see, such as an approved design or a working staging site.', 'Whether you hire a freelancer or an agency, put the payment schedule and what each instalment buys in writing.', 'Instalments after launch are possible on some projects, but expect admin access or ownership transfer to be tied to the final payment.'],
  'hidden-website-costs': ['Compare running costs over two or three years, not the first year, because introductory hosting prices and bundled free domains usually end after the first term.', 'Ask for a written list of every paid licence, subscription and account, with renewal dates and whose name each one is in.', 'Budget for upkeep and for getting visitors, since a site that is never maintained or promoted tends to cost more later than it saved at the start.'],
  'website-for-ice-cream-parlours': ['Run the flavour menu as an editable "flavour board" with today, coming back and seasonal sections, and reuse the same seasonal page every year so it keeps its search history.', 'Give party, bulk and event orders their own page with a quote form that asks for date, guests, flavours and delivery area, and keep your own ordering ahead of delivery app links.', 'Show your FSSAI details, describe frozen desserts and dietary options accurately, and keep hours and flavours consistent across your website, Google and delivery apps.'],
  'website-for-mehendi-artists': ['A mehendi artist website should lead with a well-organised portfolio, split by bridal, guest, Arabic and other styles, with real photos of your own work.', 'Clear package details and a booking form that asks for date, city, number of people and style save long chats and help you check availability quickly.', 'Instagram brings attention, but a website with a Google Business Profile, reviews and city pages helps brides find you when they search on Google.'],
  'website-for-chess-academies': ['Parents want to know the level their child will start at, who the coach is and whether classes are online, offline or both, so make those answers obvious.', 'A free demo class booking form is usually the strongest call to action for a chess academy, followed by quick WhatsApp follow-up.', 'Tournament news, student progress stories shared with consent and clear level pages give the site fresh, genuine content that helps it rank.'],
  'wordpress-xmlrpc-explained': ['XML-RPC is an older remote-access feature that most business sites no longer need; Jetpack, some apps and pingbacks are the main things that may still use it, and the REST API has replaced it for most jobs.', 'Floods of POST requests to xmlrpc.php in your access logs, slowdowns or resource-limit errors are signs that bots are using it for password guessing that can bypass login protection.', 'Block it at the server or firewall to save resources, or switch it off with a security plugin or code snippet, then test Jetpack, apps and connected services; restrict instead of blocking if something needs it.'],
  'wordpress-user-accounts-audit': ['List every account, remove people who no longer need access, and give everyone else the lowest role that still lets them work.', 'Replace shared logins with one named account per person, each with its own email, strong password and two-factor authentication.', 'An administrator nobody created is a warning sign of a hack: record its details, then close the way in, not just the account.'],
  'stop-spam-user-registrations-wordpress': ['First check whether open registration is even needed; many business sites can simply untick Anyone can register and set the default role to Subscriber.', 'For stores and membership sites, layer a CAPTCHA such as Cloudflare Turnstile, a honeypot, email confirmation and rate limiting rather than relying on one tool.', 'Clean out existing fake accounts carefully, after a backup, and watch for spam users with higher roles, which can signal a real compromise.'],
  'agency-wordpress-care-plans': ['A good white label WordPress care plan covers updates, backups, uptime, security and a fixed amount of small changes, all reported under the agency brand.', 'Write the scope down per tier: what is included, what counts as extra work, response times and who talks to the end client.', 'Outsourcing care plans to a developer in India works best with staging, clear handover notes, shared logins through a password manager and an overlap window for urgent issues.'],
  'agency-developer-handoff-checklist': ['Most outsourced WordPress projects slip because of missing inputs such as unlicensed fonts, unspecified forms or late hosting access, not because of the code.', 'Hand over one approved design file with every state, final content, all access through proper user accounts, and a written list of integrations and SEO requirements.', 'Agree who answers questions, how feedback is collected and what counts as done before the build starts, so review rounds stay short and predictable.'],
  'website-qa-checklist-before-launch-agencies': ['Run QA as a series of focused passes on staging, each with its own checklist, rather than one long click-around the day before the client review.', 'Log every issue with page, device, expected result and severity, and agree in advance which severities block a review or a launch.', 'Finish with a written sign-off that lists what was tested, what is known and deferred, and who approved it.'],
  'reduce-server-response-time-ttfb': ['TTFB is the time before the browser receives the first byte of your page; on WordPress it mostly depends on hosting, caching, PHP version and how much work plugins do per request.', 'Full-page caching is usually the biggest single fix, followed by a current PHP version, fewer heavy plugins, a clean database and object caching on busy or WooCommerce sites.', 'For Indian visitors, server location and a CDN matter: hosting in or near India, or caching HTML at the edge, cuts the network part of the delay.'],
  'eliminate-render-blocking-resources': ['Render-blocking resources are CSS and JavaScript files in the head that the browser must download before it can show the page.', 'Defer or delay non-essential JavaScript, inline critical CSS, remove unused plugin assets and self-host fonts, then test every key page.', 'The aim is a faster first paint on real mobile connections, not a perfect PageSpeed score; aggressive settings can break menus, sliders and checkout.'],
  'reduce-unused-css-javascript-wordpress': ['The "reduce unused CSS" and "reduce unused JavaScript" warnings usually come from plugins, page builders and themes loading files on pages that do not need them.', 'Start by removing plugins you do not need, then unload files per page, delay non-essential scripts and generate critical CSS, testing each change.', 'Measure on a real mid-range phone and with field data, not just a lab score, and keep a backup and staging copy before making aggressive changes.'],
  'follow-up-website-leads-fast': ['Speed matters most: get every form enquiry to the right person instantly by email and phone, and reply personally as soon as you reasonably can.', 'An honest auto-reply and thank-you page set expectations, while a short sequence of call, WhatsApp and email follow-ups over a few days catches people who were busy.', 'Record every lead in one place with its source and status, so nothing is forgotten and you can see which pages and campaigns bring real customers.'],
  'website-calculators-lead-generation': ['A calculator works when buyers genuinely want a number before calling, such as an EMI, a solar saving or a rough project quote, and the inputs are simple.', 'Show assumptions openly, present results as indicative ranges, and let people see a result before asking for contact details in most cases.', 'Give the calculator its own indexable page with helpful text, track starts, completions and enquiries in GA4, and review the numbers regularly.'],
  'website-quiz-lead-generation': ['A quiz earns leads only when the result is genuinely useful, so show the answer first and make sharing contact details an optional next step with clear consent.', 'Keep it to the few questions that change the result, map answers to results in a simple rules table, and recommend the cheaper option when it fits.', 'Send quiz answers to your CRM or inbox with the result attached, follow up on WhatsApp only with permission, and track starts, drop-offs and completions.'],
  'website-for-wedding-decorators': ['Organise the portfolio by event type and theme, such as haldi, mehendi, sangeet, wedding mandap and reception, because couples browse by function.', 'Make checking date availability the main action, with a short form that asks the date, venue, city, functions and rough guest count.', 'Keep the gallery fast with compressed images and lazy loading, and add real captions so photos help both couples and search engines.'],
  'website-for-curtain-blinds-shops': ['Show real fabric and blinds photos with honest colour, organised by room, type and light control, so buyers can shortlist before visiting.', 'Make a home measuring visit or swatch request the main call to action, with a form that asks for rooms, window count and area.', 'Sell online only what can be ordered without measuring, such as standard-size curtains, using WooCommerce or Shopify with clear size options.'],
  'website-for-upholstery-services': ['Help visitors decide between repair, reupholstery and buying new, honestly; that advice earns more trust than any slogan.', 'Show real fabrics, your own before-and-after work (published with the customer\'s permission) and a clear home visit or photo estimate process.', 'Explain what drives the price instead of printing figures that go stale, and name the areas you really cover for pickup and home visits.'],
  'seo-for-new-websites': ['A new website first needs to be indexable: submit the sitemap in Search Console, check robots settings and fix any pages Google excludes.', 'Start with specific service, location and question pages that match how Indian customers search, not broad head terms you cannot win yet.', 'New domains take months to build trust, so judge progress by impressions and indexed pages first, then by clicks and enquiries.'],
  'google-discover-small-business': ['Google Discover is a bonus traffic source, not a plan: it shows content people may like based on their interests, and you cannot request a placement.', 'You can control the basics: large good-quality images, clear honest titles, fast mobile pages and helpful articles written for a real Indian audience.', 'Check the Discover report in Search Console, if it appears, and keep investing in search, Google Business Profile and enquiry pages that bring steady leads.'],
  'seo-for-service-pages-without-blog': ['Searches that lead to paid work, like \'service in city\', are mostly won by strong service pages, honest area pages and your Google Business Profile.', 'Each service page needs its own URL, real proof, the questions customers ask, internal links and basic schema that matches what is on the page.', 'A blog helps once the basics are done and your customers research before buying, or when you need visibility beyond your city.'],
  'website-for-sports-turfs': ['A turf booking website should show live slots, prices by time band and a simple advance payment so groups can confirm a game without calling.', 'Clear cancellation, rain and late-arrival policies on the booking page prevent most disputes with captains and corporate groups.', 'One page per sport and location, plus an accurate Google Business Profile, is what helps people searching "box cricket near me" find your turf.'],
  'website-for-swimming-classes': ['Parents choose a swim school on safety, coach experience, batch timings and pool location, so put those four things above the fold and on every batch page.', 'Treat the summer season as a campaign: publish camp dates, age groups and booking early, and switch the homepage message as each season changes.', 'A simple booking or enquiry form with WhatsApp follow-up, plus a well-kept Google Business Profile, usually does more than a large site with many pages.'],
  'website-for-custom-tshirt-printing': ['A custom t-shirt printing website must let buyers choose product, size, colour and print method, upload artwork and see the price.', 'Bulk and corporate orders need their own quote form, and policies on artwork, proofs and returns prevent most disputes.', 'Shopify and WooCommerce both work; the choice depends on how complex your options and pricing rules are.'],
  'wordpress-rest-api-explained': ['The WordPress REST API lets other software and the block editor read and change site content through URLs under /wp-json/, returning data as JSON.', 'Published content is public through it by default, while drafts, user details beyond public authors, orders and settings need authentication.', 'Do not switch the REST API off, because the editor and many plugins depend on it; restrict sensitive endpoints and use application passwords for integrations.'],
  'wordpress-hooks-actions-filters': ['Hooks let you change WordPress without editing core, theme or plugin files: actions run your code at a moment, filters change a value before it is used.', 'Put hook code in a child theme, a small custom plugin or a snippets plugin, never in files that updates will overwrite.', 'Most hook problems come from wrong priority, the wrong number of arguments, forgetting to return the value in a filter or running code on every page.'],
  'wordpress-shortcodes-vs-blocks': ['Shortcodes are text tags replaced by output at render time, blocks are visual editor components, and page-builder widgets live inside a builder like Elementor.', 'For new content, blocks are usually the best default; shortcodes still suit small dynamic snippets, and widgets make sense only on builder-built pages.', 'Before removing a plugin, search the database for its shortcodes, because leftover tags show up as raw text on live pages.'],
  'website-for-computer-training-institutes': ['Give every course its own page with syllabus, duration, batch timings, mode (classroom or online), certificate details and who it suits.', 'Make a demo class or counselling session easy to book by form, call or WhatsApp, and follow up quickly.', 'Be accurate about certificates, affiliations and placement support; never promise jobs or show partnerships you do not hold.'],
  'website-for-abacus-vedic-maths-classes': ['Parents compare abacus and Vedic maths classes on age, levels, teacher, timing and mode, so make each of those easy to find on a phone.', 'A free demo class form with WhatsApp follow-up is usually the main conversion point, so put it on every page and reply quickly.', 'Local SEO and a well-kept Google Business Profile bring nearby parents, while separate pages for online batches can reach families in other cities.'],
  'website-for-pet-boarding-daycare': ['Owners decide on a boarding facility from its website first, so show real photos of every area, who is on site at night and what happens in an emergency.', 'Publish clear rules on vaccinations, temperament checks, food and medication, along with what a typical day looks like, to answer anxious questions before they are asked.', 'A booking or enquiry form with dates, pet details and documents, plus early holiday-season pages, helps you fill peak dates without endless phone calls.'],
  'woocommerce-checkout-optimization': ['A faster WooCommerce checkout asks only for what you need to deliver and invoice, lets people buy as guests and shows delivery charges before the last step.', 'Indian shoppers expect UPI, cards, net banking and often cash on delivery, with clear trust cues near the payment button.', 'Design and test checkout on a phone first, and place real test orders after every change to payments, plugins or themes.'],
  'woocommerce-sell-internationally': ['Start with a few countries where you already get interest, and set shipping zones, currencies and policies for those first.', 'Showing prices in other currencies is not the same as charging in them; check what your payment gateway supports and how it settles.', 'Export GST rules, LUT and courier paperwork need your CA and courier\'s input before the first overseas order.'],
  'woocommerce-pre-orders-backorders': ['Backorders let customers buy out-of-stock items you will restock; pre-orders sell products before they launch, usually with a release date.', 'Decide whether to charge upfront or on release, and show the expected dispatch date on the product page, cart and order email.', 'Shopify handles this with inventory settings and pre-order apps, so the same rules on clear dates and easy cancellation apply.'],
  'woocommerce-store-running-costs': ['The fixed yearly costs of a WooCommerce store are small: a domain at about ₹500 to ₹1,200 a year, hosting at about ₹2,500 to ₹15,000 a year, and any premium plugin or theme licences.', 'The costs that grow with sales are payment gateway fees, shipping, packaging, returns and marketing, so budget for them as a share of each order, not as a flat amount.', 'Maintenance matters more for a store than a brochure site; freelance plans for WordPress sites typically cost about ₹1,500 to ₹6,000 a month, with stores at the higher end.'],
  'shared-vs-vps-vs-managed-wordpress-hosting': ['Most small Indian business sites run well on good shared or entry cloud hosting; upgrade only when you see real signs such as slow server response, resource limits or traffic spikes.', 'A VPS gives control but also gives you the job of managing the server, so it suits teams with technical help, while managed WordPress hosting trades some flexibility for convenience.', 'Judge a plan on renewal price, server location, backups, PHP versions and support quality, not on the first-year discount.'],
  'landing-page-cost-factors': ['A good cost per landing page is judged by what the quote includes and whether the page can lower your cost per lead, not by the lowest price.', 'Copy, design approach, form integrations, tracking and revision rounds are what move the build cost of a landing page.', 'Never cut mobile speed, a tested form or conversion tracking, or you cannot tell whether the page or the ads are the problem.'],
  'wordpress-stuck-in-maintenance-mode': ['WordPress shows the maintenance message while updating and normally removes it in seconds; if an update is interrupted, a .maintenance file is left behind.', 'Deleting the .maintenance file from the site\'s root folder through your hosting file manager or FTP usually brings the site back immediately.', 'After fixing it, check which update failed, complete it properly and update in smaller batches with a backup to avoid a repeat.'],
  'increase-wordpress-upload-size-limit': ['WordPress takes its upload limit from PHP, using the smaller of upload_max_filesize and post_max_size, so raise both, ideally in your hosting control panel.', 'php.ini, .user.ini and .htaccess changes only work on some servers, wp-config.php cannot raise the limit, and Nginx, Cloudflare or multisite can add limits of their own.', 'Before raising the limit, check whether the file should be smaller: compressed images, embedded videos and SFTP uploads are often the better fix.'],
  'wordpress-pages-404-permalinks-fix': ['When the homepage loads but every other page returns 404, the rewrite rules are broken: resave permalinks first, then check .htaccess or the Nginx configuration.', 'Migrations, server changes, security plugins and custom post type plugins are the usual triggers, so think about what changed just before the 404s started.', 'After fixing, check Search Console and add 301 redirects for any URLs that genuinely changed, so visitors and Google land on the right pages.'],
  'website-for-commercial-kitchen-equipment-suppliers': ['Organise the catalogue by equipment type and give every product its dimensions, fuel or power, capacity and material, because that is what chefs and consultants check first.', 'Sell turnkey kitchens as a process with project pages, and word brand authorisations, approvals and food safety advice exactly as far as they are true.', 'Split enquiries into a single-product quote, a full-kitchen project brief and a service or AMC request, so each lands with the right person and the right details.'],
  'website-for-car-accessories-shops': ['Fitment is the heart of a car accessories website: let buyers pick make, model, year and variant, and show only products that fit.', 'Separate products that ship from services that need a fitting appointment, and make installation booking as easy as buying.', 'WooCommerce and Shopify both work; keep claims about materials, warranty and legality accurate, and check current rules for items like tinted film and lighting.'],
  'website-for-escape-room-gaming-zones': ['A website for escape rooms exists to get a group from a phone search to a booked slot with as little back-and-forth as possible.', 'Each room or game needs its own page with age rules, group size, duration and real photos, plus clear party and corporate packages.', 'Deposits, visible refund rules and honest safety information reduce no-shows and counter arguments.'],
  'click-to-whatsapp-ads-vs-website': ['Click-to-WhatsApp ads start a conversation instantly and suit simple, local or high-trust sales where a quick chat closes the deal.', 'A website landing page explains more, qualifies leads and tracks conversions properly, which suits considered purchases and larger budgets.', 'Many Indian businesses do best with both: a landing page for detail and tracking, with a WhatsApp button for people who prefer to chat.'],
  'video-testimonials-website': ['A short, real video from a named customer builds more trust than a polished ad, so make it easy for happy customers to record one on their phone.', 'Get written consent, keep edits honest, and never script or pay for opinions without disclosing it.', 'Embed videos so they load only when clicked, add captions and a short text summary, and place them next to the decision they support.'],
  'qr-codes-for-business-website': ['A QR code should open a specific, mobile-friendly page that matches where it was scanned, not just your homepage.', 'Use a URL you control, add UTM tags so each placement shows up separately in analytics, and test every printed code before a print run.', 'Keep payment QR codes (UPI) and website QR codes separate and clearly labelled, so customers know what will happen when they scan.'],
  'website-for-ev-charging-stations': ['EV charging websites serve two audiences: drivers who need a working charger now, and homes, societies and businesses who want chargers installed.', 'Each charging location deserves its own clear details: address, map, connector types, power, timings, payment method and access notes.', 'Installation enquiries need a form that asks for site type, parking, power supply and number of chargers, plus honest notes on approvals and timelines.'],
  'website-for-trophy-awards-shops': ['Trophy buyers have a fixed event date, so the website must show the range, explain engraving clearly and make delivery timelines obvious.', 'Combine a browsable catalogue for small orders with a quote form for bulk and custom work; WooCommerce and Shopify can both handle engraving text and quantity pricing.', 'Organise products by occasion, material and price band, and publish order deadlines before sports day, annual day and corporate award seasons.'],
  'website-for-organic-food-stores': ['An organic food store website sells on trust, so sourcing stories, farm photos and accurately stated certifications matter as much as the product catalogue.', 'Shopify and WooCommerce both handle Indian payments, local delivery slots and pincode checks; the choice depends on how much control and customisation you need.', 'Plan perishables, delivery areas, subscriptions and repeat ordering carefully, because regular customers are the core of most organic food businesses.'],
  'white-label-woocommerce-development': ['In white-label WooCommerce work the partner handles store setup, templates, payments, shipping, imports and testing, while your agency keeps the client, brand and design.', 'Payments, shipping and tax differ by market, so the client opens gateway accounts in its own name and its accountant decides tax settings.', 'Agree a written brief, a staging workflow, a checkout test plan and who handles support after launch before the first store starts.'],
  'offshore-wordpress-developer-time-zones': ['Agree overlap hours in writing, use them for calls, blockers and live changes, and recheck them when the UK, US or Australia change their clocks.', 'A fixed end-of-day update with done, next, questions and blocked items, plus staging links, keeps work moving while one side is offline.', 'Plan urgent fixes before they happen: shared alerts, access ready in a password manager, one emergency channel and response times by severity.'],
  'agency-overflow-wordpress-work': ['Overflow partners suit agencies with uneven workloads: busy months, specialist builds or maintenance that distracts the core team.', 'A clear brief, staging-first workflow, defined QA and agreed communication rules matter more than the partner\'s location.', 'Start with one small, well-defined project, agree white-label and confidentiality terms in writing, and review before scaling up.'],
  'wordpress-login-not-working-fix': ['Most WordPress login failures come from four causes: browser cookies or cache, a wrong site URL, a security plugin lockout, or a password reset email that never arrives.', 'Work from the outside in: try a private window, then check the site URL settings, then rename the plugins folder, and only then edit the database.', 'Once you are back in, add two-factor authentication, a working email setup and an extra admin account so one failure can never lock the business out again.'],
  'wordpress-http-error-image-upload-fix': ['The WordPress "HTTP error" is a generic message: the upload or the image resizing step failed on the server, and the real reason is in the server logs.', 'Most cases come down to a large photo, low memory or time limits, the image library (Imagick or GD), folder permissions or a security rule.', 'Work from the cheapest check to the deepest: retry, shrink the image, test another browser, then limits, image library, permissions, firewall and plugins.'],
  'wordpress-css-changes-not-showing': ['When CSS changes do not show, either an old copy of the file is being served by a cache, or the new rule is loading but another rule overrides it.', 'Use browser developer tools first: if your rule is missing, it is a cache or file problem; if it is crossed out, it is specificity or load order.', 'Clear caches from the inside out: plugin and Elementor CSS, then minify files, then server and CDN cache, then the browser.'],
  'white-label-elementor-development': ['White-label Elementor development lets an agency deliver Elementor sites under its own brand while a developer builds them behind the scenes.', 'The quality test is editability: global styles, a small set of reusable templates and locked layouts so clients can update content without breaking design.', 'Agree the scope, Elementor Pro licence ownership, speed targets, review rounds and confidentiality before the first project starts.'],
  'wordpress-reseller-for-marketing-agencies': ['Reselling WordPress development lets SEO and marketing agencies deliver site fixes, landing pages and redesigns without hiring in-house developers.', 'Package website work into clear offers such as fixed-scope projects, monthly care plans and blocks of hours, and scope each job in writing before quoting.', 'Choose a partner who works behind your brand, follows your process, reports clearly and keeps all accounts in the client\'s or your agency\'s name.'],
  'agency-development-retainer': ['Pick the retainer model that matches your work: an hours bank for varied requests, reserved capacity for steady volume, or an outcome-based scope for predictable maintenance.', 'Write the scope, priority levels, response times you need, rollover and overage rules into one short agreement, and review it after the first two or three months.', 'White-label etiquette matters as much as code: one request queue, no direct client contact unless you ask, no credits on client sites, and clean access removal when work ends.'],
  'search-console-sitemap-couldnt-fetch': ['Open the sitemap URL in a private window first: if it does not load as valid XML with a 200 status, fix that before anything else.', 'Firewalls, CDN bot protection, robots.txt rules, redirects and wrong URLs cause most real fetch failures on WordPress sites.', 'A brand-new sitemap often shows "Couldn\'t fetch" for a while even when nothing is wrong; check URL Inspection on the sitemap and wait a few days before changing things.'],
  'google-analytics-not-tracking-wordpress': ['First confirm whether the GA4 tag is on the page at all: view the source or use Tag Assistant, and check the measurement ID matches your GA4 data stream.', 'Duplicate tags, consent banners, caching or minification plugins, ad blockers and internal traffic filters are the usual reasons data is missing or wrong.', 'Use Realtime and DebugView to test changes immediately, because standard GA4 reports can take a day or more to fill in.'],
  'wordpress-site-not-showing-on-google': ['Start with the quick checks: the WordPress "Discourage search engines" setting, noindex tags, robots.txt and a site: search on Google.', 'Verify the site in Google Search Console, submit the sitemap and use URL Inspection to see exactly why a page is or is not indexed.', 'A new site can take time to appear, but if established pages vanish, check for hacks, manual actions, migrations and redirects.'],
  'website-for-personal-trainers': ['A trainer website should answer who you help, how you train, where and what the packages include, then make booking a first consultation easy.', 'Honest proof works best: qualifications you actually hold, client stories shared with permission and realistic expectations, never guaranteed results.', 'Link the site from Instagram and Google, take enquiries by form and WhatsApp, and add online payments only when your packages are settled.'],
  'website-for-paint-dealers': ['A paint dealer website should show the brands and ranges you stock, help customers with shades and quantities, and make quote requests easy.', 'Contractors and painters are repeat buyers, so give them a simple way to send material lists and get quotes on WhatsApp or by form.', 'Local search matters most: a complete Google Business Profile, clear location details and pages for the services you really offer.'],
  'website-for-ayurvedic-product-brands': ['An Ayurvedic brand website should explain each product\'s ingredients, usage and who it suits, with licence and label details customers can check.', 'Health claims need care: describe traditional use honestly, avoid promises to cure disease and check current AYUSH and advertising rules before publishing.', 'Repeat orders drive the business, so make reordering, subscriptions, WhatsApp updates and UPI or COD checkout simple from the first purchase.'],
  'wordpress-403-forbidden-error-fix': ['A 403 Forbidden error means the server understood the request but refuses it, usually because of a security rule, permissions or a blocked IP.', 'Work in a safe order: note where the 403 appears, check security plugins and firewalls, then .htaccess, then file permissions, then the host.', 'If only wp-admin or one action is blocked, a firewall or security rule is the most likely cause; if the whole site is blocked, check .htaccess and permissions first.'],
  'wordpress-500-internal-server-error-fix': ['A 500 internal server error is a generic failure; the real cause is almost always written in the server error log or the WordPress debug log.', 'The usual culprits are a plugin or theme fatal error, a corrupt .htaccess file, memory limits or a PHP version the code does not support.', 'Take a backup, read the log, then change one thing at a time; do not reinstall WordPress or restore old backups blindly.'],
  'wordpress-502-504-gateway-timeout-fix': ['A WordPress 502 bad gateway means PHP sent a bad reply; a 504 gateway timeout means it replied too slowly or not at all.', 'The usual causes are exhausted PHP workers, a slow plugin or query, a hanging outside service, or a Cloudflare or host problem.', '"The gateway did not receive a timely response" is a 504 message, so look for slow requests rather than broken code.'],
  'cloudflare-wordpress-setup-guide': ['Copy and check every DNS record before changing nameservers, keep mail records on DNS only, and set SSL/TLS to Full (strict), because Flexible causes redirect loops on WordPress.', 'Let Cloudflare cache static files by default; if you cache HTML, bypass wp-admin, wp-login.php, cart, checkout, my-account and logged-in visitors, and let one tool control each cache layer.', 'After switching, restore real visitor IPs, add light firewall rules, and test logins, forms, a test order, payment callbacks and email before calling the job done.'],
  'find-fix-broken-links-wordpress': ['Find broken links with Search Console and a periodic crawl from outside the site, rather than an always-on link checker plugin that slows WordPress.', 'Fix internal links at the source, use 301 redirects for moved pages, and update or remove dead external links depending on how useful they were.', 'Check links after every redesign, migration or permalink change, because that is when most broken links appear.'],
  'wordpress-search-not-working-fix': ['Default WordPress search only matches titles, content and excerpts, so products, custom fields and builder content often need a search plugin or settings change.', 'Check the basics first: the search URL, the theme search form, caching rules and a plugin conflict test on a staging copy.', 'On WooCommerce stores, fix product visibility and SKU search, and consider a dedicated search plugin for large catalogues or slow results.'],
  'woocommerce-checkout-not-working-fix': ['Start with the browser console and the WooCommerce logs, because the error message usually points straight at caching, a JavaScript conflict or the payment plugin.', 'Cart, checkout and account pages must be excluded from page caching, Cloudflare caching and JavaScript optimisation, or checkout breaks in strange ways.', 'Find conflicts on a staging copy by switching to a default theme and only WooCommerce plus the gateway, then re-enable plugins one at a time.'],
  'woocommerce-payment-gateway-not-working-india': ['Most WooCommerce payment failures in India come from gateway settings, webhooks, caching or plugin conflicts rather than the gateway itself being down.', 'Orders stuck on pending payment usually mean the gateway never told WooCommerce the payment succeeded, so check webhooks and logs first.', 'Test with the gateway\'s test mode on a staging copy, keep plugins updated and reconcile paid-but-pending orders against the gateway dashboard.'],
  'wordpress-update-failed-fix': ['Most failed WordPress updates come from a few causes: a timeout, low disk space, wrong file permissions, an expired licence or an update lock left behind.', 'Take a backup before you retry, check the error text, then work through lock, permissions, space and licence in that order rather than reinstalling everything.', 'A half-finished plugin update is fixed by replacing that plugin folder with a clean copy of the same version, not by deleting the plugin and its settings.'],
  'wordpress-media-library-not-loading-fix': ['Switch to list view first: if it works while grid view spins, the problem is usually JavaScript or admin-ajax, often a plugin conflict.', 'Large libraries, low PHP memory and slow shared hosting can stop the library loading, so check limits and error logs before reinstalling anything.', 'After a migration or CDN change, broken thumbnails usually mean wrong URLs, file permissions or missing image sizes rather than lost files.'],
  'indexed-though-blocked-by-robots-txt-fix': ['robots.txt controls crawling, not indexing: a blocked URL can still appear in Google if other pages link to it.', 'To keep a page out of Google, allow crawling and add a noindex tag; to get a page indexed, remove the robots.txt rule that blocks it.', 'Many reports are harmless, such as cart, search or admin URLs, so fix only the URLs that matter and then validate the fix in Search Console.'],
  'recaptcha-not-working-wordpress': ['Most reCAPTCHA errors come from keys: the domain is not listed, the site key and secret key are swapped, or v2 keys are used where v3 is expected.', 'Caching, script delay and duplicate reCAPTCHA scripts from two plugins are the common reasons forms fail silently after a speed or plugin change.', 'reCAPTCHA is one layer of spam protection; a honeypot, server-side validation and a working email setup matter just as much for getting enquiries.'],
  'wordpress-site-slow-on-mobile-fix': ['Mobile tests simulate a slower phone and network, so heavy images, sliders and scripts that a desktop shrugs off show up clearly on mobile.', 'The biggest mobile fixes are removing sliders and hero videos, serving correctly sized images, and delaying chat, tracking and ad scripts.', 'Always test on a real mid-range Android phone over mobile data, since that is how most Indian visitors see your site.'],
  'woocommerce-orders-not-showing-fix': ['Many "missing" WooCommerce orders are hidden by status filters, pending payment, drafts or HPOS sync settings rather than truly lost.', 'Paid-but-no-order problems usually mean the payment gateway callback failed, so compare the gateway dashboard with WooCommerce and check logs.', 'Missing order emails are a separate issue, normally fixed with proper SMTP setup and checking the email settings for each order status.'],
  'wordpress-admin-bar-dashboard-missing-fix': ['A missing admin bar on the front end is usually a profile setting, a theme missing wp_footer, or a cached logged-out page, not a hack.', 'Missing dashboard menus or "Sorry, you are not allowed to access this page" mean your account lacks a capability: check the role, plugins that edit roles, and the table prefix.', 'Change one thing at a time, keep a backup, and treat an unknown administrator account or a role that changed by itself as a possible security incident.'],
  'wordpress-multisite-seo': ['Google treats each site in a WordPress multisite network as its own website, so each needs its own sitemap, Search Console property, titles and unique content.', 'Audit SEO plugin settings site by site, because one subsite set to noindex or with a wrong canonical can quietly drop out of search.', 'Duplicate content across sites, wrong hreflang and a slow shared server are the main multisite-specific SEO risks.'],
  'wordpress-hosting-price-india': ['For a typical small business site, the cost guide on this site puts WordPress hosting at roughly ₹2,500 to ₹15,000 a year and a .com or .in domain at roughly ₹500 to ₹1,200 a year.', 'Work out the yearly cost from renewal rates, domain, email, licences, maintenance and GST, and divide by twelve for an honest monthly figure, rather than trusting the headline price.', 'Move host when the site has outgrown the plan, support cannot fix recurring problems, or renewal makes a better-fitting plan elsewhere sensible, and test the move carefully.'],
  'wordpress-multisite-slow': ['A slow WordPress multisite is usually caused by something shared: under-sized hosting, network-activated plugins, a large database, no object cache or piled-up cron jobs.', 'To speed up WordPress multisite, measure server response time on several subsites first, then fix plugins, caching, database, cron and PHP in that order.', 'If subsites need very different plugins, traffic or owners, separate installs may be faster and simpler than one network.'],
  'shopify-store-setup-india': ['Start payment provider KYC and GST checks first, because they delay a Shopify launch in India more than the store build does.', 'Connect the domain by changing only the A and CNAME records Shopify asks for, so existing MX and SPF email records keep working.', 'Begin with a GST invoice app, a courier app and COD controls, and add other apps one at a time while watching speed.'],
  'shopify-theme-customisation': ['Online Store 2.0 themes are built from templates, sections and blocks, so most layout changes can be made in the theme editor without code.', 'Metafields keep product details such as fabric or care notes in fields, while a few lines of Liquid or a theme block display them.', 'Make code changes on a duplicate theme, keep custom work in new files with a change log, and plan for theme updates.'],
  'shopify-store-slow-fix': ['A slow Shopify store is rarely a hosting problem; app scripts, leftover app code, heavy themes, images, fonts, sliders and widgets are the usual causes.', 'Measure first with the admin web performance report, PageSpeed Insights and the Chrome DevTools Network and Coverage panels.', 'Fix in priority order: remove unused apps and leftover code, size the main image properly, drop the slider, then trim widgets and fonts.'],
  'migrate-woocommerce-to-shopify': ['Products, variants, categories and customers move through Shopify CSV files or a migration app, while past orders usually need an app.', 'Shopify uses fixed paths such as /products/ and /collections/, so every old WooCommerce URL needs a 301 in Shopify URL redirects.', 'Passwords cannot move, so plan how customers sign in, and run a cutover checklist covering payments, GST invoices, redirects and DNS.'],
  'shopify-store-cost-india': ['A Shopify budget is made of a plan, apps, payment charges, a possible Shopify fee on outside gateways, and one-off theme, setup and content costs.', 'Apps and per-order fees grow as the store sells more, so work out the total from expected orders rather than headline prices.', 'Shopify bundles hosting and security into the plan, while WooCommerce has no platform fee but needs hosting and upkeep, so neither is cheaper for every store.'],
  'shopify-seo-checklist': ['Shopify handles sitemaps, HTTPS and canonicals, but titles, collection introductions, product links in the theme and duplicate structured data are your job.', 'Make product grids link to plain /products/ URLs, keep filtered and tag URLs out of menus, and turn worthwhile combinations into real collections.', 'For Indian shoppers, use the product names they search for, answer COD, delivery and returns questions on the page, and keep collection pages fast on mobile data.'],
  'shopify-cash-on-delivery-india': ['Shopify COD is a manual payment method: orders arrive unpaid and are marked as paid once the courier or aggregator remits the cash.', 'COD fees, order-value limits and pin code rules need an app or a courier tool, because the built-in option is simply on or off.', 'Confirming orders by call, WhatsApp or OTP, rewarding prepaid and matching remittances weekly keep RTO and COD costs under control.'],
  'shopify-gst-invoices-india': ['Shopify calculates tax at checkout, but a GST invoice with your GSTIN, HSN codes and tax split normally comes from an invoice app.', 'Settle rates, HSN codes, inclusive pricing and the treatment of shipping, COD fees and discounts with your CA before configuring the store.', 'Test in-state, inter-state and B2B orders, plus refunds and refused COD parcels, and have your CA review the first month of invoices.'],
  'shopify-payment-gateways-india': ['Indian Shopify stores take online payments through a third-party provider, connected in the payments settings after its KYC checks.', 'Compare two sets of fees: the provider\'s own charges and any Shopify transaction fee on your plan for third-party gateways.', 'Test UPI, cards, failed payments and refunds before launch, and reconcile settlements against Shopify orders every week.'],
  'shopify-shipping-india': ['Shopify shipping rates in India are set in shipping and delivery settings as zones with weight, order-value or flat rates.', 'Shopify does not know which pin codes your courier reaches, so serviceability and labels come from a courier or aggregator app.', 'After launch, handle non-delivery reports quickly and watch volumetric weight to keep RTO and shipping charges in check.'],
  'hire-shopify-developer-india': ['A Shopify developer handles what the admin cannot: custom sections in Liquid, theme fixes, speed and SEO, migrations and integrations.', 'Send a one-page brief covering products, theme, payments, COD, shipping, GST and launch date so quotes can be compared fairly.', 'Keep every account in your name, give access only through a collaborator or staff account, and walk away from vague scopes or ranking guarantees.'],
  'shopify-theme-change-without-losing-seo': ['A Shopify theme change keeps product, collection, page and blog URLs, so the SEO risk sits in what the theme outputs: titles, headings, structured data, section text and internal links.', 'Benchmark key pages, build the new theme unpublished, recreate custom templates and app embeds, and compare titles, H1s and structured data before publishing.', 'Publish in a quiet period, keep the old theme in the library to roll back, and watch Search Console daily for the first week, then weekly.'],
  'flash-sale-collection-meta-title-description': ['Keep one permanent sale collection URL and change its meta title and description for each event, so the page keeps whatever ranking it has built up.', 'Put the product type and the offer type in the title, and the end date, delivery or COD details and a clear reason to click in the description.', 'Only mention discounts, dates and stock that are true on the page, check the WhatsApp link preview, and update or remove sale wording the day the sale ends.'],
  'wordpress-multisite-development-services': ['Multisite development is mostly planning: which sites share what, how domains and users work, and who controls plugins and themes across the network.', 'A good service covers setup, a shared theme with per-site flexibility, roles, SEO per site, hosting sizing, backups and a written handover.', 'Check first whether you really need multisite; separate installs or a single site with sections are often simpler for small businesses.'],
  'woocommerce-multiple-suppliers': ['Record the supplier on every product with a field or taxonomy, so stock, orders, reports and purchase planning can all be filtered by supplier.', 'Decide how each supplier works with you (you hold stock, they dropship, or they ship part orders) because that drives stock sync, order routing and shipping.', 'If suppliers need their own logins, payouts and storefronts, you are building a multi-vendor marketplace, which is a different and larger project.'],
  'woocommerce-custom-pricing-plugin-development': ['Try existing pricing plugins first; a custom plugin makes sense when your pricing logic is unusual, combines several rules, or must connect to your own price data.', 'Write the pricing rules down with worked examples before development, including how GST, coupons, rounding and the cart total should behave.', 'Insist on tests for every rule, a settings screen your team can use, and code that survives WooCommerce updates and the block checkout.'],
  'wordpress-update-and-security-service': ['A proper update and security service tests updates before or straight after applying them, keeps restorable backups off the server, and watches for problems between visits.', 'Ask exactly what happens when an update breaks the site or the site is hacked, because that is where services differ most.', 'It is narrower than a full maintenance plan: content edits, design changes and new features are usually extra.'],
  'wordpress-update-button-missing': ['A missing update button usually means your account is not an administrator, updates are disabled in code or by the host, or a premium licence has lapsed.', 'Check in order: your role, the Updates screen, wp-config.php settings, must-use plugins, your host panel and premium licences.', 'Take a backup before you re-enable updates on a site that has not been updated for a while, and update in a sensible order.'],
  'gravity-forms-vs-contact-form-7': ['Contact Form 7 is free and fine for simple enquiry forms; Gravity Forms is a paid plugin built for complex, multi-step and data-heavy forms.', 'Gravity Forms includes saved entries, conditional logic, calculations and many integrations; Contact Form 7 needs add-ons for most of these.', 'Choose by the job: a basic contact page rarely needs Gravity Forms, while applications, quotes and workflows rarely work well in Contact Form 7.'],
  'squeeze-page-vs-landing-page': ['A squeeze page is a short landing page with one job: collect an email or phone number, usually in exchange for a free resource.', 'Use a squeeze page for cold traffic and lead magnets, and a fuller landing page when visitors need detail before enquiring or buying.', 'Both need one goal, one form, fast mobile loading and tracking set up before ads go live.'],
  'woocommerce-vendor-onboarding': ['Good vendor onboarding is a fixed sequence: application, verification, approval, store setup, first product review and payout setup.', 'Collect GST, PAN and bank details at registration, but keep the first form short and ask for documents after basic approval.', 'Review each new seller\'s first products manually and send a welcome checklist so vendors go live correctly the first time.'],
  'shopify-pincode-checker-india': ['A pincode checker on the product page tells Indian shoppers before checkout whether you deliver, roughly when, and whether cash on delivery is available.', 'The checker is only as accurate as its data: a pin code list you maintain, or live serviceability from your courier or shipping aggregator.', 'Enforce the same rules at checkout, because a product page message alone does not stop an order to an unserviceable pin code or a blocked COD order.'],
  'shopify-whatsapp-ordering-india': ['Keep Shopify checkout as the place orders are placed and paid; use WhatsApp for questions, order updates, COD confirmation and cart reminders.', 'A click-to-chat button needs no app, while automated order messages need the WhatsApp Business Platform through an approved provider or app, with opted-in customers and approved templates.', 'If you take orders inside chats, create them in Shopify as draft orders with a payment link so stock, GST invoices and shipping stay in one system.'],
  'remove-fake-pages-wordpress-site': ['Fake pages on a WordPress site are usually created through a hack, a spam account or a leftover file, so find the source before deleting anything.', 'Delete the pages, files and accounts behind them, then close the entry point with updates, password changes and a malware scan, or they return.', 'Return a 404 or 410 for the removed URLs and use Search Console removals and sitemaps so Google drops them quickly.'],
  'wordpress-developer-hourly-rate-india': ['A WordPress developer hourly rate in India depends on skill level, the type of work, urgency, accountability and who else sits between you and the developer.', 'A low hourly rate is not always the cheapest option: total hours, rework, communication and what happens after launch decide the real cost.', 'Ask for a written scope, an hours estimate per task and a cap, then compare quotes on the same brief rather than on the headline rate.'],
  'wordpress-revisions-not-showing': ['Revisions only appear after a post has been saved more than once, and the link sits in the editor sidebar, so first check you are looking in the right place.', 'The common causes are revisions disabled or limited in wp-config.php, a cleanup or optimisation plugin deleting them, or a post type that does not support revisions.', 'Page builders such as Elementor keep their own history, and revisions are not a backup: keep real backups so you can recover from bigger mistakes.'],
  'marathi-website-design': ['Decide first whether the site is Marathi-first, English-first with Marathi pages, or fully bilingual, because that choice sets the plugin, URL and SEO plan.', 'Use proper Unicode Marathi text with a tested Devanagari font, never text inside images or old legacy fonts, so Google and phones can read every word.', 'Have Marathi copy written or checked by a fluent writer, add a language switcher and hreflang if there are two versions, and track enquiries from Marathi pages separately.'],
  'website-amc-contract-india': ['A website AMC is a yearly agreement for updates, backups, security, small changes and support; the value is in the written scope, not the word AMC.', 'Check response times, how many content changes are included, what counts as extra work, who holds the logins and what happens if the site is hacked.', 'Ask for a monthly or quarterly report, a clean exit clause and GST invoices, and compare an AMC with a monthly plan before you sign.'],
  'website-for-pune-industrial-suppliers': ['Buyers in Pune industrial belts check a supplier website to confirm capability, machines, certifications and location before adding you to a vendor list.', 'Build pages around processes, materials, industries served and an RFQ form that accepts drawings, and state only certifications and approvals you actually hold.', 'Combine a fast WordPress site with Google Business Profile, location wording for your MIDC area and a clear way to send an enquiry on WhatsApp or email.'],
  'shopify-domain-not-connecting': ['Most Shopify domain problems come from the DNS records: the root A record and the www CNAME must point to Shopify, and old conflicting records must be removed.', 'Connect the domain by editing records at your current DNS provider rather than changing nameservers, so MX and email records for your business mailboxes keep working.', 'SSL usually appears on its own once DNS is correct; a proxy such as Cloudflare in orange-cloud mode, extra AAAA records or CAA records commonly block it.'],
  'shopify-emails-going-to-spam': ['Shopify emails most often go to spam because the sender email is a free Gmail address or a domain that has not been authenticated in Shopify.', 'Use a sender address on your own domain, add the DNS records Shopify gives you for authentication, and publish a DMARC record for that domain.', 'Simple, text-led templates with accurate order details, a clear reply-to address and a clean marketing list keep order emails and newsletters out of spam.'],
  'woocommerce-add-to-cart-not-working': ['Most add to cart failures come from caching of cart pages or cart fragments, JavaScript conflicts from plugins or the theme, or product settings such as a missing price or stock.', 'Find the symptom first: button does nothing, cart stays empty, mini cart does not update, or the product says it cannot be purchased; each points to a different cause.', 'Test on a staging copy with plugins disabled one by one, exclude cart, checkout and my account pages from every cache layer, and check outdated template overrides.'],
  'elementor-header-footer-not-showing': ['An Elementor Theme Builder header or footer that does not appear is usually missing display conditions, unpublished, or blocked by a theme that does not support Elementor locations.', 'Check in this order: template published, conditions set to Entire Site, Elementor Pro active and licensed, theme support, then caches and conflicting templates.', 'The Hello Elementor theme or a theme with full Theme Builder support avoids most header and footer problems.'],
  'elementor-mobile-view-not-working': ['Most Elementor mobile problems come from settings changed on the wrong device, a stale cache or CSS file, or custom breakpoints that do not match real phones.', 'Check in this order: device icon in the editor, Regenerate CSS, clear every cache, then breakpoints, hidden elements, fixed widths and theme CSS.', 'Test on a real Android phone over mobile data, not only the editor preview, because Indian visitors mostly arrive on mid-range phones.'],
  'elementor-not-saving-changes': ['When Elementor will not save, the editor is failing to send the page data to the server, usually because of a server error, memory or input limits, a firewall rule or a plugin conflict.', 'Check the error message, recover your work from Revisions, then test memory limits, security rules, plugin conflicts and page size in that order.', 'Very large pages and many nested widgets make saving fragile, so splitting pages and using global styles also prevents the problem.'],
  'woocommerce-product-images-wrong-size': ['WooCommerce product images look wrong when the theme, the WooCommerce image settings and the size of your uploaded photos do not agree, or when old thumbnails were never regenerated.', 'Set the catalogue crop ratio in the Customizer, regenerate thumbnails, then check the theme, lazy loading, CDN and image plugins in that order.', 'Upload square or consistent-ratio photos at around 1200 to 2000 pixels wide, so every size WooCommerce creates stays sharp and even.'],
  'woocommerce-shipping-not-showing-at-checkout': ['When shipping does not show at checkout, WooCommerce could not match the customer address to a shipping zone with an enabled method, or a condition such as weight, class or minimum order removed every rate.', 'Check the zone order, the zone regions and pin codes, the method settings, product weights and shipping classes, then caches and plugins, in that order.', 'Test with a real Indian pin code in a private window after every change, and keep a catch-all zone so no customer is ever left without an option.'],
  'woocommerce-stock-not-updating': ['WooCommerce reduces stock only when stock management is on and an order reaches a status that triggers it, so most cases come down to settings, order statuses or a payment gateway that never confirms.', 'Check the global and product stock settings, how the order was created and its status, variation-level stock, then sync plugins, caches and scheduled tasks.', 'If stock is synced with Tally, a POS or a marketplace, decide which system is the source of truth and stop two systems from writing to the same product.'],
  'shopify-checkout-not-working': ['Place a test order yourself and note the exact step and message where checkout fails.', 'Most failures come from an inactive gateway, a missing shipping rate, hidden COD or an app script in the cart.', 'Keep a flat backup shipping rate and test UPI, cards and COD after every change.'],
  'shopify-google-merchant-center-disapproved': ['Read the issue on each product in Merchant Center before changing anything.', 'Fix data in Shopify, because the Google and YouTube app overwrites Merchant Center edits.', 'If every product is disapproved, fix account issues: contact details, policies and a working checkout.'],
  'shopify-remove-password-page': ['Untick password protection under Online Store, then Preferences, and save.', 'If the option will not turn off, the store is still on a trial or has no paid plan.', 'Test payments, shipping, GST, domain and emails before removing the password.'],
};
